/* Machine translations of Baumgarten's Metaphysica in the manner of Guyer and Wood, made with the brief in
   Individuals/Jesus-La-Paz/translation-briefs.js (guyer) by portal/baum-translate.py. Not the translators' own work;
   unreviewed. Shape as Jesus's translation files: KANT_TR['met/guyer'][key] = [English, [notes]]. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['met/guyer'] = {"met:1":["Metaphysics is the science of the first principles in human cognition.",[]],"met:2":["To metaphysics are referred ontology, cosmology, psychology, and natural theology.",[]],"met:3":["Natural metaphysics is the cognition of the things occurring in metaphysics acquired by use alone, to which it is useful that the artificial, defined in §. 1, be added: 1) For the sake of the evolution of concepts. 2) For the sake of the determination and conception of the first propositions. 3) For the sake of the continuity and certainty of proofs, etc.",["'evolution': Latin evolutio, an unfolding.","'etc.': e.c. in the text."]],"met:4":["Ontology (Die Grund-Wissenschaft) (ontosophia, metaphysics, cf. §. 1, universal metaphysics, architectonic, first philosophy,) is the science of the more general predicates of a being.",["'a being': Latin ens."]],"met:5":["The more general predicates of a being are the first principles of human cognition, therefore ontology is referred, §. 2, with reason to metaphysics, §. 1, 4.",["'with reason': cum ratione, i.e. rightly; ratio is elsewhere rendered 'ground'."]],"met:6":["Ontology contains the predicates of a being, §. 4, I) internal 1) universal, which are in each single one, 2) disjunctive, of which one or the other is in each single one; II) relative.",["'in each single one': in singulis."]],"met:7":["The negative nothing, cf. §. 54, the unrepresentable, the impossible, the repugnant, (the absurd, cf. §. 13,) what involves a contradiction, the implicating, the contradictory, is A and not-A, or, of contradictory predicates there is no subject, or, nothing is, and is not. 0 = A + not-A. This proposition is called the principle of contradiction, and absolutely first.",["'nothing is, and is not': nihil est, et non est; can be read either as 'there is nothing that is and is not' or as 'nothing is and is not'.","'implicating': implicans, i.e. involving (a contradiction)."]],"met:8":["The not-nothing is something (Etwas): the representable, whatever does not involve a contradiction, whatever is not A and not-A, is possible (Möglich), §. 7.",["'The not-nothing': Nonnihil."]],"met:9":["A and not-A is not something, §. 8, hence is nothing and contradictory, §. 7, or an implicating subject has no predicates, or, whatever is and is not, is nothing. A + not-A = 0.",[]],"met:10":["Everything possible is either A, or not-A, or neither, §. 8, now neither is nothing, because it would be both, §. 9. Therefore everything possible either is A, or not-A, or, to every subject, of all contradictory predicates one or the other belongs. This proposition is called the principle of the excluded third, or middle, between two contradictories.",["'proposition': the text prints praepositio ('preposition'), apparently a misprint for propositio.","'neither is nothing': neutrum est nihilum, i.e. the alternative 'neither' is a nothing.","'belongs': convenit."]],"met:11":["Every possible A is A, or, whatever is, that is, or, every subject is a predicate of itself. If you deny: some possible A is not-A, §. 10, hence A and not-A, or nothing, §. 7, which is impossible, §. 9. This proposition is called the principle of position, or, of identity.",["'position': positio, the act of positing."]],"met:12":["When the impossible is posited a contradiction arises (Entsteht ein Widerspruch). What not only seems, but also is, is true (Wahr), what only seems, is not, is called apparent (nur scheinend). Hence a contradiction that has arisen is either true, or apparent.",["'but also is, is true': sed et est, verum; verum may also be read as the predicate of est ('but also is true').","'is not': non est, without a connective in the Latin."]],"met:13":["When A and not-A are posited, a contradiction arises, §. 9, 12. When A is posited and B, with which posited not-A is posited, the impossible is posited, §. 9, hence a contradiction arises, §. 12, the former is called patent (Ein offenbarer) (direct, immediate and explicit), the latter latent (Ein versteckter Widerspruch) (indirect, cryptic, mediate and implicit). That in which a true contradiction is patent is absurd (offenbar falsch) (dissonant).",["'with which posited': quo posito, singular; it may refer to B alone or to A and B together.","'dissonant': absonum."]],"met:14":["A ground (ein Grund), cf. §. 640, (condition, hypothesis,) is that from which it is cognizable why something is. What has a ground, or, of which something is the ground, is called its consequence (seine Folge, das in ihm gegründete), and dependent on it (das von ihm abhängende). A predicate by which something is either a ground, or a consequence, or both, is a nexus (der Zusammenhang, die Verknüpfung).",["'ground': ratio; 'consequence': rationatum.","'nexus': nexus, glossed by both Zusammenhang and Verknüpfung."]],"met:15":["What is considered, but not in nexus with those things that are posited outside it, is considered in itself (wird an und vor sich betrachtet). What is not representable even as to be considered in itself is impossible in itself (an und vor sich, innerlich, schlechterdings unmöglich), (intrinsically, simply, absolutely, *per se*). What considered in itself is possible, is possible in itself (an und vor sich, innerlich, unbedingt möglich) (intrinsically, absolutely, *per se*, simply).",["'considered': spectare."]],"met:16":["What, in nexus even with some things that are posited outside it, is nevertheless possible, is hypothetically possible (bedingt, äusserlich möglich) (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and *secundum quid*).",[]],"met:17":["What is not impossible except in some nexus with those things that are posited outside it, is hypothetically impossible (bedingt, äusserlich, in einem gewissen Zusammenhange unmöglich) (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and *secundum quid*).",[]],"met:18":["Nothing absolutely impossible is hypothetically possible, §. 15, 16. Therefore nothing hypothetically possible is absolutely impossible. Everything hypothetically impossible and possible is possible in itself, §. 17, 15. Therefore things absolutely impossible are neither hypothetically possible, nor impossible. Something absolutely possible is hypothetically impossible.",["'Something': quoddam, 'a certain'."]],"met:19":["The possible in nexus, i.e. in which there is a nexus, to which a nexus belongs, is connected (zusammenhängend, verknüpft) (rational), the impossible in nexus is irrational (ungereimt) (unconnected, incoherent). Hence irrational things are either in themselves, or hypothetically, impossible, §. 15, 17.",["'connected': connexum; 'rational': rationale, i.e. having a ground (ratio)."]],"met:20":["Everything possible either has a ground, or not, §. 10, If it has a ground, something is its ground, §. 8. If it does not have one, nothing is its ground, §. 7. Therefore the ground of everything possible is either nothing, or something, §. 10. If nothing were the ground of something possible, it would be cognizable from nothing why that is, §. 14, hence nothing itself representable and something, §. 8, nothing something, §. 14, 8. Hence something possible impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Therefore of everything possible something is the ground, or everything possible is a consequence, or nothing is without a ground, or, when something is posited, something is posited as its ground. This proposition is called the principle of ground, which you may gather also from §. 265, 279, partly by abstracting, partly by avoiding a circle.",["'or not': aut minus, literally 'or less'.","'which is absurd': q.a. (quod absurdum) in the text.","'principle of ground': principium rationis, also 'principle of reason'."]],"met:21":["The ground of each single thing in something is its sufficient ground (der zureichende Grund) (complete, total), the ground of only some things in the same is its insufficient ground (der unzulängliche) (incomplete, partial).",[]],"met:22":["Nothing is without a sufficient ground, or, when something is posited, something is posited as its sufficient ground. Each single thing in everything possible has a ground, §. 20, hence everything possible a sufficient ground, §. 21. This proposition is called the principle of sufficient ground (of fittingness).",["'of fittingness': convenientiae, also 'of agreement'."]],"met:23":["Everything possible is a ground, or nothing is without a consequence, nothing without corollary and reward, nothing altogether sterile, idle and unfruitful, or when something is posited something is posited as its consequence. For everything possible either has a consequence, or not, §. 10. If it has, something is its consequence, §. 8, if it does not have, nothing is its consequence, §. 7. Therefore the consequence of everything possible is either nothing, or something, §. 10. If nothing were the consequence of something possible, it could be cognized from this, §. 14, hence it would be something, §. 8, and so something possible impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Let this proposition be called the principle of consequence.",["'reward': auctoramentum, a wage or hire.","'which is absurd': q.a. in the text.","'it could be cognized from this': posset ex hoc cognosci; the subject is not expressed."]],"met:24":["Everything possible is a ground and a consequence, §. 20, 23, hence by a double nexus, §. 14, connected and rational, §. 19, cognizable as much *a priori* as *a posteriori*. Let this proposition be called the principle of things connected on both sides (*a parte ante*, and *a parte post*).",[]],"met:25":["The ground A of the ground B, is the ground of the consequence C. From the ground του B it can be cognized why C is, §. 23, hence A is the ground του C, §. 14.",["του: the Greek article, used in the Latin to mark the letter as a noun ('of the B', 'of the C')."]],"met:26":["The consequence C of the consequence B, is the consequence of the ground A, §. 25, 14.",[]],"met:27":["The ground A of some B, on which C depends, is the mediate ground (mittelbarer und entfernter) (further, remote) of this C, a ground not mediate is immediate (unmittelbarer und nächster Grund) (proximate).",[]],"met:28":["That ground is called a ground *secundum quid* (der Zwischen-Grund) (intermediate,) which has yet a further one, that which does not have one, such simply (der letzte Grund) (ultimate). The grounds and consequences of something either are considered also as grounds and consequences among themselves, subordinated (untereinander), or not, coordinated (neben einander stehende Gründe und Folgen).",["'such simply': simpliciter talis."]],"met:29":["When the consequence is posited, some ground of it is posited, § 20, 14, and a sufficient one, §. 22, or from the consequence to the ground, and to the sufficient one, V. C.",["V. C.: valet consequentia, 'the inference holds'."]],"met:30":["When the ground is posited, hence also the sufficient one, §. 21, the consequence is posited, §. 23, or from the ground, and the sufficient one, to the consequence V. C.",["V. C.: valet consequentia."]],"met:31":["When the ground is removed, and the sufficient one, some consequence is removed, because, this being posited, that too would be posited, §. 29.",["'removed': tollere, also 'cancel'."]],"met:32":["When the consequence is removed its ground is removed, and the sufficient one, because, this being posited, that too would be posited, §. 30.",[]],"met:33":["A and B connected with a third C are connected among themselves, A is connected with C connected with B, there is therefore in A that of which it can be cognized from B why it is, hence A and B are connected, §. 19.",["'there is therefore in A that of which': est ergo in A, de quo; the construction is elliptical, and 'why it is' may refer to A or to what is in A."]],"met:34":["What either is posited to be A, or is posited not to be A, is determined (bestimmt). But what is only posited to be either A, or not A, is indeterminate (unbestimmt). Or, if of a subject in respect of contradictory predicates nothing is posited, except that one or the other of them belongs to it, that subject is indeterminate in respect of these predicates; but it is determined if one or the other is posited in the subject. What can be determined is determinable (bestimmlich). That of which, therefore, it can be posited either that it is A, or that it is not A, is determinable.",[]],"met:35":["The ground of determining is the determining (das bestimmende). Every ground therefore, a sufficient one sufficiently, an insufficient one insufficiently, determines, §. 34, 21. Hence when the determining is posited, the determined is posited, §. 30, and vice versa, §. 29. When the determined is removed, the determining is removed, §. 32, and vice versa, §. 31.",["'vice versa': v.v. in the text."]],"met:36":["What are posited in something by determining (marks and predicates), are determinations (Bestimmungen), the one positive, and affirmative, §. 34, 10, which if it truly is, is reality, the other negative, §. 34, 10, which if it truly is, is negation (Verneinungen). An apparent negation is a cryptic reality, an apparent reality is vanity (Eitelkeit).",["'marks': notae.","'which if it truly is': quae si vere sit.","Verneinungen is plural, against the singular negatio."]],"met:37":["Determinations of the possible either are representable in it even if it is not yet considered in nexus, absolute (dem möglichen an und vor sich betrachtet schon), or only then, when it is considered in nexus, §. 10, respective (beziehungs-weise zukommende Bestimmungen) (assumptive). The respective determinations of possibles are respects (Beziehungen) (habitudes, τα προσ τι, relations in the wider sense, either *ad extra*, or *ad intra*). Respects of possibles not representable in the same considered in themselves are relations (Verhältnisse) (in the stricter sense, *ad extra*). The relations of possibles are their external (äussre) determinations (relative, *ad extra*, extrinsic), all the rest, internal (innre Bestimmungen).",["'respects': respectus; 'relations': relationes.","τα προσ τι: Greek, 'the things toward something', the category of relation.","*ad extra*, *ad intra*: 'toward the outside', 'toward the inside'."]],"met:38":["If in A there are the things that are in B, A and B are the same (einerlei). Things not the same are diverse (verschieden) (other).",[]],"met:39":["The internal determinations of the possible either are grounds of the remaining internal ones, in internal ones such simply, or not, §. 10. The former are first (principal), or essentials (die ersten oder wesentlichen Bestimmungen).",["'such simply': simpliciter tales, as in §. 28; the phrase may attach to rationes or to internis."]],"met:40":["The complex of essentials in the possible, or its internal possibility, is the essence (das Wesen) (being of the thing, formal ground, nature, cf. §. 430, quiddity, form, the formal of the whole, ουσια τινοτισ, substance, cf. §. 191, first concept of a being).",["'being of the thing': esse rei; 'formal ground': ratio formalis.","ουσια τινοτισ: Greek, 'the being of something', printed thus."]],"met:41":["Internal determinations of the possible, consequences of the essence, are affections (innere folgende Bestimmungen).",[]],"met:42":["An internal determination which is not an essential is a consequence of the essence, §. 39, 40, hence an affection, §. 41.",[]],"met:43":["When the essence in a possible is posited, the affections are posited, §. 41, 30.",[]],"met:44":["When the affections in a possible are posited, some essence is posited, §. 41, 29.",[]],"met:45":["When the essence is removed, some affections are removed, §. 41, 31.",[]],"met:46":["When the affections are removed, the essence is removed, §. 41, 32.",[]],"met:47":["All internal determinations of a possible are connected among themselves, each single one with each single one. For the affections are connected each single one with the essentials, §. 39, these with the essence, §. 40, 14. Hence each single determination with each single one, §. 33.",[]],"met:48":["A universal nexus (harmony) (ein allgemeiner Zusammenhang) is one which is in each single one.",["'in each single one': in singulis, as in §. 6."]],"met:49":["In the internal determinations of a possible there is a universal nexus §. 47, 48.",[]],"met:50":["Affections have a ground in the essence, §. 41, hence either a sufficient one, or not, §. 21, 10. The former are attributes (Eigenschaften), the latter modes (Zufälligkeiten) (predicable accidents, or logical ones, cf. §. 191, adjuncts, secondary predicates).",["'modes': modi, glossed Zufälligkeiten ('contingencies')."]],"met:51":["Attributes have a sufficient ground either in all the essentials, or in some only, §. 50, 10. The former are proper (besondre), the latter common (gemeine).",[]],"met:52":["Every determination of possibles is either an essential, §. 39, or an attribute, or a mode, §. 42, or a relation, §. 37.",[]],"met:53":["Everything possible is determined as regards possibility, §. 34, 8, hence possible in itself as regards internal possibility, §. 15, which since it is the essence, §. 40, everything possible has an essence, determined as regards essence. Therefore what is in every way indeterminate is nothing, §. 7.",["'as regards': qua.","'what is in every way indeterminate is nothing': omnimode indeterminatum nihil est, also readable as 'nothing is in every way indeterminate'.","The sentence beginning 'which since it is' is anacoluthic in the Latin."]],"met:54":["The possible, besides its essence, §. 53, either is determined as regards all the affections also compossible in it, or not, §. 34, 10. The former is called actual (wirklich), the latter a privative non-being (nothing, cf. §. 7) (merely possible) (das bloss mögliche, ein mögliches Nichts).",["'non-being': non ens."]],"met:55":["Existence (Wirklichkeit) (act, cf. §. 210, actuality) is the complex of the affections compossible in something, i.e. the complement of the essence or of the internal possibility, insofar as this is considered only as a complex of determinations, §. 40.",["'Existence': exsistentia, glossed Wirklichkeit, the German word for 'actuality' (actualitas) in §. 54."]],"met:56":["Every internal determination of a possible pertains either to its essence, or to its existence, §. 55, 42.",[]],"met:57":["Everything actual is internally possible, §. 54, or when existence is posited internal possibility is posited, §. 55, 40, from being to being able V. C.",["'from being to being able': ab esse ad posse.","V. C.: valet consequentia, 'the inference holds'."]],"met:58":["Nothing internally impossible is actual, §. 57, when internal possibility is removed, actuality is removed, §. 55, 40, or from not being able to not being V. C.",["'from not being able to not being': a non posse ad non esse."]],"met:59":["Something possible is not actual, §. 54, or when a certain possibility is posited actuality can nevertheless be removed, or from a certain being able to being N. V. C.",["'from a certain being able to being': a quodam posse ad esse.","N. V. C.: non valet consequentia, 'the inference does not hold'."]],"met:60":["Something not actual is possible, §. 59, when actuality is removed not all possibility is removed, or from not being to altogether not being able N. V. C.",["'from not being to altogether not being able': a non esse ad omnino non posse."]],"met:61":["The possible, determinable as regards existence, is a being (ein Ding).",["'a being': ens."]],"met:62":["A non-being (ein Unding) (negative, cf. §. 54) would be the possible, not determinable as regards existence. §. 61. But this is impossible, §. 10, and, if it seems to be a being, it is a feigned being (ein erdichtetes Ding) (of reasoning reason, cf. §. 647).",["'of reasoning reason': rationis ratiocinantis."]],"met:63":["Every being is possible, §. 61, has a ground, §. 20, a sufficient one, §. 22, and a consequence, §. 23, hence is connected in two ways, §. 24, has an essence, §. 53, and essentials, §. 40, hence affections, §. 43, in a universal nexus, §. 49. All determinations of a being are either essentials, or attributes, or modes, or relations, §. 52. When a being is posited, its essence is posited, hence all the essentials. When the essence is canceled, the being is canceled. When an essential is canceled, the essence is canceled, hence the being itself, §. 53, 40. Every internal determination of a being, finally, pertains either to its essence, or to its existence, §. 56.",["'Ground' and 'consequence' render ratio and rationatum; 'posited' and 'canceled' render ponere and tollere throughout."]],"met:64":["Through the essence the attributes of a being are sufficiently determined, §. 35, 50, hence when the essence is posited the attributes are posited, §. 35. Therefore when a being is posited its attributes are posited, §. 63, when an attribute is canceled the essence and the being are canceled, §. 35, 63.",[]],"met:65":["The modes of a being are not sufficiently determined through the essence, §. 50, 35, hence they are not determined, as to existence, § 55. A being, therefore, as to its actual modes, is undetermined through the essence, §. 34, 54, i.e. the modes can be absent and present with the essence of the thing remaining intact.",["'With the essence of the thing remaining intact' renders salva rei essentia.","'As to' renders qua."]],"met:66":["Existence is not repugnant to the essence, but is a reality, §. 36, compossible with it, §. 50, 55.",[]],"met:67":["The cognition of diversity is distinction (Unterscheidung), and the ground of distinction in distinguishing is the difference (*differentia*, *character*, *character distinctivus* in the wider sense, cf. §. 350, *nota*, *nota characteristica*) (Unterscheid, Merkmal, Kennezeichen, Unterscheidungs-Zeichen). Now every determination of a being is that from which it can be cognized that that being is neither undetermined, nor determined in another way, §. 36, 34. Therefore every determination is a difference of the being, §. 38, 14.",["'Difference' renders discrimen; the Latin synonyms Baumgarten lists after it are left in Latin."]],"met:68":["The differences of a being are either external or relative, or internal, §. 67, 37, and these either essential, which pertain to the essence, or accidental, which to the existence, §. 56, either absolute, or respective, §. 37.",["'Differences' renders discrimina."]],"met:69":["Internal differences can be represented in a being considered in itself, §. 68, 37, hence be cognized in whatever way, or be given (angegeben werden). Those given we either can also (without compresence) without assuming another, without relation to another, conceive (begreifen und verstehn) and understand, i.e. distinctly cognize, or we cannot. The former are qualities (Beschaffenheiten), the latter quantities (Grössen).",["'Conceive' and 'understand' render concipere and intelligere; Baumgarten's German pair is given for the first.","'The former ... the latter' renders illa ... haec."]],"met:70":["Things the same as to quality are similar (ähnlich), as to quantity, equal = (gleich), as to both, congruent _ (gleichartig). Things diverse as to quality, dissimilar (unähnlich), as to quantity, unequal (ungleich), as to both diverse, discongruent (ungleichartig).",["The signs = and _ after 'equal' and 'congruent' stand so in the transcription; they appear to be Baumgarten's signs for equality and congruence, imperfectly transcribed, and further signs after the other terms may have been lost.","'Discongruent' renders discongruentia."]],"met:71":["Merely similar things are not congruent, hence they differ in quantity, §. 70, hence quantity is the internal difference of merely similar things.",[]],"met:72":["Determinations of a being are separated (werden getrennt), if out of those posited together some are canceled. Hence those are inseparable (unzertrennlich), of which, when posited together, none can be canceled.",[]],"met:73":["When a being is posited, the essence is posited, §. 63, therefore the complex of essentials, §. 40, hence when a being is posited all the essentials are posited together, and indeed in such a way that none can be canceled, §. 63, 40. Therefore the essentials of a being are inseparable through themselves, §. 72, 15. That is one (Eins) whose determinations are inseparable, and transcendentally (wesentlich eins) indeed, whose determinations are inseparable through themselves. Therefore every being is a transcendental one.",["'Through themselves' renders per se.","Baumgarten's German for transcendentaliter unum is wesentlich eins, 'essentially one'."]],"met:74":["A one and B one, e.g., partly the same, partly diverse, are many (Viele). Whatever we may think, they are either many, or not many, §. 10. The former determination is multitude (plurality) (die Vielheit, Mehrheit), the latter categorical unity (die Einheit).",["'E.g.' renders e.c. (exempli causa).","'Multitude' and 'plurality' render multitudo and pluralitas."]],"met:75":["The single determinations of a being are each one, §. 73, partly the same, insofar as they are determinations of the same being, partly diverse, §. 38, hence many, §. 74. Multitude can therefore be given in a being considered in itself, §. 69, and is its internal difference, §. 37, which, however, we cannot understand, unless one other is assumed, §. 74, 38, hence multitude is a quantity, §. 69.",[]],"met:76":["Since the inseparability of determinations is the impossibility of separation, §. 72, it is either absolute, or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. Hence unity is either absolute, or hypothetical, §. 73.",[]],"met:77":["Whatever is, which many are not, is unique (one exclusively such) (Einzig).",["Quod multa non sunt may be read as 'which many are not' or as 'such that there are not many'; the English keeps the Latin's construction."]],"met:78":["If many are posited beside or after one another, they are conjoined (verbunden werden). The conjunction of several is either the same, or diverse, §. 10, 38. If the former, it is coordination (zusammenordnen), and its identity order (Ordnung). The science of order was once music in the wider sense.",["'Conjoined' and 'conjunction' render coniungere and coniunctio; Baumgarten's German is verbinden.","'Many' renders multa, 'several' plures."]],"met:79":["Diversity in the conjunction of several is confusion (disorder) (Unordnung und Verwirrung). The conjunction of inseparables is unition (Vereinigung).",["'Disorder' renders inordinatio; 'unition' renders unitio."]],"met:80":["Every determination has a ground, §. 20, 36, and whichever can be cognized from some determinate ground is said to be conformable to the ground (accordant, agreeing) (dem Grunde gemäss, übereinstimmig und zukommend).",["'Conformable', 'accordant', 'agreeing' render conformis, conveniens, consentiens."]],"met:81":["If, when A is posited, B is canceled, A and B are opposed (entgegen gesetzt, mit einander streitend).",[]],"met:82":["A determination opposed to that which is conformable to the ground is contrary to the ground (difform, unaccordant) or a defect (Mangel).",["'Difform' and 'unaccordant' render difformis and disconveniens."]],"met:83":["A proposition stating a determination conformable to the ground is a norm (rule (Richtschnur, Gesetz), law), indeed, in a wider sense, the representation of a determination conformable to the ground.",["The transcription reads Propositis, here taken as Propositio, 'a proposition'.","'Norm', 'rule', 'law' render norma, regula, lex."]],"met:84":["Wherever there are determinations, there are laws, §. 83, 80.",[]],"met:85":["The respect of a being determined from its conjunction with others is position (Stelle). Where, therefore, there is position, there are laws, §. 84, 37.",["'Position' renders positus (fourth declension)."]],"met:86":["By conjunction position is determined, hence there are laws in a conjunction, §. 85, in the same one the same laws, §. 38. Therefore in an order several are conjoined conformably to the same ground, §. 83, 78. What is in A and B is common (Gemein) to them. What is in A, not in B, is, with respect to B, proper (Eigen) to A, therefore in an order there are common rules.",["Baumgarten writes τῳ A, with the Greek article, for 'to A'."]],"met:87":["In a diverse conjunction there are diverse laws, §. 86, 38. Therefore in confusion there are no common laws, §. 79, 86.",[]],"met:88":["If the rule of an order is one only, it is called simple (Eine einfache), if several, a composite order (zusammengesetzte Ordnung).",["'One only' renders unica."]],"met:89":["Metaphysical truth (die metaphysische Wahrheit) (real, objective, material) is the order of several in one, truth in the essentials and attributes of a being, transcendental (die nothwendige metaphysische Wahrheit).",["Baumgarten's German for veritas transcendentalis is die nothwendige metaphysische Wahrheit, 'necessary metaphysical truth'."]],"met:90":["Since all the determinations of a being are conjoined, the essential ones according to the principle of contradiction, §. 40, 7, and the accidental ones, the attributes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 64, 7, and of ground, §. 20, and of the sufficient one, §. 22, 50, the modes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 65, 7, and of ground, §. 42, 20, the essentials and affections according to the principle of consequence, §. 23, 41, hence according to common rules, §. 83, 86, every being is transcendentally true, § 89.",["'Principle of ground' and 'principle of consequence' render principium rationis and principium rationati; 'of the sufficient one' renders the elliptical et sufficientis."]],"met:91":["Confusion opposed to transcendental truth would be a dream taken objectively (ein Traum, das geträumte) (cf. §. 593). The aggregate of dreams would be a fabulous world (das Land der Wünsche) (cf. §. 354).",["'Fabulous world' renders mundus fabulosus; Baumgarten's German, das Land der Wünsche, is 'the land of wishes'."]],"met:92":["Catholic principles (cf. §. 307, 311) (allgemeine Grund-Sätze) (universal) are those common to all single beings. Metaphysically true things are determined by catholic principles, §. 7, 20, 22, 23, agreeably, §. 90, 80, and those which are determined conformably to these principles are metaphysically true, §. 89. Hence metaphysical truth can be defined through the agreement of a being with the catholic principles.",["Principiis catholicis may be read as instrumental ('determined by catholic principles') or as governed by convenienter ('agreeably to catholic principles'); the English keeps both open.","The text twice prints methaphysice, methaphysica, a suspected misprint for metaphysice, metaphysica; translated as 'metaphysically', 'metaphysical'.","'Agreeably' and 'agreement' render convenienter and convenientia."]],"met:93":["Objective certainty (Gewissheit der Dinge) (cf. §. 531) is the apperceptibility of the truth in a being. Now all truth of a being is clearly cognizable, §. 90, 8. Therefore every being is objectively certain.",[]],"met:94":["If several taken together constitute the sufficient ground of one, they agree (übereinstimmen). The agreement itself is perfection (Vollkommenheit), and the one in which there is agreement, the determining ground of perfection (focus of perfection) (Grund oder Brennpunkt der Vollkommenheit).",["'Agree' and 'agreement' render consentire and consensus here, as convenientia in §. 92."]],"met:95":["In perfection several are determined conformably to the same ground, §. 94, 80, therefore there is order in perfection, §. 78, and common rules of perfection, §. 86.",[]],"met:96":["If the determining ground of perfection is one only, the perfection is simple (eine einfache), if several, then the perfection is composite (eine zusammengesetzte), §. 88, 95.",[]],"met:97":["Opposed rules are said to collide (mit einander streitende Richtschnuren), and a defect from colliding rules of perfection an exception (Ausnahme), according as the norms collide by a true or by a merely apparent opposition, §. 81, either true, or apparent, §. 12.",["'According as' renders prout."]],"met:98":["The agreement of the essentials is (essential) transcendental (wesentliche) perfection, that of the affections accidental (innre ausserwesentliche), each internal (innre). The agreement of relations is external (äussre) perfection.",["'Each' renders utraque, 'each of the two'."]],"met:99":["The essentials of every being agree toward its essence, §. 63, 40, and the attributes, §. 50, 94. Therefore every being is transcendentally perfect.",["'Agree toward' renders consentiunt ad."]],"met:100":["That is good (gut) which, when posited, perfection is posited. Therefore every being is transcendentally good, §. 99.",["Quo posito ponitur perfectio: 'with which posited, perfection is posited'."]],"met:101":["That is necessary (nothwendig) whose opposite is impossible, what is not necessary is contingent (zufällig).",[]],"met:102":["That whose opposite is impossible in itself is necessary in itself (an sich, schlechterdings, unbedingt, nothwendig) (metaphysically, intrinsically, absolutely, geometrically, logically). That whose opposite is only extrinsically impossible is hypothetically necessary (bedingt nothwendig) (*secundum quid*). The determination of a being by which it is necessary is its necessity (Nothwendigkeit). Therefore necessity is either absolute (die schlechterdings so genannte (unbedingte)) (*consequentis*), or hypothetical (die bedingte) (*consequentiae*), the former that by which something is in and through itself, the latter that by which something is only hypothetically necessary.",["Necessitas consequentis and consequentiae: 'necessity of the consequent' and 'of the consequence'; secundum quid, 'in a certain respect'.","'In and through itself' renders in et per se; the clause stands so, without a predicate."]],"met:103":["Possibility involves the opposite, §. 81, 7, hence the possibility of a being is a necessary determination of it, §. 101, the intrinsic intrinsically, the extrinsic extrinsically, §. 102.",["Possibilitas oppositum involvit is translated as it stands; the sense may require Possibilitatis oppositum involvit, 'the opposite of possibility involves [contradiction]', and the text may be defective."]],"met:104":["That whose opposite is absolutely possible is contingent in itself (an sich, schlechterdings (unbedingt)) (through itself, intrinsically), that whose opposite is also hypothetically possible is also extrinsically contingent (auch bedingt und äusserlich zufällig) (hypothetically). The determination of a being by which it is contingent is its contingency (Zufälligkeit). Therefore contingency is either absolute (die unbedingte, schlechterdings so genannte), by which something is at least in and through itself, or hypothetical (die bedingte), by which something is hypothetically contingent.",["'At least' renders saltim; the clause stands so, without a predicate."]],"met:105":["Nothing absolutely necessary is in any way contingent, §. 102, 104. Therefore whatever is in any way contingent is not absolutely necessary. Everything hypothetically necessary is in and through itself contingent, §. 18. Therefore something in and through itself contingent is hypothetically necessary. Everything hypothetically contingent is also in itself, §. 104.",[]],"met:106":["The essences of things are absolutely necessary in them, §. 40, 103.",[]],"met:107":["When the essentials and attributes are canceled the essence is canceled, §. 63, 64, hence the internal impossibility of the being is posited, §. 81, 40. Both the essentials, therefore, and the attributes are absolutely necessary determinations of a being, §. 103.",[]],"met:108":["The opposite of modes is absolutely possible in a being, §. 65, 81, hence modes are determinations of a being contingent in themselves, §. 104. Therefore not absolutely necessary, §. 105. The opposite of the absolutely necessary determinations in a being is absolutely impossible in the same, §. 102. Therefore they themselves are absolutely possible in the being, §. 81, hence representable in the same considered in itself, §. 15. Relations, however, are not representable in a being considered in itself, §. 37. Therefore no relations of a being are absolutely necessary, all are contingent, §. 101.",[]],"met:109":["Since the opposite of existence is possible in something, §. 54, 55, existence will either be absolutely necessary, or contingent in itself, §. 102, 10. A being whose existence is absolutely necessary is a necessary being (das nothwendige Ding), one whose existence is intrinsically contingent is a contingent being (ein zufälliges Ding).",["'Being' renders ens; Baumgarten's German here is Ding, 'thing'."]],"met:110":["All the internal determinations of a necessary being are absolutely necessary. For they pertain either to the essence, or to the existence, §. 56. Both the latter and the former is absolutely necessary in a necessary being, §. 109, 106.",[]],"met:111":["In a necessary being there are no modes, §. 110, 108. Therefore that in which modes inhere is a contingent being, §. 109.",[]],"met:112":["Every contingent being has modes. For suppose that no modes inhere in some contingent being, all its affections are absolutely necessary, §. 52, 107, hence its existence too is absolutely necessary, §. 55, which is absurd, §. 109.",["'Which is absurd' renders the abbreviation q.a. (quod absurdum)."]],"met:113":["The determinations of a one are inseparable, §. 73, hence the opposite of each is impossible, §. 72, 81, they are therefore necessary determinations of a one, §. 101, and indeed absolutely those of an absolutely one, hypothetically those of a hypothetically one, §. 102, 76. Things united are necessarily conjoined, §. 79, either in themselves, or hypothetically, §. 102.",[]],"met:114":["Necessary things are determinable in only one single way and manner. For they are either A, or non-A. A third way, a third manner of determinability is impossible, §. 10. Now suppose a necessary thing to be determinable by A; non-A is the opposite of this determination, §. 81, hence impossible, §. 101. There are therefore not several ways and manners of determinability, besides the unique A, §. 77. Let things determinable in only one single way and manner be A, their opposite would be non-A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. Let them be non-A, their opposite would be A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. A third is not given, §. 10. Therefore the opposite of things determinable in only one single way and manner is impossible, and so they themselves are necessary, §. 102. Contingent things are not necessary, hence they are neither determinable in only one single way and manner, nor in more than two, §. 10. They are therefore determinable in a twofold way. Things determinable in a twofold way and manner are not necessary, hence contingent, §. 102. Therefore there can be defined 1) the necessary, through 'in only one single', 2) the contingent, through 'determinable in a twofold way', 3) necessity through the uniqueness, 4) contingency through the twofoldness of determinability.",["'Way and manner' renders modo ac ratione; ratio is elsewhere 'ground'.","'Uniqueness' and 'twofoldness' render unicitas and duplicitas.","'Nor in more than two' renders nec pluribus ac duobus."]],"met:115":["A one that is hypothetically such has determinations separable in themselves, §. 76, 18, hence its unity is intrinsically contingent, §. 104.",[]],"met:116":["Transcendental unity is absolutely necessary, §. 73, 102, hence there is no opposite of it, §. 102, 15.",[]],"met:117":["Since the opposites of order are confusion, §. 79, 78, and conjunction not made, §. 78, 81, order is absolutely necessary in things necessarily conjoined through themselves, and indeed in the same way, §. 102. Wherever, therefore, both the conjunction and the identity of the conjunction are not absolutely necessary, there order is contingent in itself, §. 104.",[]],"met:118":["Transcendental truth is order in the essentials and attributes, §. 89, hence in things necessarily conjoined through themselves, §. 78, 107, and indeed in the same way, §. 7, 22, hence transcendental truth is absolutely necessary, §. 117, and has no opposite, §. 102, 15.",["'through themselves': per se."]],"met:119":["Order of several things in one, of which the non-conjunction or confusion is internally possible in a being, is truth contingent in itself, §. 117, 89.",["'non-conjunction': non coniunctio."]],"met:120":["A dream taken objectively and a fabulous world are non-beings, §. 118, 91, and if they seem to be beings, they are feigned beings, §. 62.",["'taken objectively': obiective sumptum."]],"met:121":["The opposite of perfection is imperfection (Unvollkommenheit), and indeed 1) simple non-agreement, if in several things taken together certain ones are not grounds of one thing: this is imperfection privatively so called, 2) disagreement, if in several things taken together certain ones agree toward one thing, certain ones toward its opposite: this is imperfection contrarily so called, §. 81, 94.",["'non-agreement': non consensus; 'disagreement': dissensus.","'grounds': rationes.","'agree toward one thing': consentiunt ad unum."]],"met:122":["The perfection of those things of which the non-agreement, and hence also the disagreement, is impossible in itself, is absolutely necessary, §. 121, 102. But perfection in those things for which not to agree or also to disagree is possible in itself, is contingent in itself, §. 104, 121.",[]],"met:123":["Transcendental perfection is absolutely necessary, §. 122, 94, hence has no opposite imperfection, §. 121, 102.",[]],"met:124":["Those things succeed one another (Folgen aufeinander) (are successive to one another), of which one exists after another. The determination of beings by which they are successive to one another is their succession (die Folge).",["'succession': successio, glossed die Folge, the word otherwise rendered 'sequel; sequence'."]],"met:125":["That whose determinations succeed one another is changed (wird verändert): hence mutable (veränderlich) (variable) is that whose determinations can succeed one another; that whose determinations cannot succeed one another is immutable (unveränderlich) (fixed, invariable, constant). But the very succession of determinations in a being is the change (Veränderung) of it, and at the same time of its determinations.",["'is changed': mutatur; 'change': mutatio."]],"met:126":["The change of a being is either internal (eine innre), of internals, or external (äussre Veränderung) or relative, of relations, §. 125, 37.",["'of internals': internorum."]],"met:127":["Mutability (Veränderlichkeit) of a being, or the possibility of changes in the same, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 16. Immutability (Unveränderlichkeit) of a being, or the impossibility of changes in the same, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 17.",[]],"met:128":["Nothing immutable through itself is hypothetically mutable. Therefore nothing hypothetically mutable is absolutely immutable. Everything hypothetically immutable is mutable in itself. Everything hypothetically mutable is mutable in itself. Therefore absolutely immutable things are neither hypothetically mutable, nor immutable. Something absolutely mutable is hypothetically immutable, §. 127, 18.",["'through itself': per se."]],"met:129":["The determinations of mutables can succeed one another, §. 125, hence mutables are determinable in several ways, §. 74, 34. Necessary things are not determinable in several ways, §. 114. Therefore necessary things are immutable, §. 125.",[]],"met:130":["Absolutely necessary things are absolutely immutable, hypothetically necessary things are hypothetically immutable, §. 129, 127.",[]],"met:131":["Nothing mutable is necessary, §. 129, hence everything mutable is contingent, §. 101, and indeed what is mutable in itself is contingent in itself, what is hypothetically mutable is hypothetically contingent, §. 104, 127.",[]],"met:132":["The essences of things, §. 106, essentials and attributes, §. 107, the existence of a necessary being, §. 109, all its internal determinations, §. 110, unity, §. 116, truth, §. 118, and perfection, transcendental, §. 123, are absolutely and internally immutable, §. 130, 126.",["'transcendental': transcendentales, plural in the Latin, so qualifying unity, truth and perfection together."]],"met:133":["The modes of a being can succeed one another in it considered in itself, §. 124, 65, hence both they themselves, and the being in which they are, are absolutely mutable, §. 125, 127. Now every contingent being has modes, §. 112. Therefore every contingent being is absolutely and internally mutable, §. 126. The relations of a being are intrinsically contingent, §. 108. Therefore they can succeed one another in it considered in itself, §. 124, 104. Hence they are absolutely mutable in every being, §. 127. Every being, as regards all its relations, is mutable, §. 125.",["'as regards': qua."]],"met:134":["The essence is not mutable, §. 132, hence every contingent being is mutable as regards existence, §. 133, 56. Hence the existence of a contingent being is mutable, §. 125, and is thus neither an essential, nor an attribute, §. 132, yet an internal determination, §. 55, therefore a mode, §. 52. That whose existence is a mode, its existence is absolutely mutable, §. 133, hence also intrinsically contingent, §. 131. Therefore that whose existence is a mode is a contingent being, §. 109. A contingent being can hence be defined through a being whose existence is a mode.",[]],"met:135":["When a negation is posited a reality is removed, §. 36, 10. Hence negations and realities are mutually opposed to one another, §. 81. Both the realities themselves and the beings in which they are, are called real beings (etwas bejahendes) or positive ones. But negations, negative beings (etwas verneinendes).",[]],"met:136":["A merely negative being would be that in which there was no reality, hence neither possibility, §. 8, nor rationality, §. 19, nor actuality, §. 55, nor unity, §. 73, nor truth, §. 89, nor perfection, §. 94, realities, §. 36. Therefore a merely negative being is a non-being, and, if it seems a being, it is a feigned being, §. 62. Since therefore some reality is in every being, every being is real, §. 135.",[]],"met:137":["Since every being is real, §. 136, either no negation will be in it, or some will be in it together with its realities, §. 10, and this indeed either absolutely necessary, or contingent in itself, §. 102, 104. The former is negation strictly so called (eine Verneinung in engerer Bedeutung) (negative being strictly so called). The latter is privation (eine Beraubung) or privative being. Negative essentials and attributes are negations strictly so called, §. 106, 107, negative modes are privations, §. 108.",["'strictly so called': stricte dicta."]],"met:138":["No privations are absolutely necessary in a being, §. 137, 105, hence internal ones are neither essentials, nor attributes, §. 107. Therefore modes, §. 52. A necessary being therefore does not have internal privations, §. 111, and that in which they are is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"met:139":["A negative being, as such, is not positive, §. 135, but, if it agreed, as negation, toward one reality, it would be a reality, §. 94, 36, hence a negative being, as such, does not agree in the real being in which it is, §. 137, toward one reality.",["'as such': qua tale; 'as negation': qua negatio."]],"met:140":["Realities, as such, do not agree except toward realities. For to be the consequence of mere realities is a reality, §. 36, 14.",["'consequence': rationatum."]],"met:141":["Every being is perfect, §. 99, and real, §. 137, hence its perfection, as such, is the agreement of realities toward one, §. 94, 140.",[]],"met:142":["When negations are posited in a being, non-agreement is posited, §. 141, 139, hence imperfection, §. 121, hence when negations strictly so called are posited in a being, an absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 137.",[]],"met:143":["An absolutely necessary imperfection of a being either is an attribute of it, or an essential, §. 107, 108, but, because in realities there is not the sufficient ground of negations, §. 139, there is no imperfection in the attributes of a being in whose essentials it is not also, §. 50, nor is there an imperfection in the essentials of a being in whose affections it would not also be, §. 23.",["'in whose': in cuius, referring to the being (entis)."]],"met:144":["When privations are posited in a being, imperfection is posited, §. 142, but one contingent in itself, §. 138.",[]],"met:145":["There is no essential perfection without accidental, §. 98, 140, nor accidental without essential, §. 98, there is no essential imperfection without accidental, nor accidental without essential, §. 143, 98, nor are essential perfection and imperfection opposed, §. 81, 142.",[]],"met:146":["That by the positing of which imperfection is posited is evil (Das Übel, Böse), hence negations are evil, §. 142, and these either strictly so called, metaphysical evil (das schlechterdings nothwendige), by the positing of which an absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 142, or privations, contingent evil (das zufällige) (physical broadly so called, cf. §. 788), by the positing of which an imperfection contingent in itself is posited, §. 144.",["'by the positing of which': quo posito.","'evil': malum."]],"met:147":["When the realities of a being are posited its perfection is posited, §. 141, hence realities are good, §. 100, and indeed the absolutely necessary ones, metaphysical good (das schlechterdings nothwendige), those contingent in themselves, contingent good (das zufällige Gute) (physical broadly so called, cf. §. 787).",["'are good': sunt bona, plural ('goods')."]],"met:148":["The complex of all the determinations compossible in a being is its determination in every way (durchgängige, völlige, vollständige Bestimmung). Hence a being either is determined in every way, or less, §. 10. The former is singular (einzeln) (individual), the latter universal (allgemeine). Each, in respect of all the less determined things which it contains in itself, is called lower (das untere, niedrigere, tiefere, bestimmtere), those in respect of this, higher (die obern, höheren, allgemeinern, unbestimmtern).",["'determination in every way': omnimoda determinatio; 'determined in every way': omnimode determinatum.","'lower': inferius; 'higher': superiora."]],"met:149":["A universal being considered in its lower, and a singular considered as regards its other predicates also, besides a certain universal, is considered in the concrete (in mehrerer Bestimmung betrachtet) and is then called concrete (unabgesondert). A universal being which is indeed attended to, yet not in its lower, and a singular in which yet only a certain higher of it is attended to, is considered in the abstract (nur in gewisser Bestimmung betrachtet), and is then called abstract (abgesondert). The universal in the concrete is the physical universal (das allgemeine im bestimmtern) (in the many, in the thing), the universal in the abstract is the logical (das allgemeine im denkenden) (after the many, after the thing).",["'in the concrete', 'in the abstract': in concreto, in abstracto.","'in the many, in the thing': in multis, in re; 'after the many, after the thing': post multa, post rem."]],"met:150":["The universal representable in the concrete in individuals alone, or, that which contains only individuals under itself, is a species (Art, Gattung), that which is representable in the concrete also in universals, or, that which contains universals also under itself, is a genus (Geschlecht), and of these the lowest (das Unterste), that which is in no genus, or, that which contains no genus under itself, the highest (das Oberste), that in which there is no genus, or, that which is contained under no genus, subalterns (untergeordnete, oder Untergeschlechte), finally, are called those which are not highest.",["'species' is glossed both Art and Gattung, the latter the word otherwise rendered 'genus'.","The clauses 'that which is in no genus' (lowest) and 'that in which there is no genus' (highest) read as if transposed; translated as printed."]],"met:151":["The determinations of a lower being, undetermined in its higher, are the difference (die genauere Bestimmung) of the former. Hence the generic difference (eines Geschlechtes) is the complex of the determinations determined in a genus, undetermined in its higher. The specific difference (einer Art) is the complex of the determinations of a species undetermined in its lowest genus. The numerical difference (eines jeden einzeln) (haecceity, principle of individuation) is the complex of the determinations of an individual undetermined in the species, §. 148.",["'of the former': illius, distinguished in the Latin from eius ('its')."]],"met:152":["Singulars are internally determined altogether, §. 148, hence actual, §. 54.",[]],"met:153":["Since the higher is in the lower, §. 149, 148, there is in the individual the species, the lowest genus, the subalterns, the highest, in the species the genera, in the lowest and subaltern genus the higher ones, in all the lower ones the highest, §. 150.",[]],"met:154":["The determinations of the highest genus are in its lower ones, §. 148, i.e. the subaltern genera, the lowest, species and individuals; the determinations of a subaltern genus are in its lower genera, species, and individuals; the determinations of the lowest genus are in the species and individuals; the determinations of a species are in the individuals, §. 153, 35, i.e. the determinations of the higher are in its lower, whether they be positive or negative, §. 36. This proposition is called the *dictum de O. et N.*",["*dictum de O. et N.*: dictum de omni et nullo, 'what is said of all and of none', abbreviated in the text."]],"met:155":["One thing altogether the same as many taken together is a whole (das Ganze), and many things taken together altogether the same as the whole are its parts (Theile). Those things which are to be taken together with a given part, so that it is altogether the same as the whole, are its coparts, or complements to the whole (Ergänzungen) (supplements).",["'coparts': compartes."]],"met:156":["Any part taken together with its complements to the whole is an actual part (ein wirklicher), taken together with others it is a potential part (ein möglicher Theil).",[]],"met:157":["The whole is altogether the same as its actual parts, §. 155, 156, hence both equal, and similar, and congruent, §. 70.",[]],"met:158":["A being whose essence is a part of another is an incomplete being (ein unvollständiges), one whose essence is not a part of another is a complete being (ein vollständiges Ding).",[]],"met:159":["A multitude of parts is magnitude (die Grösse des Ganzen) (absolute, cf. §. 161) or quantity, §. 75, continuous. A multitude of wholes is number (eine Zahl) (absolute, cf. 161) or quantity, §. 75, discrete. If the wholes of which there is a number are again considered as parts, the number is fractional (ein Bruch) (fraction, *minutia*), if not, the number is integral (eine ganze Zahl).",["'magnitude': magnitudo; 'quantity': quantitas.","'the number is': numerusest, printed run together."]],"met:160":["That of which a part is equal to a whole is greater > (das grössere), a whole equal to a part is less < (das kleinere).",["The signs > and < are printed in the text."]],"met:161":["The minimum (das kleinste) is greater than nothing alone, or, that than which a lesser is impossible, the maximum (das grösseste) is, less than nothing alone, or, that than which a greater is impossible. A greater multitude is comparative multitude (eine Menge), Cf. §. 74, a lesser, fewness (Wenigkeit). A greater magnitude is comparative magnitude (eine Grösse des Ganzen und Zahl, auch in der Vergleichung), cf. §. 159, a lesser, smallness (Kleinigkeit). A greater number is comparative, cf. §. 159, a lesser, rarity°°). °°) Seltenheit.",["'less than nothing alone': solo nihilo minus, as printed; the sense looks doubtful.","°°) Seltenheit: the German equivalent of raritas, given as a footnote."]],"met:162":["To be changed into less is to be diminished (vermindert), to be changed into greater, to be increased (vermehrt werden). Whatever therefore can be increased or diminished is mutable, §. 125, and indeed as regards quantity, §. 160, 70, hence internally, §. 126, 69.",[]],"met:163":["The essences of things, essentials and attributes, all the internal determinations of a necessary being, unity in beings, truth, and perfection, transcendental, cannot intrinsically be increased or diminished, §. 162, 132.",["'transcendental': transcendentales, plural, as in §. 132."]],"met:164":["An internal determination of a being which can be increased or diminished is a mode, §. 163, 52, and that in which such an internal determination occurs, which can be increased or diminished in it itself, is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"met:165":["The least possibility is the non-repugnance of the fewest least things, §. 161, 8. The more, therefore, the greater the things that are compossible, the greater is the possibility, §. 160, until it is the greatest, where the most, greatest things are compossible, §. 161. Hence every hypothetical possibility of a being is greater than its intrinsic possibility, §. 16, 15.",["'least', 'greatest': minimus, maximus, as in the minimum and maximum of §. 161."]],"met:166":["The least ground is that which has a single least consequence, §. 161. The more, therefore, the greater the consequences it has, the greater it is, §. 160, until it becomes the greatest, having the most, greatest consequences, §. 161. The magnitude of a ground from the number of consequences is fecundity (Fruchtbarkeit), from their magnitude weight (Wichtigkeit) (gravity, dignity, nobility).",["'ground': ratio; 'consequence': rationatum.","'weight': pondus."]],"met:167":["The least nexus is that of a single least ground, §. 166, 14. The more, therefore, the greater the grounds, the greater the nexus, §. 160, until it becomes the greatest, of the most, greatest grounds, §. 161, i.e. the most fecund and weightiest, §. 166. A greater nexus is harmony.",[]],"met:168":["The least hypothetical possibility is that by which a single least being enjoys the least nexus, §. 16, 161. Therefore the more things, the greater things, in the greater nexus are possible, the greater is the hypothetical possibility §. 160, until it is the greatest, where the most and greatest things are possible in the greatest nexus, §. 161, i.e. the most fruitful, weightiest consequences of the most fruitful and weightiest grounds, §. 167.",["'ground': ratio; 'consequence': rationatum.","'fruitful': fecundus; 'weighty': gravis."]],"met:169":["The sufficient ground is the most fruitful of grounds, §. 166, 21, among sufficient ones, however, the least is that which suffices for a single least consequence, §. 166, 161. Therefore the more, the greater consequences it suffices for, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is the greatest, sufficient for the most and greatest consequences, §. 161, and the same the most fruitful and noblest, §. 166.",[]],"met:170":["A more remote ground is greater than a nearer, §. 166, 27, hence one sufficient simply as such is greater than all those sufficient *secundum quid*, §. 169, 28.",["'more remote': ulterior; 'nearer': propior.","*secundum quid*: in a certain respect, as opposed to simpliciter, 'simply'."]],"met:171":["The least essence is the complex of the fewest least essentials, §. 40, 161. The more, the greater the essentials are, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is the greatest, the complex of the most and greatest essentials, §. 161.",["'complex': complexus."]],"met:172":["The least universal nexus is where the single things are either least grounds, or their consequences, §. 48, 167. Therefore the more the single things become either greater grounds, or consequences of greater grounds, or both, the greater is the universal nexus, §. 160, until it is the greatest, where the single things are the greatest grounds, §. 161, i.e. the most fruitful and weightiest, §. 166.",["'the single things': singula."]],"met:173":["The least unity is if the fewest least determinations of a single least thing are inseparable, §. 73, 161. Therefore the more, the greater the determinations, the more things, the greater things they are inseparable of, the greater the unity is, §. 160, until it is the greatest, where the most and greatest determinations of the most and greatest things are inseparable, §. 161. The least uniqueness is that by which one least thing differs from the fewest least things by a single least distinction, §. 77, 161. Therefore the greater that is, from the more, the greater things, by the more, the greater differences it is distinguished, the greater is the uniqueness, §. 160, until it is the greatest, by which the greatest thing is distinguished from all, even the greatest in their kind, by the most and greatest differences, §. 161.",["'uniqueness': unicitas.","'from all': the text prints ob omnibus, apparently for ab omnibus; translated as 'from'.","'kind': genus."]],"met:174":["The least identity is if a single least determination is common to the fewest least things, §. 38, 161. Hence the more, the greater the determinations, the more, the greater the things to which they are common, the greater is the identity, §. 160, until it is the greatest, the community of the most and greatest determinations in the most and greatest things, §. 161.",["'community': communio."]],"met:175":["The least order is the least identity in conjunction, §. 78, 161. Therefore the greater the identity of conjunction is, the greater the order becomes, §. 160, 174, until it is the greatest, where there is the greatest identity of conjunction, §. 161, i.e. where the most and greatest things are conjoined as often, and as much, in the same way as often, and as much, as they can, §. 174. The least union is the least necessary conjunction of the fewest least things, §. 79, 113, 161. Therefore the more things are conjoined, the greater the union is extensively, the greater the things, the greater the necessity with which they are conjoined, the greater the union is intensively, until the conjunction of the most things is necessary extensively, or of two only, or three, but of the greatest and the most necessary that can be between them, is intensively the greatest union.",["'union': unitio; 'conjunction': coniunctio.","The last clause is construed as in the Latin: whether 'sit intensive maxima unitio' governs both alternatives or only the second is left open."]],"met:176":["Since the cognizability of a determination from its ground is its conformity with the ground (die Übereinstimmung einer Bestimmung mit ihrem Grunde), §. 80, this conformity will be the least, if the determination is cognizable only from the least ground, §. 161, 166, with the least possibility of cognition, §. 165. The greater the ground from which the determination is to be cognized, the more possible this cognition, the greater the conformity of the determination with the ground, §. 160, until it is the greatest, most cognizable from the greatest ground, §. 161.",["'cognizability': cognoscibilitas; 'conformity': conformitas."]],"met:177":["A determination conformable to a sufficient, more fruitful, weightier ground, §. 166, 169, hence to a more remote one and to one simply such, §. 170, has a greater conformity than one conformable to an insufficient, less fruitful, and less weighty one, §. 166, 169, to a nearer one or to one sufficient *secundum quid*, §. 170, only, §. 176.",["'conformable': conformis."]],"met:178":["The least defect is the one opposed to a determination of the least conformity with the ground, §. 82, 161, hence it is the greater, the greater the conformity in its opposite, §. 160, 176, until it is the greatest, opposed to a determination in which there is the greatest conformity, §. 176, 161.",[]],"met:179":["A defect contrary to a sufficient, more fruitful, weightier, more remote and simply such ground is greater than one contrary to an insufficient, less fruitful, less weighty, nearer ground and one sufficient only *secundum quid*, §. 178, 177.",[]],"met:180":["The magnitude of the conformity with the ground in the determination that a law enunciates is the strength of the law (Stärke der Richtschnur). A law enunciating a determination of comparatively great conformity with the ground is strong (ein starkes), of small, weak (schwaches Gesetz). Hence the weakest law, or that of least strength, is the one that enunciates a determination of the least conformity with the ground, §. 161, 176. The greater the conformity with the ground of the determination enunciated by the law, the stronger the law is, §. 160, until it is the strongest, enunciating a determination in which there is the greatest conformity, §. 161.",["'strength': robur; 'enunciate': enuntiare.","The German equivalents are split as printed: ein starkes ... schwaches Gesetz."]],"met:181":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a sufficient, more fruitful, weightier, more remote ground, whose consequence the nearer one to be compared is, or to which it is subordinated, and to one simply such, is stronger than the weaker law enunciating a determination conformable only to an insufficient, less fruitful, less weighty, nearer ground, which is subordinated to that with which it is compared, more remote, and sufficient *secundum quid*, §. 180, 177.",["The second list (propiori, quae subordinetur illi, cum qua comparatur, ulteriori) is rendered as printed, though 'more remote' seems out of place in it; the reading may be doubtful."]],"met:182":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a more remote sufficient ground is called higher (eine höhere), one enunciating a determination conformable to a nearer, lower (niedrigere Richtschnur). Therefore higher laws are stronger than lower ones, and the supreme law is the strongest, §. 181.",["'supreme': summa."]],"met:183":["The greatest order has the most common rules, §. 175, 86, hence it is the most composite, §. 88, and so a simple order, however great it may be, is nevertheless not the greatest, §. 175, 88.",[]],"met:184":["The least metaphysical truth is the least order of many in one, §. 175, 89, or the least conformity with the universal principles, §. 176, 92. Therefore the more things in a being, the greater things, are conjoined according to the more, the stronger rules, the greater is the truth in it, §. 175, 180, until it is the greatest, where the most and greatest things are found most agreeing with the strongest rules (of the universal principles, §. 182), §. 160, 161.",["'universal principles': principia catholica."]],"met:185":["The least perfection is the single least agreement of the fewest least things toward one least thing, §. 94, 161, hence the more, the greater things, the more, the greater the thing in which, the more often, the more they agree, the greater is the perfection, §. 160, until it is the greatest, the greatest agreement of the most and greatest things toward one, §. 161, 169. But since the supreme perfection is thus the most composite, §. 183, 96, a simple perfection, however great it may be, is nevertheless not the greatest, §. 96.",["'agreement': consensus; 'agree': consentire."]],"met:186":["The least exception is one made from the weakest rule of perfection on account of the strongest, §. 178, 97. Therefore the stronger the rule of perfection from which, the weaker the one on account of which an exception is made, the greater is the exception, §. 160, until it is the greatest, excepting from the strongest, hence the supreme rule of perfection, §. 182, on account of the weakest law, §. 161. If a sufficient and an insufficient ground of perfection collide, the exception from the sufficient is greater, from the insufficient lesser. If a more fruitful and a less fruitful collide, the exception from the more fruitful is greater, from the less fruitful lesser. If a weightier and a less weighty ground of perfection collide, the exception from the weightier is greater, from the less weighty lesser. If a more remote and a nearer one to be subordinated to it collide, the exception from the more remote is greater, from the nearer lesser. If a ground simply sufficient and one such *secundum quid* collide, the exception from the one simply such is greater, from the one such *secundum quid*, lesser, §. 181. If a higher and a lower rule of perfection collide, the exception from the higher is greater, from the lower lesser, §. 182. Hence the exception contrary to the supreme law of perfection, i.e. which is made from the supreme rule of perfection on account of the least, i.e. one conformable to the least, is the greatest, §. 187, 182.",["The first sentence reads as printed, 'from the weakest rule ... on account of the strongest', though the sequel suggests the converse; the reading may be doubtful.","'collide': collidi."]],"met:187":["The least good is that by which, when it is posited, the least perfection is posited, §. 100, 161. The greater the perfection to be posited when the good is posited, the greater is the good, §. 160, until it is the best (das Beste), by which, when it is posited, the supreme perfection is posited, §. 161.",[]],"met:188":["The least contingent is that whose opposite is least possible, §. 104, 161. Therefore the greater the possibility of one of the opposites, the greater the contingency of the other, §. 160. The greatest contingency is that of the thing whose opposite has the greatest possibility, §. 161.",[]],"met:189":["The greater the separability of the determinations of what is hypothetically one, the greater is the contingency of this unity, §. 115, 188. The more both non-conjunction and confusion are possible, the more contingent is the opposite order, §. 117, 188. The more the non-conjunction or confusion of certain various things of a being is possible, the more contingent is the truth opposed to it, §. 119, 188. The more imperfection is possible, the more contingent is the opposite perfection, §. 122, 188.",["'what is hypothetically one': hypothetice unius; the genitive could also attach to 'determinations' as 'of one thing hypothetically'."]],"met:190":["The least change is the succession of a single least thing in a single least thing, §. 161, 125. Therefore the more things, the greater things, in the more, the greater things they succeed, the greater is the change, §. 160, until it is the greatest, the succession of the most and greatest things in more and greatest things. The least possibility of the least change in a being is its least mutability, §. 161, 127. Therefore the more possible, the greater the change in a being, the greater is its mutability, until it is the greatest, the greatest possibility of the greatest change, §. 161. The least reality in a being is the utmost fewness and smallness in it of truly positive determinations, §. 135, 161. The more of these, the greater ones it has, the more real it is. Therefore the most real is that in which there are the greatest, most realities, §. 161, 36. These, absolutely necessary, are the supreme good, or metaphysical best (das nothwendige höchste Gut), §. 137, 147, contingent in themselves, the contingent supreme good (das zufällige höchste Gut) (physical in the broad sense).",["'mutability': mutabilitas; 'reality': realitas.","'in more and greatest things': in pluribus maximis, as printed, where plurimis might be expected."]],"met:191":["A being either cannot exist except as a determination of another (in another), or it can, §. 10. The former is an accident (ein nur in andern) (predicamental or physical, cf. §. 50, whose being is being-in, συμβεβηκοσ), the latter is a substance (ein vor sich bestehendes Ding) (a being subsisting by itself, form, ἐντελεχεια οὐσια ὑποστασισ ἐνεργεια), which can exist, even if it is not in another, even if it is not a determination of another.",["'whose being is being-in': cuius esse est inesse.","The Greek is given as printed, without accents on some words and with final σ for ς."]],"met:192":["The existence of an accident, as such, is inherence (das nur in andern), the existence of a substance, as such, is subsistence (das vor sich bestehn).",[]],"met:193":["Accidents, if they seem to subsist by themselves, are substantiated phenomena (das vor sich zu bestehn scheinende).",["'substantiated phenomena': phaenomena substantiata."]],"met:194":["Accidents cannot exist except in others. Now nothing is diverse from accidents except substances, §. 191. Therefore accidents cannot exist except in substances, or they do not exist outside their substances, §. 58.",["The text prints Accidentianon, run together, for Accidentia non."]],"met:195":["Essentials, attributes, modes, relations, accidents, §. 191, 52, cannot exist except in substances, §. 194.",["The list may be read either as five items or with 'accidents' in apposition to the four before it."]],"met:196":["That in a substance in which accidents can inhere, or the substance, insofar as it is the subject (cf. §. 344), that in which accidents can inhere, is called the substantial, nor do accidents exist outside the substantial, §. 194.",["'the substantial': substantiale."]],"met:197":["If accidents inhere in a substance, there is something that is the ground of the inherence, §. 20, or force in the broader sense (eine Kraft in weiterer) (efficacy, energy, activity, cf. §. 216), and a sufficient one, §. 22. This is force (in the stricter sense (eine Kraft in engerer Bedeutung), and for the sake of brevity sometimes simply).",["'force': vis.","'(eine Kraft in weiterer)': the German breaks off as printed."]],"met:198":["Force in the stricter sense is either a substance, or an accident, §. 191. Now it is not an accident, since it is the sufficient ground of them all, §. 197. Therefore it is a substance, and insofar as accidents can inhere in it, as in a subject, the substantial, §. 196.",[]],"met:199":["Every substance is endowed with a substantial, §. 191, 196, hence with force, §. 198, is a substantial, §. 196, 191, hence force both in the broader, and in the stricter sense, §. 198, 197.",["The sentence reads as printed (est substantiali ... praedita ... est substantiale); the text may be corrupt."]],"met:200":["Substances, if they seem to be accidents, let them be predicated substances; singular ones are supposita (einzle vor sich bestehende Dinge).",["'let them be': sint, as printed; the sentence may be corrupt.","*supposita*: kept in Latin."]],"met:201":["To substantiated phenomena force is attributed, §. 199, 193, and they are also forces in the broader sense, or endowed with forces in the broader sense, §. 197, 23. But if force in the stricter sense is attributed to accidents, they are substantiated phenomena, §. 198, 193.",[]],"met:202":["Every substance has essentials and attributes absolutely necessarily, §. 107, hence every substance has accidents, §. 191, 195. But modes it either has, or does not have, §. 10. One that has modes is a contingent being, one that does not have them, a necessary being, §. 111. Therefore a substance is either necessary or contingent. The subsistence of a contingent substance is a mode, §. 134, 192.",[]],"met:203":["The least force is the ground of only a single least inhering accident, §. 197, 166. Therefore the more, the greater inhering accidents it is the ground of, the greater it is, until it is the greatest, of the most and greatest inhering accidents, §. 197, 166.",[]],"met:204":["The science of forces is dynamics, philosophical as well as mathematical, dynameometry.",["'dynamics': dynamica; 'dynameometry': dynameometria."]],"met:205":["A contingent suppositum is determined as to modes and relations, §. 184, 200. Hence there coexist in it fixed things, or things intrinsically immutable, §. 107, 132, with mutable ones, §. 133. Coexistence of this kind is a state (Zustand). Therefore a contingent suppositum has a state. The state of things united is union (Einigkeit).",["'union': unio, here distinct from unitio (§. 175)."]],"met:206":["The coexistence of modes with fixed things is the internal state (der innre Zustand), hence a contingent suppositum has an internal state, §. 205.",[]],"met:207":["The relations of a substance are intrinsically mutable in it, §. 133. Therefore coexisting with fixed things they give a state, §. 205, which is called external (der äussre Zustand).",[]],"met:208":["When a mode is changed the internal state is changed, §. 125, 200, when a relation is changed, the external state is changed, §. 125, 207. Now modes and relations are mutable, §. 133. 207. Therefore in a contingent substance changes of state are possible. §. 206, 207.",[]],"met:209":["The change of a mode is modification (eine innre). Therefore modification is the change of the internal state, §. 208, and a contingent substance is modifiable, §. 128. The change of a relation is variation (eine äussre Veränderung).",["'(eine innre)': the German breaks off as printed."]],"met:210":["Changes of state are accidents, §. 191, hence they cannot exist except in substances, §. 194, and indeed when a force, even one more strictly so called, has been posited, §. 197, 22. That force, the sufficient ground of the change, or in general of the inhering accident, §. 197, is either the substantial, which is changed, or in general in which the accident inheres, or a force diverse from it, §. 10, 38. If the former is the case, the substance whose state is changed, or in general in which the accident inheres, acts (thun, handeln); if the latter is the case, the substance whose state is changed, or in general in which the accident inheres, suffers. Hence action (Handlung) (act, operation) is the change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance, through its own force: passion (Leiden) the change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance, through an alien force.",["'suffers': patitur; 'passion': passio.","'through its own force': per vim ipsius."]],"met:211":["A substance acting on a substance outside itself flows into it, so that influx (Einfluss) (transient action) is the action of a substance on a substance outside itself. An action which is not influent is immanent.",["'flows into': influit."]],"met:212":["If the passion of that substance into which another flows is at the same time an action of the patient itself, the passion and the influx are called ideal. But if the passion is not an action of the patient, the passion and the influx are called real.",["'the patient': patiens, that which suffers."]],"met:213":["The action of the patient on the agent is reaction (Gegenwirkung), and the mutual action and reaction of substances conflict (Streit).",[]],"met:214":["Action and passion and reaction are smallest through which no more than a single smallest accident is actuated, §. 161, 210. Therefore the more, the greater the accidents that are actuated, the greater the action, passion, reaction, §. 160, 213, until they are greatest, where the most and greatest accidents are actuated, §. 161, 210.",["'smallest', 'greatest': minimae, maximae."]],"met:215":["That action and passion is called simple (ein einfaches) which is not a whole of others; that which is, composite (ein zusammengesetztes Thun und Leiden); and the more partial ones it consists of, the more composite it is, §. 160. Hence the greatest action and passion is the most composite, §. 214, 161.",[]],"met:216":["Every existing substance acts, §. 210, 199, hence has the possibility of acting, or faculty (Vermögen) (active potency, force, cf. §. 197), §. 57; if it suffers, it has the possibility of suffering, i.e. (passive potency, capacity) receptivity (Fähigkeit, Empfänglichkeit). §. 57.",["'potency': potentia; 'capacity': capacitas."]],"met:217":["The faculty and receptivity of real influxes are real, of ideal ones ideal, of simple actions simple (einfache), of composite ones composite (zusammengesetzte Vermögen und Fähigkeiten).",[]],"met:218":["Faculties and receptivities are either absolute, §. 216, 15, or hypothetical, §. 16. These indeed are always greater than those, §. 105, 216.",["'These ... those': hae ... illis, i.e. the latter (hypothetical) ... the former (absolute)."]],"met:219":["The smallest hypothetical faculty would be that by which only a single action would be possible in the smallest nexus of the substance, §. 216, 161. The more, the greater the actions, the greater the nexus of the substance in which they are possible, the greater is the hypothetical faculty, §. 168, 160. A greater hypothetical faculty is a habit (Fertigkeit) (promptitude, dexterity).",["In the second sentence substantiae can be genitive ('of the substance') or dative ('possible to the substance')."]],"met:220":["When faculty and receptivity are posited, since action or passion is not posited, §. 216, 259, but is nevertheless posited when a force more strictly so called is posited, §. 210, 30, this will be the complement of the faculty to the act, i.e. what is added to the faculty so that action exists. Hence a given certain force more strictly so called is either sufficient for a given certain action, or less, §. 21, 210; the former is called living (eine lebendige), the latter dead (eine todte Kraft) solicitation.",["'or less': vel minus, i.e. or not.","'solicitation': sollicitatio."]],"met:221":["An impediment (Hinderniss) (obstacle) is the opposite of the inherence of an accident, hence also what is opposed to changes is an impediment, §. 210.",[]],"met:222":["Resistance (Wiederstand) is the impediment of an action. And since the opposite of the inherence of accidents and of changes is an accident, §. 191, 81, impediments and resistances have a sufficient ground in a force, §. 197, 27. When an impediment is posited, an impeding force is posited: when a resistance is posited, a resisting force is posited, §. 22.",["'Wiederstand': so spelled in the text."]],"met:223":["A substance flowing more closely into a substance is present (gegenwärtig, zugegen) to it, and substances most closely present to each other touch each other, are contiguous (sich einander berühren), so that presence (Gegenwart) is closer influx, and immediate mutual presence, or immediate conflict, contact (Berührung). Insofar as something does not flow into another, nor suffers from it, more closely, it is called absent (abwesend) from it.",["'more closely', 'most closely', 'closer': propius, proxime, propior.","In the last sentence propius stands at the end, after both verbs, as here."]],"met:224":["A composite being (strictly and simply so called) (ein zusammengesetztes Ding in enger Bedeutung) is a whole of parts outside parts; a being not composite is simple (simply and rigorously so called) (einfach in genauer Bedeutung). A composite being more broadly so called (zusammengesetzt in weiterer Bedeutung) is whatever has parts, and simple comparatively (einfach in Vergleichung) the less composite.",[]],"met:225":["The parts of composites are either, singly and taken together, accidents, or some parts of composites are substances, §. 10, 191. If the former is the case, the composite being is an accident, §. 224, 155. The latter is a composite being more strictly so called (real) (ein zusammengesetztes Ding in engerer Bedeutung).",[]],"met:226":["Suppose that things posited outside one another can in no way constitute a whole, or be composed (zusammengesetzt werden): the composite will be intrinsically impossible, §. 224, 15. The mode of composition is therefore the opposite of internal impossibility in composites, §. 81, i.e. the internal possibility and essence of composites, §. 40.",[]],"met:227":["Origination (das Entstehn) is the change from non-existent to existent. The change from existent to non-existent is perishing (der Untergang). Hence the origination and perishing of a necessary being and of a necessary substance is absolutely impossible, §. 132, 202.",["'Origination': ortus; 'perishing': interitus."]],"met:228":["Origination out of nothing (das aus nichts entstehn) is the origination of that of which no part pre-exists it, and annihilation (Vernichtung) the perishing of that of which no part exists surviving. The origination out of nothing and annihilation of a necessary being and substance is absolutely impossible, §. 227.",["'exists surviving': exsistit superstes."]],"met:229":["Origination and origination out of nothing, perishing and annihilation cannot exist except in substances, §. 227, 228, because they are accidents, §. 210, but not in the necessary one, §. 227, 228. Therefore in contingent ones, §. 202.",[]],"met:230":["A substance is either simple, or composite, §. 224. The former is called a monad (atom, perfect unity).",[]],"met:231":["Every part of a composite substance is either the substantial or an accident. Accidents are not outside the substantial, §. 196. Therefore the substantials of a composite substance are posited outside one another, §. 124.",["'outside one another': extra se."]],"met:232":["A composite substance has forces, hence substances posited outside one another as parts, §. 231, 198, hence is a composite being more strictly so called, §. 225.",["'outside one another': extra se, as in §. 231; it can also be read 'outside itself'."]],"met:233":["A composite substance cannot exist except as a complex of other substances posited outside one another, §. 232, 155, and composed in a certain way, §. 226. Therefore it cannot exist except as a determination of others, §. 36, 38. Therefore it is an accident, §. 191, and, if it seems to subsist by itself, and force is attributed to it, it is a substantiated phenomenon, §. 193, 201.",["'substantiated phenomenon': phaenomenon substantiatum."]],"met:234":["Every substance is a monad, §. 233, 230; a composite being more strictly so called is not a monad, §. 225. Therefore a substantiated phenomenon, §. 193, 201.",[]],"met:235":["A composite more strictly so called consists of monads, §. 225, 234.",[]],"met:236":["A monad cannot originate except out of nothing. For its parts are the substantial and the accidents inhering in it, §. 196. The substantial does not pre-exist the substance; for when it exists, force exists, hence substance, §. 198. Of accidents none pre-exists its substance, §. 194. Therefore of a monad that is to originate no part pre-exists, hence it cannot originate except out of nothing, §. 228.",["'a monad that is to originate': oriundae monados."]],"met:237":["While the substantial survives, the substance has not yet perished, §. 198, but no accident survives its substance, §. 194. Therefore a monad cannot perish except through annihilation, §. 228, 196.",[]],"met:238":["Conjoined things posited beside one another are simultaneous (neben einander seiende), those posited after one another successive (aufeinanderfolgende). A whole of simultaneous things is a simultaneous being, of successive things a successive being.",[]],"met:239":["The order of simultaneous things posited outside one another is space (Raum), of successive things time (Zeit).",[]],"met:240":["When simultaneous things outside one another are posited, space is posited. When space is posited, simultaneous things outside one another are posited. When successive things are posited, time is posited, and when time is posited, diverse things are posited, §. 74, succeeding one another, §. 239, 78.",[]],"met:241":["That in which space is, is extended (ausgedehnt), and is said to fill space or place (to be in space, or place, namely repletively). Now in every composite strictly so called there is space, §. 240, 224. Therefore every composite strictly so called is extended and fills space. Everything extended has parts outside parts, simultaneous ones, §. 240, hence is composite, §. 224.",["'repletively': repletive.","'simultaneous ones': simultaneas, which may agree with either partes."]],"met:242":["A monad is not extended, nor does it fill space, §. 241, 230. But a whole of monads, §. 235, is extended, §. 241.",[]],"met:243":["Every composite has magnitude, §. 159, 224. The magnitude of a composite strictly and more strictly so called is quantitative (ausgedehnte Grösse). Hence a monad does not have quantitative magnitude, §. 230.",["The German gloss means 'extended magnitude'."]],"met:244":["Physical division (Zerlegung, Theilung) is the diminution of quantitative magnitude. Just as of an individual logical division is impossible, §. 148, so that is indivisible (untheilbar) of which physical division is impossible. Therefore indivisibility is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 17. Nothing absolutely indivisible is hypothetically divisible. Something absolutely divisible is hypothetically indivisible, §. 18. Hence a monad is indivisible, §. 243, and indeed in itself, §. 15.",["'in itself': per se."]],"met:245":["Composites cannot exist except as determinations of others, §. 225, 233. Now there are no others, besides composites, than simples, §. 224, 38. Therefore when a composite exists, monads exist, §. 230, 233.",[]],"met:246":["The quantity of a quality is degree (eine Stufe, Staffel) (quantity of virtue). Hence we cannot understand a degree except by assuming another, §. 69.",["'virtue': virtus, strength or power.","'understand': intelligere."]],"met:247":["The lowest or smallest degree is that than which a smaller is impossible; that than which a greater is impossible is the greatest, §. 246, 161. Greater degrees are wholes of several smallest ones, §. 155, 160. Hence from an affirmed greater degree to affirming a smaller, from a negated smaller degree to negating a greater degree the inference holds, §. 157. In any greater degree there is a multitude of degrees, which is called intension (das höhere), §. 159. If this is increased, the quality whose degree it is is intensified (zunehmen, stärker, angestrengt), if it is diminished, the quality whose degree it is is remitted (schwächer werden, nachlassen, abnehmen).",["'the inference holds': valet consequentia.","'is intensified', 'is remitted': intenditur, remittitur."]],"met:248":["To be a real being is a quality, §. 69, belonging to every being, §. 136. And since in every being there is a certain number of realities, §. 136, 159, every being has a certain degree of reality, §. 246, 159. Hence it will be either the greatest, or not the greatest, §. 10, 247. And since the degree of reality than which a greater is possible, or not the greatest, §. 247, is called a limit (der Schranken) (term, cf. §. 350, end, cf. §. 341), that having a limit will be finite (endlich, eingeschränkt) (cf. §. 341, limited), that not having a limit infinite (uneingeschränkt) (real, unlimited). The being, therefore, having the greatest degree of reality, or the most real, §. 190, is infinite, all the rest finite. The finite whose limits we either cannot, or it does not please us to, determine is the indefinite (mathematisch unendlich, unendlichscheinend) (imaginary infinite, mathematically such).",["'limit': limes; 'term': terminus; 'end': finis.","'real': reale, as the text prints it."]],"met:249":["Finite things have a limit, hence a degree, §. 248, therefore a quantity, §. 246, hence of all finite things philosophical and mathematical cognition is possible, §. 93, 22. the limit of a given finite thing, short of which more beyond in the same thing is impossible through its essence, is essential (der wesentliche Schranken). There is a mathematics of non-extended intensive things (Wissenschaft unausgedehnter Grössen), §. 247.",["'short of which more beyond': citra quem plus ultra.","'mathematics': mathesis.","The sentence beginning 'the limit' opens in lower case in the text."]],"met:250":["In every finite thing some reality is to be removed, §. 248, 247. Therefore some negation is to be posited, §. 135, 81, and so imperfection, §. 142, as much essential as accidental, §. 143, hence absolutely necessary, §. 107, negation, §. 36, strictly so called, §. 137, metaphysical evil, §. 146.",[]],"met:251":["The greatest degree of reality of the infinite is absolutely necessary, §. 248, 102, hence absolutely immutable, §. 130.",[]],"met:252":["If the infinite being were internally mutable, the determinations which succeeded others would change the degree of reality, §. 246, 125. But this is internally immutable, §. 251. Therefore the infinite being is internally immutable.",[]],"met:253":["The internally immutable is, as regards internal determinations, actually whatever it can be, §. 125. Therefore the infinite being, as regards internal determinations, is actually whatever it can be, §. 252.",["'as regards': qua; 'actually': actu, in act."]],"met:254":["What is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, is a finite being, §. 253, 248.",[]],"met:255":["The internally mutable is a finite being, §. 252, 126. Therefore every contingent being is finite, §. 133, although it can be in many respects indefinite and mathematically infinite, §. 248.",["'in many respects': multis rationibus; ratio is elsewhere rendered 'ground'."]],"met:256":["The infinite being is a necessary being, §. 255, 109.",[]],"met:257":["That which has a degree of reality than which a greater is possible, a certain quality of it, §. 248, hence an internal determination, can be intensified, §. 247, 69, hence changed, §. 247, 162. Therefore the finite being is internally mutable, §. 248, 126, hence is not a necessary being, §. 132, its existence is a mode, §. 134, and it itself a contingent being, §. 109.",["The opening is anacoluthic in the Latin: Quod habet ... eius qualitas quaedam."]],"met:258":["The necessary being is infinite, §. 257, 248.",[]],"met:259":["What is actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, is a necessary being, §. 132, therefore infinite, §. 258. Hence the infinite being can be defined through a being which actually is, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 253.",["'defined through': definiri per."]],"met:260":["A contingent being is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 133. A finite being is a contingent being, §. 257. Therefore a finite being can be defined through a being which is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 254. Which is also evident from this, that, of contradictory opposites, when one is defined affirmatively, the other can be defined by the same marks, but negative ones, §. 81, 248.",["'marks': notae."]],"met:261":["Infinitude is a reality, §. 36, whose ground is the greatest degree of reality, §. 248, 14; finitude or limitation is a negation, §. 36, whose ground is a limit, §. 248, 14.",[]],"met:262":["The modes and relations of a being are either realities, or negations, §. 36, hence when these are changed in a finite being its limit is changed, §. 248. Now every change of a finite being is a change of a mode or of a relation, §. 52, 132. Therefore every change of a finite being is a change of its limits, hence also of its limitation, §. 261, 30.",[]],"met:263":["A finite being has modes, §. 257, 112. These are either realities, or negations, §. 36. The opposite of both is absolutely possible in a finite being, §. 108. Hence privations are absolutely possible in every finite being, §. 137. Hence also imperfection and contingent evil, §. 144, 146 (physical, broadly so called).",[]],"met:264":["Every finite being is partly evil, partly good, §. 147, 137, and for every finite thing contingent good and evil is intrinsically possible, §. 147, 263.",[]],"met:265":["The least similarity is in two things in which a single least quality is common, §. 174, 70. But now in all beings certain qualities are common, §. 8-100. Therefore all beings are similar to one another in some degree, §. 246. There is hence in beings a certain similarity, and thus a universal identity, §. 70. The more, the greater the qualities, the more things they are common to, the greater is the similarity, §. 174, 70. The least equality is in two things in which a single least quantity is common; the more, therefore, in the more things, the greater the quantities that are common, the greater is the equality, §. 174, 70.",[]],"met:266":["The least congruence is the least similarity and equality, and the greater those are together, the greater is the congruence, §. 265, 70. The identity, similarity, equality, congruence of essentials are called essential (wesentliche), of essentials and attributes necessary (nothwendige), of modes contingent, of affections accidental (zufallige).",["\"quoque\" read as quo + que (\"and the greater\"), not the adverb \"also\"."]],"met:267":["Identity and diversity, insofar as it concerns each single predicate, is total (völlig, gänzlich), insofar as certain ones, partial (zum Theil statt findend). Therefore similarity, equality, congruence are either total or partial, §. 70.",["\"insofar as it concerns each single predicate\": Latin qua singula praedicata; singula = each and every one taken singly."]],"met:268":["Since there is a partial similarity of all beings, §. 265, beings are not totally diverse, §. 267. Let us call this proposition the principle of denied total dissimilarity and diversity.",[]],"met:269":["The total identity of singulars is numerical. Two singulars outside one another that are entirely or totally the same are impossible. For since two are posited, many are posited, hence partly the same, partly diverse, §. 74. Therefore they are not totally the same, §. 267. Singulars that are totally the same are the same in number, and not partly the same, partly diverse, §. 267. Hence they are not many, nor two, §. 74. This proposition is called the principle of (the identity of) indiscernibles taken broadly, or of denied total identity.",[]],"met:270":["Several actual things outside one another that are totally congruent are impossible. For while they existed outside one another, its own and proper existence would belong to each, §. 86, diverse from the existence of the other, §. 38, hence either an attribute or a mode, at least a single one, §. 77, would be in the one which would not be in the other, §. 38, 55, and thus either a quality or a quantity, §. 69, and hence both would not be totally congruent, §. 70, 267. Things totally congruent are the same as regards all internal distinctions, §. 267, 70, hence the existence of the one is not diverse from the existence of the other, §. 70, therefore they do not exist outside one another. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total congruence.",["\"distinctions\": Latin discrimina."]],"met:271":["Several actual things outside one another that are totally similar are impossible. For either they would also be totally equal, or less. If the former, they would be totally congruent, §. 70, 267, which is absurd, §. 270. If they were not totally equal, there would be in the one a quantity which would not be in the other, §. 70, 38. This would have a sufficient ground, §. 22. Hence there would be in one of the totally similar things a quality which would not be in the other, §. 69, 14, which is absurd, §. 70, 267. This proposition is the principle of (the identity of) indiscernibles taken strictly, or of denied total similarity.",["\"which is absurd\": the abbreviation q.a. (quod absurdum).","\"sufficient ground\": Latin ratio sufficiens.","\"or less\": Latin aut minus, i.e. or not."]],"met:272":["Several actual things outside one another that are totally equal are impossible. For either they will at the same time be totally similar, or only partially, §. 265. If the former, they would be totally congruent, §. 70, 267, which is absurd, §. 270. If they were only partially similar, there would be a quality in the one which would not be in the other, §. 267, 70, hence there would not be in both totally the same degree of reality, §. 248, and thus some quantity of the one would not be a quantity of the other, §. 246, therefore they would not be totally equal either, §. 267, 70. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total equality.",["\"which is absurd\": the abbreviation q.a. (quod absurdum)."]],"met:273":["All actual things outside one another are partially diverse, §. 268, discongruent, §. 270, 70, dissimilar, §. 271, unequal, §. 272.",[]],"met:274":["Things A and C that are the same as the same third B are the same as each other. For the things that are in B are in A, §. 38. The things that are in C are in B, §. 38. Therefore the things that are in C are in A. Hence A and C are the same, §. 38.",[]],"met:275":["Things congruent, similar, equal to the same third are congruent, similar, equal to each other, §. 70, 274.",[]],"met:276":["When the same, a congruent, similar, equal sufficient ground is posited, the same, a congruent, similar, equal consequence is posited, and vice versa, §. 38, 70.",["\"consequence\": Latin rationatum, the correlate of ratio (ground)."]],"met:277":["When the same essence is posited, the same attributes are posited, and vice versa, §. 276, 50.",[]],"met:278":["When a diverse, discongruent, dissimilar, unequal sufficient ground is posited, a diverse, dissimilar, discongruent, unequal consequence is posited, and vice versa, hence when a diverse essence is posited, diverse attributes are posited, and vice versa, §. 277, 276.",["\"consequence\": Latin rationatum."]],"met:279":["The identity, diversity, §. 38, congruence, discongruence, similarity, dissimilarity, equality, inequality, §. 70, of actual things outside one another are representable in none of them, unless it is regarded in nexus with those things that are posited outside it, §. 14, hence they are relations, §. 37. Those things, therefore, between which it intervenes are connected, §. 14, 19. But now it intervenes between individual actual things outside one another, §. 265-273. Therefore individual actual things are connected, §. 47. Hence in all actual things the nexus, indeed the harmony, is universal, §. 48, 167.",["\"it is regarded\", \"it intervenes\": the Latin verbs (spectetur, intercedit) are singular with no expressed subject, after a plural list; kept as in the Latin.","\"individual\": Latin singula, each taken singly."]],"met:280":["Since extension is the quality of a composite being by which it fills space, §. 69, 241, and space can be greater and smaller, §. 239, 175, in the extended a degree of extension is possible, §. 246, i.e. figure. Hence a monad does not admit, but a whole of monads does admit figure, §. 242. A composite of greater extension is spacious (räumlich), of smaller, narrow (eng).",[]],"met:281":["The position of a simultaneous thing actual outside others is its place (Ort), the position of a successive thing is its age (Alter).",["\"position\": Latin positus."]],"met:282":["The place of any simultaneous thing whatever is determined from its conjunction with diverse things actual outside it, §. 85, 281. Hence it is diverse from the place of coexisting things, §. 38. Therefore things simultaneous with one another, posited outside one another, are not in the same place, §. 281. The age of any successive thing whatever is determined from its conjunction with actual things posited before and after it, §. 85, 78, hence it is diverse from their age, §. 38. Hence things successive to one another cannot be in the same age.",[]],"met:283":["Change of place is motion (Bewegung), hence all motion is a change of relation, §. 281, 85, and it would be least if only a single position of a single least thing with respect to a single least thing actual outside it were changed, §. 161, hence it is the greater, the more positions, of the more things, of the greater things, are changed with respect to the more, the greater things outside the movables. Things that are not moved rest (ruhen), and the absence of motion is rest (Ruhe).",[]],"met:284":["Coordinate things touching each other are contiguous (zunächst an einander), those not contiguous are distant (von einander abstehend). The place of things distant from one another is situation (Lage). An action, or immediate and proximate influx, into a distant thing is impossible in and through itself, §. 223, 15.",["\"situation\": Latin situs, distinguished from positus (\"position\")."]],"met:285":["A simultaneous and successive being of contiguous parts is continuous (stetig, ununterbrochen, in einem fortgehend), but a simultaneous and successive being of parts distant from one another is interrupted (unterbrochen).",[]],"met:286":["The parts of an extended thing posited outside one another are either simple or composite, §. 224. The former, insofar as they are not extended, §. 242, are called points. A continuous series of points interposed between distant points is a line.",[]],"met:287":["The extension of a line is determined from the number of points of which it consists, §. 241, 286, and the one in which, between given distant points, the fewest are interposed, this line is the shortest (die kürzeste) between those. The shortest line between given points is straight (grade), one not straight is curved (krumm).",[]],"met:288":["The straight line between distant things is their distance (Entfernung), and the greater this is, the more the distant things are distant from each other, §. 287, 284. But if the distance should be greater, the distant things are more remote (entfernter), if smaller, nearer (näher).",["Latin distantia serves both as the noun \"distance\" and as the participle \"distant things\"; the English separates them."]],"met:289":["A continuous series of lines interposed between distant lines is a surface (Fläche). The extension of a surface is determined from the number of lines of which it consists, §. 241, 175, and that which is shortest between given distant lines is a plane (ebene) surface, one not plane is curved (gebogene) (gibbous). A continuous series of surfaces interposed between distant surfaces is the mathematically solid (das der dreifachen Ausmessung fähige) (body, cf. §. 296, mathematical).",["\"the mathematically solid\": Latin mathematice solidum."]],"met:290":["Extension possible in a line alone is length (Länge), that which is added to length in a surface is breadth (Breite), that which is added to the extension of surfaces in mathematically solid things is (height) or depth (Dicke, Höhe, Tiefe).",["\"is added to\": Latin accedit."]],"met:291":["If from a quantity assumed as one we understand another homogeneous, or similar, one, we measure (messen) this, the measured (das gemessene), from that, the measure (das Maass), and this action itself is called dimension (Ausmessung).",["\"dimension\": Latin dimensio, here the act of measuring."]],"met:292":["A line admits only a single dimension of extension, §. 291, 287, a surface a twofold, §. 291, 289, the mathematically solid a threefold, §. 280, 246.",[]],"met:293":["Since the parts of space are in simultaneous things posited outside one another, §. 239, 155, they are simultaneous, and space is thus a simultaneous being, §. 238.",[]],"met:294":["Since motion is an accident, §. 283, it cannot exist except in substances, and indeed when a force is posited, §. 210, which force is called motive force (bewegende Kraft). And since rest is an impediment of motion, §. 221, 283, when rest is posited a force resisting motion is posited, §. 222, which is called force of inertia (Kraft der Trägheit, widerstehende Kraft) (antitypy, sluggishness, implanted force).",["\"force\": Latin vis; \"antitypy, sluggishness, implanted force\": antitypia, ignavia, vis insita."]],"met:295":["An extended thing to which force of inertia is attributed is matter, cf. §. 344, and a substantiated phenomenon, §. 234, 201. Matter to which this force alone is attributed is first, cf. §. 423 (merely passive).",["\"substantiated phenomenon\": Latin phaenomenon substantiatum.","\"first\": Latin prima, i.e. materia prima."]],"met:296":["Matter to which motive force is attributed is a physical body (cf. §. 289, second matter, cf. §. 295) and a substantiated phenomenon, §. 295, 201.",["\"second matter\": Latin materia secunda."]],"met:297":["The time simultaneous with the thought about it is present (die gegenwärtige), that to which the present succeeds, past (vergangene), that successive to the present, future (zukünftige Zeit).",[]],"met:298":["Actual things of present time are existing things (das jetzt daseiende) (present in time, beings in act). Actual things of past time, if they are not at the same time beings in act, or if they no longer exist, are past things (das vergangene) (matters of fact). Actual things of future time are future things (das zukünftige), and these, if they are not at the same time beings in act, are beings in potency (das noch werden soll).",["\"beings in act\", \"beings in potency\": Latin entia actu, entia in potentia; \"matters of fact\": res facti."]],"met:299":["Continuation of existence is duration (die Dauer). That in which duration is possible is called perdurable (einer Dauer fähig). An actual thing, however, that is not perdurable is instantaneous (keiner Dauer fähig) (momentary). A perdurable thing of greater duration is lasting (dauerhaft) (stable, constant, perpetual, eternal), of smaller, brief (von kurzer Dauer) (fleeting, transitory, inconstant).",[]],"met:300":["The time simultaneous with a momentary thing is called an instant (moment) (ein Augenblick). What is successive to others is posterior (das spätere), that to which others are successive, prior (das frühere), that prior to all, first (das erste), that posterior to all, last (das letzte der Zeit nach).",[]],"met:301":["The change of a being into the present is beginning (Anfang), into the past, end (Ende). Unition is the beginning of union, §. 79, 205, the beginning of contact is impact (Stoss).",[]],"met:302":["Eternity (die Ewigkeit) (strictly so called, cf. §. 299) is duration without beginning and end. Duration only without end is aeviternity (das nur ohne Ende), and that simultaneous with all time, sempiternity (das zu aller Zeit sein).",[]],"met:303":["What continues existence without beginning is simultaneous with all past time and present, §. 301, 297, what will continue the same without end will be simultaneous with all future time, §. 301, 297. Therefore everything eternal is sempiternal, §. 302.",[]],"met:304":["A being in potency either has the proximate sufficient ground of existence in beings in act, or not, §. 27, 10. The former is a being in proximate potency (das sogleich), the latter a being in remote potency (das einmal werden soll).",["\"or not\": Latin vel minus."]],"met:305":["A being in act, while it endures, cannot not endure, §. 9, 7, hence it exists necessarily, §. 299, 101, or whatever is, while it is, is necessarily.",[]],"met:306":["Things that mutually determine place and age for each other are connected, §. 281, 85. Hence simultaneous things are connected as regards space, successive ones as regards time, §. 238, 239. But now individual actual things posited outside one another are either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 298. Therefore between individual actual things there intervenes a universal nexus and harmony, §. 48, 167.",[]],"met:307":["That which contains the ground of another is its principle (die Quelle). What depends on a principle is the principiate (das abgeleitete). The principle of existence is the cause (die Ursach), the principiate of a cause the caused (das verursachte). What cannot exist except as the caused of another posited outside itself is a being from another (abhängend) (dependent); what can also exist, although it is not the caused of another posited outside itself, is a being from itself (selbstständig) (independent).",["\"ground\": Latin ratio; \"principiate\": principiatum."]],"met:308":["The existence of a contingent, and hence also finite, being is a mode, §. 134, 257. Hence it is not sufficiently determined through the essence of the contingent and finite being, §. 65. Therefore not through its attributes either, §. 64, 25. Therefore in the internal determinations of a contingent and finite being there is not the sufficient ground of its existence, §. 52. But it is nevertheless necessary to the contingent and finite being for existing, §. 22, 101. Therefore it is necessary that the sufficient ground of its existence be outside the contingent and finite being, and since what contains it are causes, §. 307, the finite and contingent being cannot exist except as the caused of something posited outside itself, i.e. it is a being from another, §. 307.",[]],"met:309":["A being from itself is not contingent, nor finite, §. 308, 307, hence necessary, §. 109, and infinite, §. 258.",[]],"met:310":["A necessary and infinite being, §. 258, can exist although it is not the caused of another posited outside itself, §. 109, 102, therefore it is a being from itself and independent, §. 307.",[]],"met:311":["A principle of possibility is called a principle of being (Quelle der Möglichkeit) (of composition), a cause a principle of becoming (Quelle der Wirklichkeit) (of generation), a principle of cognition a principle of cognizing (Erkenntniss-Quelle), and this, if neither more nor fewer things can be cognized from it than the given principiate, is called adequate (hinlänglich-eigen) to that principiate. Essence is the principle of being and of cognizing of the modes, §. 65, 50.",["\"principle of being\", \"of becoming\", \"of cognizing\": Latin principium essendi, fiendi, cognoscendi."]],"met:312":["The principle of related things is called the term of the relation (der Grund), but the dependent the subject of the relation (der Gegenstand der Verhältniss). And things of which one can become the term of relation of the other are correlates (beziehen sich wechselweise auf einander).",[]],"met:313":["Between cause and caused there is a nexus, §. 307, 14, which is called causal (Zusammenhang der Ursachen), and, insofar as it is attributed to the cause, causality (das Verursachen), insofar as to the caused, dependence (verursacht sein, oder werden, die Abhänglichkeit).",[]],"met:314":["Several causes of one and the same caused are concauses (Mitursachen), and are said to concur (zusammenkommen) toward the caused. A cause that does not have a concause is solitary (die einzige Ursach). Of the concauses, that one which among the rest contains the greatest ground of the caused is principal (primary) (die Haupt-Ursach). The concauses of the principal cause are (less principal) secondary (Neben-Ursachen). All concauses are connected among themselves, §. 313, 33.",["'the caused' renders causatum throughout; 'ground' renders ratio."]],"met:315":["Of concauses one either is the cause of the other, or not, §. 10. In the former case the concauses are subordinate (unter einander), in the latter coordinate (bei und neben einander geordnete Ursachen). Of all the subordinate ones the first cause (die erste), the rest are second (Unter-Ursachen).",["The last sentence is elliptical in the Latin ('Subordinatarum omnium causa prima, reliquae secundae sunt'); left so."]],"met:316":["In subordinate concauses either the same thing in the nearer cause will depend on the further cause on which the caused of the nearer depends, or something else, §. 315, 27. If the former, the concauses are subordinated essentially (so sind die Mitursachen in einerlei), if the latter, accidentally (in verschiedenen Stücken untereinander geordnet).",[]],"met:317":["The cause of a cause essentially subordinated to it is also the cause of the caused, §. 316, 25.",[]],"met:318":["A cause either is sufficient or insufficient, §. 307, 21, and this one either in a certain respect, or simply such, §. 28. A cause either is mediate, on which a given caused depends through another, or immediate, on which a given caused depends, but not through another, §. 27.",["'this one' renders ista, which may refer to the insufficient cause or to either; 'in a certain respect' renders secundum quid."]],"met:319":["The cause of a reality through action is efficient (eine wirkende), but of a negation deficient (fehlende Ursach). A cause efficient as well as deficient act, hence they are substances, §. 210, and therefore accidents regarded as efficient causes are substantiated phenomena, §. 201, 200. The caused things of an efficient or deficient cause are effects (Wirkung).",["The plural verb after a singular subject ('Causa tam efficiens, quam deficiens agunt') is kept."]],"met:320":["An efficient and deficient cause either is mediate, which through another efficient or deficient cause, or immediate, which actualizes the effect, but not through another efficient or deficient cause. An efficient and deficient cause coordinated with another efficient and deficient one is called its associate (vergesellschaftete Ursachen), and if the other or one associate should be insufficient for a given effect, the other associate is called auxiliary (eine helfende Ursach).",["The first clause about the mediate cause lacks its verb in the Latin; left elliptical.","'actualizes' renders actuare."]],"met:321":["Aid (Hülfe) is the complement toward the effect of an insufficient associate cause. To be an auxiliary cause is to help (helfen). Therefore one helping is not a solitary cause, §. 320, and a solitary cause does not help, §. 314.",[]],"met:322":["An efficient or deficient cause, less principal, subordinated to an efficient or deficient one, is instrumental (ein Werkzeug) (administering, and ministerial).",["administra, ministerialis."]],"met:323":["A singular action with its effect is called an event (ein Vorfall, eine Begebenheit). A relation of an event is a circumstance (ein Umstand). The complex of relations concurring toward an event is the occasion (die Gelegenheit), and its cause is the occasional cause (eine gelegentliche Ursach). The least agreement of the fewest least circumstances toward a given event is the least occasion, §. 161. The more, the greater the circumstances, the more they agree toward it, the greater is the occasion, §. 160, until it is the greatest, the greatest agreement of the most, greatest circumstances toward a given event, §. 161. A greater occasion with respect to place is opportunity, and its opposite inopportunity, with respect to time timeliness, and its opposite untimeliness (bequemre und unbequemre Zeiten und Orte).",["'event' renders eventus; 'agreement' renders consensus.","opportunitas, inopportunitas, tempestivitas, intempestivitas."]],"met:324":["If the least circumstance should be in event A which is not in event B, the events A and B are not totally the same, §. 267, 323, hence partially diverse, §. 267, i.e. the least circumstance varies a thing externally.",[]],"met:325":["Let events A and B be internally the same in some degree, and let them be regarded in the most diverse circumstances, the diversity depending thereon is not, except in relations, §. 323, hence external, §. 37. Therefore the internal identity of events is by the diversity of circumstances, of place, of age, §. 281, 85, etc., neither increased, nor diminished, §. 162, i.e. Place and time do not vary a thing internally.",["'age' renders aetas; 'etc.' renders e.c."]],"met:326":["The principle being posited, the principiate is posited, §. 307, 30. The cause being posited, the caused is posited, §. 311. The efficient and deficient cause being posited, the effect is posited, §. 319, the occasional cause being posited, the occasion is posited, §. 323, and vice versa, §. 29.",["'principle' renders principium, 'principiate' principiatum."]],"met:327":["The same, a similar, an equal, a congruent principle being posited, hence causes, §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, occasional, §. 323, the same, similar, equal, congruent principiates are posited, §. 276, 307, caused things, effects, occasions, and vice versa.",[]],"met:328":["The principle being removed, hence the cause, §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, and occasional, §. 223, the principiate, the caused, the effect, the occasion is removed, §. 307, 31, and vice versa, §. 32.",["'§. 223' may be a misprint for § 323, where the occasional cause is defined; left as printed."]],"met:329":["Every effect is similar to the deficient or efficient cause, §. 265, i.e. as the cause, so the effect, §. 70. An effect either is similar to the efficient cause as regards the specific difference of both, or not, §. 10. The former is called a univocal effect (eine Wirkung von einerlei), the latter equivocal (von verschiedener Art).",["'as the cause, so the effect' renders 'qualis causa, talis effectus'."]],"met:330":["The effect having in a given action the proximate ground of existence is its immediate (unmittelbare) effect (proximate, and continuous, cf. §. 285). Mediate (mittelbare) (remote) are those which have only a further ground of their existence in the given action. All the effects of an action taken in every intension are the full (völlige) effect. A less full (Wirkung, die nicht völlig ist) effect is either only a certain one, or one regarded in a lesser degree.",["'full' renders plenus; 'intension' renders intensio."]],"met:331":["The full effect is as great as the action through which it is actualized, §. 330, 214, hence it is equal to the action, §. 70. Now the action is as great as the living force through which it is actualized, §. 220, 166, hence the action is equal to the living forces by which it is actualized, §. 70. Therefore the action of an efficient cause is equal to its living forces, §. 319. Hence the full effect is equal (proportionate) to the living forces of the efficient cause, §. 275.",["'living force' renders vis viva."]],"met:332":["The nobility or dignity of some effect to be essentially subordinated to some efficient cause pertains to the full effect of the latter, §. 330, 317, 166. Therefore an effect is not nobler than its efficient cause, to which they are essentially subordinated, §. 331, 160.",["The source reads 'subordiantur', evidently for subordinantur; its plural is kept as 'they are'."]],"met:333":["From the effect the qualities, §. 329, and the quantities, §. 331, hence the determinations of the cause can be cognized, §. 70, 67. Therefore the effect is the principle of cognizing the cause, §. 311, i.e. the effect testifies of the cause.",["'principle of cognizing' renders principium cognoscendi."]],"met:334":["Every contingent and finite being is a being from another, §. 308. Therefore existence does not inhere in the existent through a force proper to it, §. 307, hence a foreign force posited outside the finite and contingent real is the sufficient ground of the existence inhering in the finite and contingent real being, §. 210. Therefore a substance posited outside it acts upon it by influencing existence, §. 211. Hence every contingent and finite real being is an effect, §. 319, and has an efficient cause, §. 326.",["'a being from another' renders ens ab alio.","'by influencing existence' renders exsistentiam influendo."]],"met:335":["The causal nexus between the efficient, deficient cause and the effect is called effective (Zusammenhang der Ursachen und Wirkungen). Hence associate causes are connected by an effective nexus, §. 320, 314.",[]],"met:336":["The useful (nützlich, nutzbar) is good for another, the not good for another useless (unnütz), the bad for another harmful (schädlich). Hence usefulness (Nutzbarkeit) is a respective goodness, §. 37, which, if it is attributed to the thing which another can benefit, can be called passive (des, dem genutzt werden kann), if to that which can benefit, active (die thätige Nutzbarkeit).",["'harmful' renders noxium; 'benefit' renders prodesse."]],"met:337":["The least usefulness is that by which a single least thing posits in a single other least thing a single least perfection, §. 336, 161, hence it is increased the more, the greater things, in the more, the greater things, posit the more, the greater perfections §. 187. The degree of usefulness is called value (Werth), and the judgment about value price (Preis, Achtung, Schätzung, Würdigung) (estimation). Hence estimation with price either is true, or apparent or imaginary, §. 12.",["'estimation' renders aestimatio; 'or' before 'imaginary' renders s. (sive)."]],"met:338":["Use (der Gebrauch, Nutzen) is the actualization of usefulness. Abuse is either apparent use (Missbrauch), or use by which the useful perishes (Verbrauch). And since from the useful the perfection of that which it benefits can be cognized, §. 336, 100, the useful and that which it can benefit are connected, and their nexus can be called the nexus of usefulness (Zusammenhang der Nutzbarkeiten).",[]],"met:339":["If someone uses a useful thing, through it the perfection of another is actualized, §. 338, 100, and the useful through use becomes the cause of the perfection of that which it benefits, §. 307. And this causal nexus can be called the nexus of uses (Zusammenhang der Nutzen). That which someone uses is useful, §. 336, 57, the entirely useless no one uses, §. 338, 58, yet a certain useful thing lacks use, §. 59, hence something which no one uses is nonetheless useful, §. 60.",[]],"met:340":["Things useful to one and the same thing are connected with it, §. 339. Therefore all things useful toward one are connected among themselves, §. 314. The useful A, useful to the useful B essentially subordinated to it, is useful also to that C which B benefits, §. 317.",["The Latin ('Utile A utili B ... utile est') does not repeat 'useful' before B; the English repeats it to carry the dative."]],"met:341":["If someone uses or abuses something to actualize a good seen as such by him: the good itself seen as such by the agent is called the end (Zweck), cf. §. 248, the causes of the end which he can use or abuse toward the end, means (Mittel) (destined, finite, cf. §. 248, remedies), and the representation of the end intention (Absicht). Now the end is the principle of use or abuse, §. 338, 307, hence the final cause, §. 338, 307.",["'seen as such by him' renders sibi visum, which can also mean 'seeming good to him'.","'finite' renders finita, here possibly 'directed to an end'."]],"met:342":["The grounds of intention in the one intending are called impulsive causes (Trieb oder bewegende Ursachen). The end is the effect of the action and of the means which the agent uses or abuses, §. 341, 319. Hence the full end is equal to the action and the means. The means and the action are equal (proportionate) to the full end, §. 331. The seizing of the occasion, §. 323, and the removal of impediments, §. 221, are means, §. 341.",["'impulsive causes' renders causae impulsivae."]],"met:343":["The causal nexus of means and ends is final (Zusammenhang der Mittel und Zwecke), all co-ends are connected among themselves, §. 314, 341. The end of ends essentially subordinated to it is also the end of the means, §. 317, 341. The first end, or that to which all co-ends are subordinated, is called the ultimate or the goal (Endzweck), and this is either simply ultimate, or such in a certain respect in a given subordination of ends, §. 28, the rest intermediate (Mittel-Zwecke).",["'co-ends' renders cofines; 'goal' renders scopus."]],"met:344":["If a being is conceived as determinable, matter, cf. §. 295, 296, from which (der Stoff, der Zeug), in the very act of determination matter about which (object, subject of occupation) (der Gegenstand), the determination having been made matter in which, and this with the matter from which is called the subject.",["The Latin has a single verb, vocatur, at the end; the earlier members are elliptical and left so.","materia ex qua, circa quam, in qua."]],"met:345":["Since matter and form contain the ground of an actual determination, §. 344, 40, they are causes, §. 307. The former material, the latter formal. Material and formal concauses are connected among themselves, §. 314, and their nexus with their principiates, §. 307, and among themselves, §. 314, the former can be called subjective, the latter formal (essential).",[]],"met:346":["The exemplar (das Muster) is that to which something similar is intended, and since it is an impulsive cause, §. 342, it is a cause, §. 307, which is called exemplary, and its caused is called the exemplified (der Abdruck) (ectype, copy). An exemplar which does not have another is the archetype (das Urbild) (original). The exemplar and the exemplified and co-exemplars are connected by a causal nexus. §. 313, which nexus is exemplary (typical).",["The source reads 'intenditus', evidently for intenditur.","'the exemplified' renders exemplatum; the period before '§. 313' is as printed."]],"met:347":["The means of cognizing the existence of another is a sign (das Zeichen), the end of the sign the signified (das bezeichnete). Hence the sign is the principle of cognizing the signified, §. 311, and the nexus between sign and signified is significative (der Zusammenhang der Zeichen), and that attributed to the sign is called signification (die Bedeutung) (force, power).",["'signification' renders significatus; 'force, power' renders vis, potestas."]],"met:348":["The actual signified, §. 347, either is present, then the sign is called demonstrative (ein Anzeigungs-Zeichen); or past, then the sign is called mnemonic (ein Erinnerungs-Zeichen) (rememorative, μνημοσυνον); or future, §. 298, then the sign is called a prognostic.",["The Greek μνημοσυνον ('memorial') is kept as printed, unaccented."]],"met:349":["The science of signs is (semiotics, philosophical semiology, symbolics) characteristic (die allgemeine Zeichen-Kunst), and it is, I. heuristic (die erfindende), concerning the signs to be found, primitive (einfache) ones, which do not have signs for parts, as well as derivative ones (zusammen gesetzte Zeichen), which are composed of signs. If the mode of composition of the latter should be more similar to the essence of the signified, they are essential (wesentliche Zeichen). The heuristic of derivative signs is combinatorial (die verbindende), and II. hermeneutic (die deutende), concerning the signifieds of signs to be cognized. This hermeneutic is universal (die allgemeine Auslegungs-Kunst). The characteristic of prognostics is mantic (die Kunst der Vorbedeutungen).",["'Haec hermeneutica universalis est' can also be read 'This is universal hermeneutics'.","'to be found' renders inveniendis (finding or inventing)."]],"met:350":["A sign of a representation is a term (cf. §. 248, symbol). Terms more customary through the human voice are words (Wort). A series of words signifying connected representations is speech (in the wider sense) (Rede). The complex of words more customary in a certain larger region is a particular language (besondre Sprache). A language more customary in a certain smaller region, differing from itself as it is more customary in other smaller regions, is an idiom (Mund-Art). A more perfect idiom is a dialect. Terms, strokes to be observed by the eyes, are characters in the stricter sense (Züge), cf. §. 67. These are either of words, nominal (Wort-), or of the things to be signified, immediate to words, real (Sach-Charactere), which if they signify another thing than that to which they are most similar, are hieroglyphic, the insignia of societies (Siegel, Pettschaft, Wapen), if they can be read in every particular language, their complex is a universal language (die allgemeine Sprache).",["'rerum signandarum vocabulis immediati' is obscure: 'immediate to words' may mean standing for things without words between; left so.","'customary' renders usitatior (comparative)."]],"met:351":["General cosmology is the science of the general predicates of the world, and it is either from experience more nearly, empirical, or from the notion of the world, rational.",[]],"met:352":["Cosmology, containing the first principles of psychology, of the theologies, of physics, of teleology, of practical philosophy, is referred, §. 2, with reason, to metaphysics, §. 1.",["'with reason' renders cum ratione: 'rightly' or 'together with its ground'."]],"met:353":["Cosmology teaches of the world 1) the notion, 2) the parts, 3) the perfection.",[]],"met:354":["The world (die ganze Welt) (cf. §. 91, 403, 434, universe, παν) is a series (multitude, whole) of actual finite things, which is not a part of another.",["The Greek παν ('all') is kept as printed."]],"met:355":["This world exists. Therefore the world is possible in itself, §. 57, 18.",[]],"met:356":["In this world there are actual things posited outside one another, hence an actual universal nexus, §. 279, 306.",[]],"met:357":["In every world there are actual parts, §. 354, 155, these are singly connected with the whole, §. 14, 157, hence singly connected with each single one, §. 33. Therefore in every world there is a nexus of parts and universal harmony, §. 48, i.e. in the world there is no island. Otherwise: the parts of the world either are actual things posited outside one another, and are connected each with each, §. 279, 306, or are its internal determinations not posited outside one another, §. 10, 37, and are connected each with each, §. 49. It is not episodic, it is like an army.",["The Greek, as printed: 'Ουκ εστιν επεισοδιωδησ εστιν ωσπερ στρατευμα', unaccented and apparently garbled; translated as it stands.","'Otherwise' renders Aliter."]],"met:358":["In this world there exists an effective nexus (realm of power), §. 335, of utility, §. 338, of uses, §. 339, a final (realm of wisdom), §. 343, a subjective and formal, §. 345, an exemplary, §. 346, a significative, §. 347. Hence nexus of this kind are possible in the world, §. 57.",["'nexus': Latin nexus, kept as a term throughout.","'realm of power', 'realm of wisdom': regnum potentiae, regnum sapientiae."]],"met:359":["Since every world is a being, §. 355, 62, it will be one, §. 73 (§. 354, 155), true, §. 90 (§. 357, 355, 354, 92). Hence in every world there is order, §. 89, and common rules, §. 86. A fabulous world is not a world, §. 120.",["'fabulous': fabulosus, i.e. of fable."]],"met:360":["Every world is perfect, §. 99, 359, and good. §. 100.",[]],"met:361":["The parts of every world, each single one, are contingent beings, §. 354, 257. Hence their existence is a mode, §. 134. Now the existence of the single parts of the world taken together is the existence of the world, §. 155. Therefore the existence of the world is a mode. and every world is a contingent being, §. 111. Otherwise: Posit that some world is a necessary being, all its internal determinations will be absolutely necessary, nor hence will any part of that world have modes, §. 108, 157, nor will it be a contingent being, §. 134, but every part of that world will be a necessary being, §. 109, hence infinite, §. 258, which is absurd, §. 354.",["'which is absurd': the text has the abbreviation q.a. (quod absurdum).","'and every world': lower case after the full stop, as in the text."]],"met:362":["Every world is one, §. 359, yet it has modes, §. 361, 112, hence determinations separable in themselves, §. 72, 65, hence hypothetical unity, §. 76, and intrinsically contingent, §. 115.",["'intrinsically contingent': contingentem agrees with unitatem, the unity."]],"met:363":["The conjunction of the parts of no world is absolutely necessary, §. 362, 102, yet is a coordination, §. 78. Therefore in every world there is an order contingent in itself, §. 117, hence also a truth intrinsically contingent, §. 119.",["'yet is a coordination': est tamen coordinatio; can also be read 'yet there is coordination'."]],"met:364":["Since all the real parts of the world, §. 136, agree toward the degree of reality to be attributed to their world, §. 248, 140, yet so that in themselves they could also not agree, or not agree so much, inasmuch as contingent beings, §. 354, 257: in every world there is a perfection contingent in itself, §. 122.",["'inasmuch as contingent beings': utpote entia contingentia."]],"met:365":["Every world is absolutely and internally mutable, §. 361, 133.",[]],"met:366":["In every world there are absolutely necessary realities, §. 359, 360, hence metaphysical good, §. 147, and ones contingent in themselves, §. 362-364, hence physical good broadly so called, §. 147.",["'broadly so called': late dictum."]],"met:367":["This world is one, §. 359, contingently in itself, §. 362. There is in this world order, §. 359, and truth intrinsically contingent, §. 363, there is also perfection and goodness intrinsically contingent, §. 364, metaphysical and physical good broadly so called, §. 366, and this world is absolutely, is internally mutable, §. 365, 154.",["'intrinsically contingent' (first): contingentes is plural, agreeing with both order and truth.","'is absolutely, is internally mutable': so the text; cf. §. 365, 'absolutely and internally mutable'."]],"met:368":["The smallest world would be a whole of the fewest smallest actual things, §. 161, 354, hence the more, the greater finite things it has for parts, the greater it becomes, §. 160, until it be the greatest aggregate of the most numerous greatest among finite things, §. 161.",["'of the most numerous greatest among finite things': plurimorum maximorum in finitis."]],"met:369":["The state of the world (der Zustand einer ganzen Welt) is the whole of all the simultaneous states in its parts. Now this world has parts in which fixed things coexist with mutable ones, §. 367. Therefore this world has a state, §. 205.",["'state': status."]],"met:370":["The existence of every world and of this one is a mode, §. 361, 134, hence it can be absent and be present with the essence of the world preserved, §. 65. And since the actuality of every world and of this one is absolutely mutable, §. 133, the beginning and end of every world is absolutely possible, §. 301, 65.",["'with the essence of the world preserved': salva mundi essentia."]],"met:371":["A world cannot arise except from nothing. For let some world be posited to arise in such a way that yet some part of it had already preexisted it: since and as long as this part of it existed, it was in part existing, as it always will be, if it has existed, but not non-existing, hence, seen to arise after the preexisting part, it does not arise: §. 227, since such a world had not yet arisen, it was nevertheless no longer merely possible, while it was the whole of an existing part, and so already, as regards existence, determined, §. 54. Therefore a world not arising from nothing does not arise, §. 228. So too a world not annihilated does not perish, while, with a part surviving, it remains in part existing, as it always was, if it existed, hence it is not changed into the non-existing, §. 227. Therefore a world cannot perish except through annihilation, §. 228.",["'seen to arise': oriri visus; also 'seeming to arise'.","'as regards existence': qua exsistentiam."]],"met:372":["Every world and this one is a finite being, §. 255, 361, hence a philosophical and mathematical cognition of it is possible, §. 249. Every world and this one has metaphysical evil, §. 250, and contingent, or physical broadly so called, an imperfection contingent in itself absolutely possible, §. 263, partly good, partly evil, §. 264.",["'or': s. (seu) in the text.","'partly good, partly evil': bonus, malus are masculine, agreeing with mundus, the world."]],"met:373":["All worlds are both to the infinite being, and to one another, and to any of their parts partially similar, §. 265, 267, but impossible are several actual outside one another totally the same, §. 269, diverse, §. 268, congruent, §. 270, similar, §. 271, equal, §. 272.",["'totally' (totaliter) may govern all five adjectives that follow it."]],"met:374":["Since the parts of the world are either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 354, if they are posited outside one another, they will be connected in the world either through time, or through space, or through both, §. 239, 306.",[]],"met:375":["Every world is a being from another, §. 361, or dependent, §. 308, and this world has an efficient cause posited outside itself, and is its effect, §. 334, testifying of the cause, §. 333.",[]],"met:376":["The parts of every world are actual things, §. 354, hence each single one in its world, and so the parts of this world in this world, have truth, §. 90, and certainty, §. 93, as internal determinations, §. 37, 93, determined, §. 54.",["'as internal determinations, determined': ut determinationes internas determinatas, accusative, hence also 'as they have determined internal determinations'."]],"met:377":["Finite things which are possible not absolutely only, nor hypothetically merely in any nexus whatever, but also in the universal nexus of some world, are called possibles (das Mögliche einer gewissen ganzen Welt) of that world. Hence the possibles of this world are those which, regarded in its universal nexus, are yet hypothetically possible, hence have a greater degree of possibility, §. 165, 246.",["'possibles': possibilia, neuter plural."]],"met:378":["If even a single part of this world were other than it is, this world would not be totally the same as it is, §. 155, 267. Now all the parts of this universe can be other than they are, § 354, 260. Therefore worlds are possible partly diverse from this one, §. 38, partly the same with it, §. 265, i.e. several, §. 74.",[]],"met:379":["This world is uniquely singular, §. 77. For posit that there are several, they would constitute with this one a multitude or series, §. 74. Hence this would not be a world, §. 354, or all those worlds parts of this one, the only unique one, §. 354, 77.",["'parts of this one, the only unique one': partes huius, tantum unici; the Latin has no verb in this clause."]],"met:380":["A progression (regression) to infinity (der Fort- oder Rückgang in das Unendliche) would be a series of contingent beings posited outside one another, of which one is the cause of another in a certain respect, without a cause simply such. A progression to infinity in which the caused is taken as cause of itself would be curvilinear (dergleichen Fortgang im Kreise) (circular): one in which the caused is not taken as cause of itself would be rectilinear (der grade Fort- oder Rückgang).",["'in a certain respect': secundum quid; 'simply': simpliciter.","'progression': progressus."]],"met:381":["A progression to infinity, however great it were posited, would be a contingent being, §. 380, 155, hence it would have an efficient cause posited outside itself, §. 334. This could not be a contingent being, for while that would again be a being from another, §. 308, it would not be the cause of the progression to infinity except one such in a certain respect, §. 28. Therefore not posited outside the progression itself, but a part of it, §. 155, 380. Therefore the efficient cause of the progression to infinity would have to be a necessary being, §. 109, and independent, §. 310. In whatever way this can exist, in that way it exists, §. 259. But it can exist, although it is not the caused of another posited outside itself, §. 310. Therefore neither is it the caused of another posited outside itself, and so it is the cause of its effects simply such, §. 28. Hence a progression to infinity, which would have to be without a cause simply such, §. 380, and yet to have one, is impossible, §. 7, to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",["'for while': dum enim; dum may be read as causal ('since').","'the caused': causatum."]],"met:382":["Fate (das Schicksal, Verhängniss) is the necessity of events in the world. Fate from the absolute necessity of the world would be Spinozistic, a non-being, §. 361, 105, to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",["'a non-being': non ens; also 'not a being'."]],"met:383":["An event in the world whose sufficient ground is unknown is chance (ein Zufall, ein ungefähr). Chance of which there is not even a sufficient ground would be pure (ein blinder Zufall), impossible, §. 22, to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",["'chance': casus, which also means 'case'.","'sufficient ground': ratio sufficiens."]],"met:384":["An event is ordinary (eine ordentliche) whose existence is determined according to the rules of some certain order: extraordinary (ausserordentliche) is one not ordinary. Absolutely extraordinary (ganz und völlig ausserordentliche) would be one whose existence would be determined according to no rules of any order; relatively (gewisser maassen ausserordentliche) such is one that does not happen through the rules of a given certain order. The extraordinary on account of confusion is disordered (unordentliche Begebenheit).",["The German equivalents are feminine, agreeing with Begebenheit (event), which is printed only in the last."]],"met:385":["The absolutely extraordinary would not be true, §. 384, 89, hence impossible, §. 90, 62, to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"met:386":["An event without any proximate sufficient ground would be an absolute leap (das völlig durch einen Sprung geschähe). An event without an ordinary proximate sufficient ground is a respective leap (wobei gewisser maassen ein Sprung statt hat)",["'leap': saltus.","The text has no closing full stop."]],"met:387":["What would exist without any proximate sufficient ground, §. 27, would exist through pure chance, §. 22, 383, hence an absolute leap is impossible, §. 386, 284, to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58. Every respective one too, if it is not something of the disordered, is yet something of the extraordinary, §. 384.",[]],"met:388":["The world is neither an infinite substance, §. 372, 248, nor an internal determination of it, §. 365, 252, hence neither the essence of the infinite being, §. 40, nor an attribute, nor a mode, §. 50, nor a modification, §. 209. Hence every world is to be posited outside the infinite substance, and so this world too exists outside the infinite being, which is therefore called the extramundane being (das Wesen ausser der Welt), a being actual outside this world.",[]],"met:389":["Every world, hence also this one, is either a substance or an accident, §. 191. If this world is a substance, it subsists outside the infinite substance, §. 388, so that the infinite substance is not the unique substance, §. 77.",["'unique': unica."]],"met:390":["If this world is an accident, it is nevertheless not an accident of the infinite substance, otherwise it would not exist outside its substantial, §. 196, 388, nor can it exist except in a substance, or substances, §. 194. Therefore if this world is an accident, the infinite substance is not then either the unique substance, §. 77, 389. Now one or the other case is necessary, §. 191, 10. Therefore the infinite substance is not the unique substance.",["'its substantial': suum substantiale, the adjective used as a noun."]],"met:391":["Infinite force is not the unique force, §. 390, 198, in every world and this one there are finite forces, §. 390, 388.",["'force': vis."]],"met:392":["Every world is either a simple being or a composite, §. 224, this one is a composite being. Who thinks this world to be himself, a simple being, is an egoist.",["'thinks this world to be himself, a simple being': hunc mundum se putat ens simplex; also 'thinks himself, a simple being, to be this world'."]],"met:393":["The parts of a composite world, singly and taken together, cannot be accidents. For since such a world could not exist except in substances, §. 155, 191, and yet could neither inhere in the infinite, §. 388, nor in a unique finite one, §. 224, 194, it would require several finite substances in which it would inhere, §. 77. But these would constitute a series of actual finite things, which, if it were not a part of the world composed of mere accidents, would yet with it constitute a series of actual finite things greater than it, §. 160, hence it itself would not be a world, §. 354. If that series, which the finite substances would constitute, were a part of that world, it would not be composed of mere accidents, §. 155. Hence a world composed of mere accidents is impossible, §. 61, 62. Every composite world is a composite more strictly so called, §. 225. Every world is either a substance, §. 389, 392, or a composite more strictly so called.",["'more strictly so called': strictius dictum."]],"met:394":["The parts of a composite world are either substantial or accidental, §. 393, and the former indeed monads, §. 235. Hence every composite world, and this one, consists of monads, §. 392. Whence it is again evident that no world can arise except from nothing, §. 236, nor perish except through annihilation, §. 237, 393.",[]],"met:395":["This world, a composite more strictly so called, §. 393, consists of monads, nor is there anything substantial in it besides monads, §. 394. Who denies the existence of monads is a universal materialist. Who denies the existence of monads, the parts of a universe, e.g. of this one, is a cosmological materialist (cf. §. 757).",["'of monads, the parts of a universe, e.g. of this one': monadum universi, e.g. huius, partium, with partium in apposition to monadum."]],"met:396":["The monads of every, hence also of this, composite world are possible, §. 8, rational, §. 24, beings, §. 63, one, §. 73, true, §. 90, objectively certain, §. 93, perfect, §. 99, good, §. 100, contingent beings, §. 257, mutable, §. 133, real, §. 136, universally connected, §. 357, they are endowed with force, indeed are forces, even more strictly so called, §. 199, they have an internal state, §. 206, and an external, §. 207, modifiable, §. 209, not extended, nor filling space singly, but aggregated, §. 242, nor do they have quantitative magnitude, §. 243, indivisible, §. 244, finite, §. 354, hence they have a certain limit of their forces, §. 249, metaphysical evil, §. 250, partially similar to one another, §. 265, 268, but partially also as much dissimilar as unequal, §. 273, singly not admitting figure, yet so that the whole of them admits figure, §. 280.",["'rational': rationalia, in the sense of §. 24, having a ground.","'a certain limit': certum limitem; certain also in the sense 'definite'.","'of their forces': virium, without a possessive in the Latin."]],"met:397":["The monads of every, hence also of this, composite world, since they are actual posited outside one another, §. 354, 224, will be either simultaneous beings, or successive, or both, §. 238, hence as singulars they have position, §. 148, either, in the simultaneous, place, or, in the successive, age, or both, §. 281, although singly they do not fill a place, §. 396.",["'as singulars': singulares; 'singly': singulae.","'position': positus; 'age': aetas."]],"met:398":["Of the monads of every, hence also of this, universe, finite, §. 354, any one, outside the rest, if it is posited to subsist, being about to subsist, §. 192, cannot be with any other in totally the same place, §. 282. A substance in whose place another posited outside it cannot be is impenetrable (undurchdringlich) (solid). Therefore every substance, hence also the monads, as much of this as of every composite world, are impenetrable, §. 230.",["'being about to subsist': substitura, future participle, i.e. 'which would subsist'.","'finite': finitarum agrees with monadum, the monads."]],"met:399":["The monads of this and of every composite, hence extended, world, §. 241, 393, are points, §. 286, but by no means mathematical ones, in which nothing besides non-extension is posited, §. 396-398, nor when posited beside one another are they congruent or do they coincide, §. 70, 396, but when several of them are posited coexisting, since each is impenetrable, §. 398, they are posited simultaneous outside one another in a certain order, §. 396, 78, hence in their aggregate there is space, §. 239. Therefore every aggregate of monads of this and of every composite world is extended, §. 241. A mathematical point, an abstract possible, §. 149, if it is feigned to exist, is a Zenonian point, a fictitious being, §. 62. If you call a physical point actual and, besides simplicity, determined in every way, certain monads of this universe are physical points, namely those from whose aggregate an extended thing.",["'namely those from whose aggregate an extended thing': quarum ex aggregato extensum; the Latin has no verb.","'feigned': fingatur."]],"met:400":["All the monads of every composite world, hence also of this one, are in a universal nexus, §. 357, hence single ones of single ones are either grounds or consequences, or both, §. 14, 48. From the consequence the ground can be cognized, §. 29. Therefore from any monad whatever of every composite world, hence also of this one, the single parts of the world to which it belongs can be cognized, §. 14, i.e. the single monads of every composite world, hence also of this one, are forces, §. 199, representative of their universe (they are its active mirrors, §. 210, indivisible ones, §. 244, microcosms, worlds in compendium, and concentrations of their world, or they have a force, are endowed with a force representative of their universe).",["'ground': ratio; 'consequence': rationatum.","'single ones of single ones': singulae singularum."]],"met:401":["Monads representing their world, §. 400, either represent it to themselves, conscious of their perception, at least in part, or not, §. 10. Hence also the monads of this universe represent this world either only obscurely, or clearly, at least in part. The former are bare monads (im tiefen Schlaf liegende Monaden) (slumbering).",["'or not': aut minus, here and below.","'slumbering': sopitae, lulled to sleep."]],"met:402":["Monads clearly representing the world either represent it to themselves distinctly, at least in part, or not, §. 10. The former understand, §. 69. Therefore they have the faculty of cognizing distinctly, §. 216, i.e. an understanding (Verstand) (more strictly so called, cf. §. 519). An intellectual substance (das mit Verstand begabt), i.e. one endowed with understanding, is a spirit (ein Geist) (intelligence, person). Therefore the intellectual monads of this universe are spirits, §. 230. One admitting only spirits in this world is an idealist.",["'understanding': intellectus; 'understand': intelligunt.","'spirit': spiritus."]],"met:403":["The nexus of the spirits of some world among themselves is pneumatic (Verbindung der Geister). Now in every world, and in this one, in which spirits are present, single ones are connected with single ones. Therefore in this and every world in which spirits are present there is a universal pneumatic nexus, §. 357 (the world, cf. §. 354, pneumatic, intellectual, moral, cf. §. 723, the kingdom of grace).",[]],"met:404":["Every substance is a monad, §. 234. Every spirit is a substance, §. 402. Therefore it is a monad and a simple being, §. 230.",[]],"met:405":["All the spirits of every world, and of this one, in which spirits are present, are monads, §. 404, forces representative of their world at least in part distinctly, §. 402, not extended, nor single ones filling a space, nor do they have quantitative magnitude, indivisible, finite, endowed only with a certain degree of forces, subject to metaphysical evil, partially similar to one another, but also single ones partially dissimilar and unequal, nor single ones admitting a figure, §. 396, a single one of all is the most perfect, §. 77, 185.",["'admitting': agnoscentes, literally 'acknowledging'.","'a single one of all is the most perfect': unicus omnium perfectissimus est; the clause stands without connective in the Latin."]],"met:406":["A whole of monads is a *monadatum*. Therefore this world and every composite one is a *monadatum*, §. 394.",["*monadatum*: Baumgarten's coinage for what is made up of monads; left in Latin."]],"met:407":["Monads in a composite world either have also its parts for their *monadata* or not, §. 10, 406. In either case a *monadatum* of this kind will be extended, §. 396, of impenetrable parts, §. 398, partially dissimilar ones, §. 396, i.e. partially heterogeneous (difform, dissimilar), hence it will not be a totally homogeneous extended thing (uniform, similar), i.e. one of totally similar parts, §. 271.",["'dissimilar', 'similar' in the parentheses: dissimilare, similare, distinct from dissimilium, similium (dissimilis, similis) in the text."]],"met:408":["The monads of this world, simultaneous ones mutually determine for one another place, successive ones age, §. 281, 85, hence they mutually influence one another, §. 211, placed in conflict, §. 213. Therefore there is in this world a universal influence and conflict, §. 48, 306 (a war of all against all, discordant concord, concordant discord, §. 364).",["'influence': influxus (n.), influunt (v.).","'all against all': omnium contra omnia; the second is neuter, 'all things'."]],"met:409":["Of the monads in this world some influence one another more remotely, § 408, 27, some more nearly, present to one another, §. 223, and because an absolute leap in the world is impossible, §. 387, some most nearly, touching one another, §. 223.",["'touching': contingentes, from contingere, to touch."]],"met:410":["Because all the monads of this world mutually influence one another, §. 408, there is not of them a transeunt action or influence without reaction, §. 213.",["'transeunt action': actio transiens."]],"met:411":["Transeunt action or influence of parts of the world on others outside themselves is either simple or composite, §. 215. The simple is also the least, §. 247, 214. To this corresponds a reaction, either the least, or a greater, §. 160, 161. If it is the least, it is equal to the influence, §. 70. If a greater reaction be posited, that by which the reaction exceeds the influence posits a reaction of the influencing thing, §. 410, hence its influence will not be simple, §. 215, which is against the hypothesis. Therefore to a simple influence corresponds an equal reaction, §. 70.",[]],"met:412":["Composite influence of parts of the world on others outside themselves is a whole of several simple influences, §. 215, 214. To any one of these corresponds an equal reaction, §. 411. Now if you add equals to equals, the sums are equal, §. 70. Therefore to every composite influence of parts of the world corresponds an equal reaction. Hence every reaction of parts of the world on others outside themselves is equal to the influence or transeunt action, §. 411.",[]],"met:413":["The living forces of monads in the world conflicting with one another through mutual contact are equal, §. 412, 331, and because the same cannot be proximate grounds of contradictories, §. 140, 36, determining for one another most nearly place, §. 408, 409, hence conjunction, §. 281, 85, they do not contain the proximate ground of mutual separation, §. 72. Hence, unless a third force is added, they touch one another inseparably, §. 386, constituting one, §. 73, united, §. 79.",["'living forces': vires vivae.","'most nearly': proxime, which may go with 'determining' or with 'place'."]],"met:414":["Things touching one another in such a way that they cannot be separated except through a third force cohere (hangen an einander). Therefore the monads of the world touching one another cohere, §. 413. No contact is without cohesion. Therefore some monads of this world cohere, §. 409, constituting one, §. 413, an extended thing, §. 407.",["'No contact is without cohesion': Nullus contactus sine cohaesione est; it can also be read 'There is no contact without cohesion'."]],"met:415":["No change happens in a composite world without motion. For let A be to be changed from B into not-B. It had coexisted with its simultaneous things posited outside it as B, now it will coexist as not-B, hence it will obtain a diverse relation to them, §. 37, 38, a position, §. 85, and a place, §. 281, and there will be motion, §. 283, 125. As often as such a change, so often does such a motion happen in a composite world, the state of the changed thing, and of the universe of which it is a part, is partly the same as the preceding ones, §. 265, partly diverse, §. 125. Hence just as there was some certain motion insofar as the new state differs from the former one; so, insofar as the state has remained the same, this duration of the state is in a composite world at the same time a duration of place, §. 299, an absence of a certain motion, rest, §. 183, and there was an impediment of a certain motion, §. 221, and resistance, §. 222.",["'position': positum, probably for positus (Baumgarten's term for position); doubtful reading.","'As often as such a change, so often does such a motion happen': Quoties talis mutatio, talis ... motus fit; the 'so often' is carried by the correlative construction."]],"met:416":["The monads in the universe constituting an extended thing, §. 414, always act, §. 216, 285, by their force, §. 400, representing the single states of their universe, and their own, even future ones, §. 298, insofar as these are the same as the antecedent ones, persisting, impeding a certain motion, resisting a certain motion, §. 415, 210, but insofar as these are diverse from the antecedent ones, effecting a certain other motion, or moving, §. 415, 210. The aggregate of the predicates belonging to the single parts is attributed to the whole, §. 155. Hence certain monads of this universe constitute an extended thing to which a force of inertia is to be attributed, §. 294, hence matter, §. 295. No matter, either of this universe or of any, can be totally homogeneous, §. 407.",["'persisting': perdurantes, plural, agreeing with the monads; 'impeding', 'resisting', 'effecting', 'moving': impediente, resistente, efficiente, movente, singular, agreeing with 'force' (vi).","'or': s. (seu)."]],"met:417":["The monads of this universe constituting matter do not exhibit it as first, in the signification of §. 295, and merely passive, but as matter to which a moving force is attributed, §. 416, second, and a physical body, §. 296. If one part of this world is moved, its relation to the single remaining simultaneous ones is changed, §. 283, 281, hence there is not a particular motion (eine besondre Bewegung) in the world, that is, of some part of the world, without a universal one, §. 283. Hence in this world all matter is in motion, §. 415, and its rest is not, unless respective (Ruhe von einer gewissen Bewegung, oder in Absicht auf eine gewisse Bewegung), i.e. the absence of some determinate motion, §. 283, which, if it should be so great that no motion of the resting thing is observed, will be vanishing motion (eine unmerklich werdende, oder verschwindende Bewegung). No absolute rest in the world, absence of all motion.",["'first', 'second': primam, secundam, i.e. first (prime) and second matter, materia prima and secunda.","'No absolute rest in the world, absence of all motion': the sentence has no verb in the Latin."]],"met:418":["An extended thing, an aggregate of monads of which the single ones are impenetrable, will already on this account resist the motion of things posited outside it that would occupy its place, §. 398, 222, will already on this account exert a force of inertia, §. 294. An extended thing, an aggregate of monads of which no force is totally equal to any other posited outside itself, of which no whole is totally equal, as forces, to any other *monadatum* posited outside the former, §. 272, 406, is never in total equilibrium (Gleichgewicht der Kräfte), equality of forces in conflicting things, and already on this account will exert a moving force, §. 294. Such an extended thing, however, is every *monadatum* in this world, §. 414, 396. Therefore the *monadata* in this world, on account of one and the same representative force of their monads, §. 400, insofar as it contains in impenetrable things the ground of enduring place, of rest, and of a certain motion absent, will display a force of inertia, insofar as the same contains in things never totally in equilibrium the ground of change of place and of a certain motion, a moving force, matter, §. 416, and physical bodies, §. 417. So far is it from the case that these two forces are so opposed to one another that, one being posited, in a diverse respect the other could not at the same time be posited in the same subject, §. 87, that rather, in respect to diverse motions, they are at the same time in any extended thing whatever of this world, §. 417, and the same force attributed to a given extended thing is moving in respect to a given motion, in respect to the motion opposite to this a force of inertia, and vice versa, §. 415.",["'in a diverse respect': diverso respectu, which may go with 'one being posited' or with 'the other could not ... be posited'.","'matter, §. 416, and physical bodies': materia ... et corpora physica, in apposition to the *monadata*.","'vice versa': v.v."]],"met:419":["Since forces are attributed to bodies, §. 296, they are composites more strictly so called, §. 225, 198, hence the mode of composition is their essence, §. 226, they consist of monads, §. 235, they have quantitative magnitude, §. 243.",[]],"met:420":["Bodies have parts outside parts, §. 296, 224. The first actual parts of bodies outside one another are called elements (die allerersten Grund-Theile).",[]],"met:421":["Elements are either absolutely first parts of bodies, i.e. those which do not themselves again have parts outside parts, or relatively such, beyond which it does not please one to progress in attending to composition, §. 420. The former are simple beings or monads, §. 419, 224.",["'it does not please one to progress': progredi non placet, impersonal."]],"met:422":["What is not matter is called immaterial, what is matter is called material. Elements of bodies that are absolutely such are immaterial, §. 421, 295. Incorporeal is what is not a body, what is, corporeal. Now everything corporeal is material, §. 296. Therefore elements of bodies that are absolutely such are incorporeal, hence single ones are not bodies, §. 296.",["The transcription runs 'Immaterialevocatur' together, for Immateriale vocatur."]],"met:423":["The philosophy taking the respectively first for the absolutely first is called the philosophy of the lazy, and since it is false, relatively such elements, §. 421, material and corporeal, §. 422, are not to be taken for absolutely such elements, immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422. Hence the latter are more conveniently called elements simply, the former first matters (ein allererster Grund-Stoff), cf. §. 295.",["'first matters': materiae primae, which could also be genitive singular, 'of first matter'; the German gloss is singular."]],"met:424":["An atom (das an und vor sich untheilbare) is called a being indivisible through itself. Every monad is indivisible through itself, §. 244. Hence every monad is an atom. Therefore elements are atoms, §. 423, hence they are called atoms of nature.",["'through itself': per se."]],"met:425":["We call observable things (das wahrzunehmende) (phenomena) those which we can cognize through the senses (more confusedly). Smaller bodies not observable by us are called corpuscles. The philosophy explaining the phenomena of bodies from corpuscles is corpuscular.",[]],"met:426":["Corpuscles either have other corpuscles again for parts, or not, §. 10, 155. The latter are called primitive (die ersten), the former derivative (zusammengesetztere).",[]],"met:427":["Elements constitute all matter, such as it is in this world, §. 418, 419. Hence the quantitative magnitude to be attributed to it, §. 419, 423, is the multitude of elements united to constitute a certain matter, §. 159, 413. Hence elements, parts of a certain matter, are in the same place with it, as with their *monadatum*, §. 155, 406. Therefore if their place is so changed that they are not in the same place with the matter which they had previously constituted, they do not remain parts of that matter, §. 155. Now indeed every change of relation is possible in a being considered in itself, §. 133. Therefore every change of place is possible in all elements considered in themselves, §. 281, 85. Therefore also that motion by which they cease to be parts of the matter of which they had been parts, §. 283. If they are moved in this way, matter is divided, §. 244, 162. Therefore all matter, such as it is in the world, is divisible, at least in and through itself, §. 18.",["'considered': spectato, spectatis."]],"met:428":["If matter in the world is said to be divisible to infinity: this is understood either absolutely, that there are not indivisible parts to it, and it is false, §. 424, 419, or respectively, that for us in dividing it there are not observable parts, unless again divisible, §. 248, and it is true that matter is divisible to the indefinite, §. 425.",["'to infinity': in infinitum; 'to the indefinite': in indefinitum."]],"met:429":["A material atom would be a corpuscle indivisible through itself, which is nothing, §. 427, 425. The atomistic philosophy, therefore, explaining the phenomena of bodies from material atoms, is mistaken.",[]],"met:430":["The nature (cf. §. 431, 466) of a being is the complex of those of its internal determinations which are the principles of its changes, or in general of the accidents inhering in it. Hence to the nature of a being belong 1) its essentials, §. 39, 2) the essence, §. 40, 3) the faculties, 4) receptivities, §. 216, 5) the forces with which it is equipped, all, §. 197. The beginning of nature is origin (Erzeugung), the duration life (Leben), the end death (Tod), cf. §. 556.",["'complex': complexus."]],"met:431":["The nature of bodies is their mode of composition, §. 419, with every faculty, receptivity, force of inertia and moving force, which they possess, §. 296, 430. Sometimes it is also called nature simply, cf. §. 430, 466.",["'which they possess': qua pollent, 'with which they are powerful'."]],"met:432":["Since in the world there is universal truth, §. 363, 90, all its changes have common norms, §. 86, 89, according to which the nature of its parts is determined, §. 431, hence also the motions of bodies in the world, §. 283, of which the higher are called laws (gemeine), the lower rules of motion (besondre Gesetze der Bewegung).",["'of motion' (motus) may go with both 'laws' and 'rules'; the German glosses read 'common' and 'particular laws of motion'."]],"met:433":["A machine is a composite strictly so called movable according to laws of motion. Therefore every body in the world is a machine, §. 419, 432. Nor is there given in the world a machine, unless a contingent being, §. 361. The nature of a machine determined through laws of motion is mechanism. But whatever is not composite is not a machine, hence no monad is a machine, §. 230.",[]],"met:434":["The nexus of machines is mechanical, hence the nexus of bodies in the world is mechanical (the world, cf. §. 354, corporeal, material, the kingdom of nature), §. 433. But in whatever world there are spirits and bodies, in it bodies are also connected with spirits, §. 357, hence in a world in which there are spirits and bodies there is a nexus 1) of bodies among themselves, 2) of spirits among themselves, §. 403, 3) a mutual one of bodies and spirits, pneumatico-mechanical and mechanico-pneumatic (the harmony of the kingdom of nature and of grace).",[]],"met:435":["Mechanical philosophy is that explaining the phenomena of bodies from their mechanism. Fate from the mechanism of bodies is physicomechanical (physical or mechanical simply), but if any event of the world is determined through it, it is in itself contingent , §. 361, 354, not otherwise than hypothetically necessary, §. 382, 105.",["'it is in itself contingent': the subject is not expressed; it may be the event or the fate."]],"met:436":["The most perfect world is that in which the greatest of the most numerous, and the most numerous of the greatest parts compossible in a world agree toward one as much as can happen in a world. Hence the most perfect world has the most composite perfection, and any world to which nothing but simple perfection belongs is not the most perfect, §. 185.",["consentiunt ad unum: agree toward one (the focus of perfection); consensus is rendered agreement throughout."]],"met:437":["When the most perfect world is posited, the highest perfection that is possible in a world is posited, §. 436. Hence the most perfect world is also the best of all possible worlds, §. 187. Now the parts of a world are actual things, §. 354, these either simultaneous or successive, §. 306. Therefore the most perfect world comprises as many 1) simultaneous, as many 2) successive things, and 3) things as great, as many and as great as are compossible in the best world, i.e. it is 1) extensively, 2) protensively, 3) intensively the best greatest of worlds, §. 436, 368.",["optimus maximus: best greatest, the two superlatives side by side without a conjunction, as in the Latin.","complectitur: comprises (also embraces)."]],"met:438":["If even only two contingent things posited outside each other are compossible in a world, either as simultaneous or as successive, of which the perfection of the one detracts either nothing, or not as much from the perfection of the other as it adds to the perfection of the whole, an egoistic world, such as the egoist posits this one to be, is not the most perfect. If even only a single non-intellectual monad is possible in itself and compossible with spirits in a world, whose perfection detracts either nothing, or not as much from the perfection of spirits as it adds to the perfection of the whole, an idealistic world, such as the idealist posits this one to be, is not the most perfect, §. 437.",[]],"met:439":["The materialist denies that there is a monad in this world, §. 395, hence forms for himself an impossible world, §. 394, therefore the world is neither any world at all, nor the most perfect, such as the materialist feigns this one to be, a materialistic one, §. 436.",["fingit: feigns (also imagines, invents)."]],"met:440":["Even the most perfect world is finite, §. 372, and all its parts, even the most perfect, finite, §. 354, 257. Therefore neither it itself, nor anything in it is really infinite, §. 248. Not only, however, can both it itself and many of its parts be mathematically infinite in many respects, but it is also, for all who either cannot, or whom it does not please, to determine the limits of the greatest contingent thing, of an extension, protension, intension infinite mathematically as such, or intensively, extensively, protensively indefinite, §. 248, 437.",["The source runs Perfectissimusetiam together; read as Perfectissimus etiam.","multis rationibus: in many respects (ratio, elsewhere ground).","talis (as such) may go with infinitae or with ipse; left as ambiguous as the Latin."]],"met:441":["In the most perfect world there is the greatest universal nexus that is possible in a world, §. 437, 94, harmony and agreement, §. 436, 357.",[]],"met:442":["Since every world is perfect, §. 360, the most imperfect is that in which is the least perfection that can be in a world, the least agreement of the fewest least things toward one least thing, §. 354, 185, hence simple, §. 96, without exceptions, §. 97. The most imperfect world is the least, §. 368.",["The Latin leaves the copula of the relative clause unexpressed; is is supplied."]],"met:443":["Even if the most perfect world is posited as the best of worlds, §. 437, the highest good, or metaphysical optimum, is not on that account posited; even if there are posited in it the greatest most numerous realities that are compossible in any world, yet not absolutely the most numerous greatest, §. 190, much less absolutely necessary ones, since the existence of each of them is a mode, §. 361. Even if to the most perfect world there is attributed the greatest degree of reality to be obtained in some universe, that will nevertheless in a contingent being be nothing but a limit, §. 248, 250, from the absolutely greatest, necessary, immutable degree of reality of the really infinite, §. 251, thoroughly diverse, §. 38.",["summum bonum, s. optimum metaphysicum: the highest good, or metaphysical optimum."]],"met:444":["In every perfection there is order, §. 95. Hence in the most perfect world there is the greatest order that is possible in a world, §. 437, 175. Therefore the most numerous common rules of perfection, e.g. The more, the greater, the more spacious, §. 280, the more lasting, §. 299, other things being equal, the better, §. 437, 187, and the most composite order, §. 183, in such a way, however, that the lower and higher rules can at last all be cognized toward one from one highest rule of perfection, and that the strongest, §. 182, 185.",["The second sentence has no verb; the Latin is equally elliptical.","ceteris paribus: other things being equal."]],"met:445":["Since in the most perfect world there are the most numerous rules of perfection, §. 444, there can also be very many exceptions, §. 97, 372, provided they do not remove the greatest agreement, §. 440, hence that they be, as far as they can, other things being equal, the fewest, §. 161, the least, §. 186.",[]],"met:446":["If in the most perfect world a sufficient and an insufficient ground of perfection collide, an exception is made from the insufficient: if a more fruitful and a less fruitful collide, an exception is made from the less fruitful; if a weightier and a less weighty, an exception is made from the less weighty; if a more remote and a nearer one subordinate to the more remote, an exception is made from the nearer; if one sufficient in a certain respect and one simply such, an exception is made from the one sufficient in a certain respect; if a lower and a higher, an exception is made from the lower. Whatever rule of perfection, finally, collides in the most perfect world with the highest, an exception is made from it, §. 186, 445.",["ratio perfectionis: ground of perfection.","secundum quid ... simpliciter: in a certain respect ... simply.","Quaecumquae is evidently a misprint for quaecumque."]],"met:447":["Of the most perfect world not only do §. 361-376, 354, 436 hold, but there are also most remote from it the things which §. 380-388, 247, 354 teach are absent from every world. e.g. So far is Spinozistic fate from it, §. 382, that rather indefinite contingency and mutability, extensively, intensively and protensively, are to be attributed to it, §. 440, 437, hypothetical too, §. 104, 127.",[]],"met:448":["Since in this world there is harmony, §. 356, there is universal influx, §. 408, this will be either everywhere real, or everywhere ideal, or sometimes real, sometimes ideal, §. 212. The harmony of the monads of a world influencing one another is the commerce of mundane substances (die Verbindung des in einer ganzen Welt vor sich bestehenden). Therefore the substances both of every world and of this one are in universal commerce, §. 357. The opinions which seem suited to explaining this are called systems for explaining the commerce among the substances of the world (Meinungen von der Art und Weise solcher Verbindung), and if they are applied to explaining every commerce of all, universal (allgemeine), cf. §. 761 (general). The changes of parts of a world placed in commerce, of which, occurring in one part of the commerce, the sufficient ground can be cognized from the force of the other part of the commerce, are harmonic (übereinstimmende Veränderungen). The ideal influx of all substances of the world into one another is universal pre-established harmony (die allgemeine vorherbestimmte Übereinstimmung), cf. §. 462, and one positing it in this world is a universal harmonist, whose system is called the system of universal pre-established harmony (die Meinung der allgemeinen vorherbestimmten Übereinstimmung).",["Cum ... sit ..., sit ...: both clauses stand under since in the Latin, the second without a conjunction.","commercium: commerce; influxus: influx; ratio sufficiens: sufficient ground.","(generalia): Baumgarten's alternative term, rendered general."]],"met:449":["The system of universal pre-established harmony does not remove the influx of the substances of this world into one another, but posits it, §. 448, does not remove that one substance of the world suffers from another, §. 448, 212, but nevertheless posits that it produces by its own force any change of its own which it suffers from another substance of this world, §. 210, does not remove the mutual conflict of the parts of this world, §. 213, the faculty and receptivity of transeunt actions, §. 217, much less the influx of the infinite substance, and the receptivity of that influx in finite things, §. 448, not only does not remove that spirits can act upon bodies, and bodies upon spirits, but even posits that in this world bodies and spirits influence one another, §. 408, 434, and can touch one another, §. 223, 409.",["pati: suffer, i.e. be acted upon; its noun passio is rendered passion."]],"met:450":["The real influx of a substance, part of a world, into another part of the world is physical. Hence universal physical influx is the universal harmony of substances in the world by which one really influences another, and one positing it in this world is a universal influxionist. His system is the system of universal physical influx.",[]],"met:451":["The system of universal physical influx does not remove the mutual harmony of the substances of the world, and that a universal one, §. 450, 48, but the pre-established one, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the substances of this world influence one another, conflict with one another, suffer from one another, and that bodies can do both with respect to spirits, spirits with respect to bodies, indeed that they can touch one another, is a universal influxionist, §. 449, 450. The universal influxionist 1) denies that any substance of this world, when it suffers from another substance of this world, acts and produces its passions by its own force, §. 450, 212, 2) posits that the passions of one substance of this world are really produced by another substance of this world influencing it, §. 450. Hence through the system of universal physical influx no substance, part of the world, acts by its own force in any of its harmonic changes, §. 448. But now all changes of a mundane substance can be sufficiently cognized from the force of any other monad belonging to the same world, §. 354, 400. Therefore all are harmonic, §. 448, and hence in none of its changes does any mundane substance act, according to the system of universal physical influx, really suffering in all of them from other mundane substances, which nevertheless for the same reason would not act at all, §. 210, hence would not be forces either, §. 197, *q.c.*, §. 199.",["sufficientur is evidently a misprint for sufficienter (sufficiently).","*q.c.* is kept as printed; probably quod contradicit (which contradicts).","utrumque posse: can do both (act and suffer)."]],"met:452":["Whoever posits that into all substances of this world seen to suffer from other substances of this world, and, according to his own opinion, really suffering ones, the infinite substance alone really influences, is a universal occasionalist (*assistentianus*), and his system is the universal system of occasional causes (die Meinung der allgemeinen sogenannten nur gelegentlichen Ursachen) (Cartesian, or rather Malebranchian, of assistance), contradicting §. 400, 408, hence false. The same is evident from this, that through occasionalism no harmonic change in any mundane substance is its own action, but only that of the infinite substance, §. 448. Hence this system too removes all force and energy in finite things, §. 451.",["energian: Greek accusative form of energia in the Latin.","The construction of the first clause (in ... influere with the substances before the verb) is kept in its Latin order."]],"met:453":["The system of occasional causes removes, if not harmony, yet the true universal mutual influx of the substances of this world, §. 452, 448, this, the physical, §. 450, and the ideal, §. 212 (unless you so call an imaginary, apparent one, §. 12), that, the pre-established, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the infinite substance really influences all passions of finite substances, nor everyone who posits that the infinite substance alone really influences them, is a universal occasionalist, but he who posits 1) that all passions of finite substances are real, 2) that they depend on the influx of the infinite substance alone, §. 452.",["hunc ... illam: this (masculine, the influx) ... that (feminine, the harmony)."]],"met:454":["The systems of universal pre-established harmony and of universal physical influx are similar in this, §. 70, that 1) they remove neither the mutual influx of the substances of this world, nor harmony, nor the real influx of the infinite substance into them, universal ones, §. 448, 450, 2) they posit that the passions of the substances of this world are actions of some finite substance; they are dissimilar in this, that the latter excludes the suffering substance from action, the former does not exclude it, §. 449, 451.",["universales (universal ones) may qualify the influxes and the harmony removed; left as unattached as in the Latin."]],"met:455":["The universal system of pre-established harmony and that of occasional causes are similar in this, that they remove the real influx of a substance of this world into another, a part of the same, §. 70, they are dissimilar in this, §. 70, that the former posits as ideal certain passions of them which the latter posits as real, §. 449, 453.",["The Latin has singular Systema with plural universalia, as if one system of two kinds."]],"met:456":["The universal system of physical influx and that of occasional causes are similar in this, that each posits as real the passions of one substance in this world from another part of the same world; they are dissimilar in this, §. 70, that the former removes, the latter posits, that the infinite substance alone influences them, §. 451, 452.",[]],"met:457":["Universal systems for explaining the commerce of mundane substances, if they explain the individual commerces of the individual substances of the world in a similar manner, are called simple (die allgemeine Meinungen von der Art und Weise der Verbindung des vor sich bestehenden in einer ganzen Welt sind entweder einfach). Therefore the universal systems of pre-established harmony, of physical influx and of occasional causes are simple systems, §. 448, 456. Composite (oder zusammengesetzt) universal systems are those which explain certain commerces of certain substances of the world in a different manner than others.",[]],"met:458":["Besides the universal systems of pre-established harmony, of physical influx, and of occasional causes, some fourth simple universal one, §. 457, is not possible, §. 448, 452, infinitely or rather indefinitely many can be composite, §. 457, 248.",[]],"met:459":["Through pre-established harmony, the ideal influx of several substances of the world placed in commerce into one another, a greater nexus is actualized than through the physical influx of the same, §. 167. For in physical influx the passion of what really suffers does not have a sufficient ground in the forces of what suffers, §. 450, 212. In pre-established harmony the passion of the suffering substance has a sufficient ground 1) in its own forces, 2) in the ideally influencing substance, §. 449. Hence the influencing substance in pre-established harmony is just as fruitful as in physical influx, but the suffering substance is more fruitful in pre-established harmony than in physical influx, §. 166.",["fecundus: fruitful."]],"met:460":["Through the influx, attributed to the infinite substance alone, into substances placed in commerce really suffering whatever they suffer, the suffering substance is not more fruitful than through physical influx, §. 453, but the other, finite part of the commerce is still less fruitful, §. 453, 166. Therefore through pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance a greater nexus is actualized in substances placed in commerce than through the influx attributed to the infinite substance alone, §. 167.",[]],"met:461":["In the most perfect world there is the greatest nexus of things possible in a world, §. 441. Therefore the substances which, and as many as, can in it, are connected through pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance, §. 460, 459.",["quae et quot possunt in eo: the infinitive after possunt is unexpressed; left elliptical as in the Latin."]],"met:462":["If universal pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance is possible in the most perfect world, that world is such as the universal harmonist posits this one to be, §. 448, 461, and is not such as anyone about to adopt some composite system might conceive it, §. 451, whereas every particular pre-established harmony (besondre vorherbestimmte Übereinstimmung), i.e. of certain parts of the world, is to be admitted in it, §. 154.",[]],"met:463":["Since the condition *sine qua non* of universal pre-established harmony is its possibility in the most perfect world, this must be demonstrated by one who would demonstrate pre-established harmony, by §. 462. Otherwise it is evident thus: from any monad whatever of any world whatever the single parts of the world to which it belongs can be cognized, §. 400, therefore also the single worldly changes, §. 354, 155. But such are all the passions of any worldly monad whatever, which it suffers from another monad of its world, §. 210. But a suffering monad is a force, §. 199. Therefore that very monad suffering from another worldly monad is the ground of this its passion, and of its single parts, §. 354, 155, hence the sufficient ground of this its passion, §. 14, 21, and so its given passion is at the same time an action of the sufferer, §. 210. Therefore all passions of the monads of any world whatever, which they suffer from other worldly monads, are merely ideal, §. 212. Therefore such also is always the influx of a worldly monad into another, never real, never physical, §. 450. Hence in this world too all its substances are in universal commerce only through pre-established harmony, §. 448, none ever through physical influx. The system of universal pre-established harmony is a true opinion, therefore also every particular pre-established harmony is true, §. 462, every physical influx, both universal and particular, and its system, §. 450, and the system of occasional causes, §. 452, are false.",["'passion', 'suffers', 'suffering', 'sufferer': passio, patitur, patiens; also 'undergoing'.","'commerce': commercium; 'influx': influxus; 'opinion': sententia."]],"met:464":["In the most perfect world there is the nexus which is the greatest possible in a world, §. 441. Therefore if there are in the most perfect world bodies and spirits, §. 438, the mechanical, pneumatic, and pneumatico-mechanical nexus is the greatest in the same, §. 441 (the greatest harmony of the kingdom of nature and of grace, §. 434, 403, pre-established, §. 463).",["'there is the nexus which is the greatest possible in a world': nexus est, qui in mundo possibilis, maximus; literally 'the nexus is, which is possible in a world, the greatest'."]],"met:465":["A dualist is one who posits that this world consists of spirits and bodies outside one another. Hence the most perfect world is not egoistic, not idealistic, §. 438, it is such as the dualist conceives this one, §. 439.",["'outside one another': extra se, also 'outside themselves'."]],"met:466":["The complex of natures in the single and in the jointly taken parts of the world is universal nature (*naturata*, cf. §. 859). Hence the nature of this and of the most perfect universe is the aggregate or complex of all essentials, essences, faculties, receptivities, forces, with which all its parts, monads, elements, spirits, matters, bodies are furnished, §. 430, hence every mode of composition of all the bodies in it, the force of inertia, the moving force, and the mechanism is a part of universal nature, §. 431, 433.",["'universal nature': natura universa; *naturata* qualifies it, i.e. natura naturata."]],"met:467":["The essence of the world and of its composite, the mode of composition, §. 226, together with the essences of its single parts, is nothing but a part, and a rather small one, §. 161, of universal nature, §. 466, 155.",["'a rather small one': satis parva; satis here 'fairly, quite', not 'sufficiently'.","'and of its composite, the mode of composition': eiusque compositi modus compositionis; the essence of the world, as of any composite, is taken to be its mode of composition."]],"met:468":["Universal nature has internal mutable parts, §. 466, and the universal nature of the most perfect world very many of them, §. 437. Hence the universal nature of any world whatever is a contingent being, §. 131, and finite, §. 255, and indeed the universal nature of the most perfect world has a mathematically thrice infinite contingency, §. 447.",[]],"met:469":["An event to be actualized by the nature of any contingent being is natural, as contradistinguished from supernatural, cf. §. 474. But an event to be actualized by the determinate nature of a determinate contingent being is natural, as contradistinguished from preternatural, cf. §. 474. Whatever can be actualized by the nature of something is physically possible to it, whatever cannot, is physically impossible to it. Certain things possible in themselves can nevertheless not be actualized by just any nature, §. 15, 430, therefore they can be physically impossible to many. Not everything physically impossible to some is also absolutely such. Things physically impossible to some contingent thing are such either in whatever state its nature is regarded, naturally impossible simply (merely, altogether, utterly), or only then, if its nature is regarded in a certain state, naturally impossible in a certain respect (for now and thus). Natural impossibility simply such of realities is merely natural impotence. Not every natural impossibility is merely natural impotence or absolute impossibility, §. 467. The opposite of the physically impossible is the physically necessary, of the physically possible the physically contingent. Not everything physically necessary is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Certain things contingent in themselves can be physically necessary to many, §. 104. Physically necessary things are either simply, or in a certain respect such.",["'to be actualized': actuandus; 'by the nature of any contingent being': ab ullius entis contingentis natura.","'simply' and 'in a certain respect': simpliciter and secundum quid; 'for now and thus': pro nunc et sic.","'Natural impossibility simply such of realities': impossibilitas naturalis simpliciter talis realitatum; 'simply such' may go with 'natural impossibility' or with 'of realities'."]],"met:470":["Natural to a certain body is what is to be actualized by its determinate nature; to a certain corporeal world it is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the bodies of a certain world, §. 466. Natural to a certain contingent spirit is what is to be actualized through its determinate nature. Natural to a certain pneumatic world is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the spirits of a certain world, §. 469.",[]],"met:471":["The succession of natural things in the world is the course of nature. Hence the course of nature is opposed to the succession either of supernatural or of preternatural things, §. 469, and is regarded either in some body, or in some whole corporeal world, or in some spirit, or in some whole pneumatic world, or in universal nature, §. 470, 466.",[]],"met:472":["The order of natural things in the world is the order of nature, and it is opposed to the conjunction either of supernatural or of preternatural things, and is regarded either in a certain body only, or corporeal world, or spirit, or pneumatic world, §. 469, 470, or in universal nature, §. 466. Each single one of these orders has its common norms, §. 86. Therefore there are in this world, as norms of the natural order, common laws and rules, §. 432, diverse, §. 38, but similar, §. 70. The complex of similar laws is a right (ein Recht), cf. §. 971 (said in the wider sense). The complex of the laws of the order of nature is the right of nature in the widest sense, its parts are the laws and rules of motion, §. 432, and the laws of the nature of spirits.",["'right': ius, which also means 'law' as a body of laws; 'right of nature': ius naturae, also 'natural law'.","'conjunction': coniunctio; 'norms': normae."]],"met:473":["If natural follows natural, it happens according to the course of nature, and that according to the course of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain material nexus in a certain world, or of a certain spirit, or of a certain pneumatic nexus in a certain world also §. 471, or at least according to the course of universal nature, §. 466, if it succeeds, or coexists with, natural, it happens according to the order of nature, and that according to the order of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain material nexus in a certain world, or of a certain spirit, or of a certain pneumatic nexus in a certain world also, §. 472, or at least according to the order of universal nature, §. 466. Therefore the natural succeeding and coexisting with natural things is actualized according to natural laws, §. 472. What is natural to a certain body, or to a certain nexus in the material world, is actualized according to the laws and rules of motion through the mechanism of bodies, §. 433, hence can be explained mechanically, §. 435, 433.",["'material nexus', 'pneumatic nexus': materialis and pneumatici agree with nexus, not with mundo.","The text has no comma before the first '§. 471'."]],"met:474":["An event of the world actualized by the nature of no contingent being is supernatural. An event not actualized by the determinate nature of a certain contingent being, in which it happens, is preternatural with respect to that being. Hence supernatural things are preternatural with respect to universal nature; and preternatural things with respect to universal nature are supernatural, §. 466. A supernatural event, insofar as it is regarded as extraordinary, is a miracle (ein Wunder, Wunderwerk). Hence a miracle is preternatural with respect to universal nature. Every miracle is supernatural, but not everything supernatural is referred to miracles.",[]],"met:475":["Supernatural things and miracles are the opposites of natural things, and of naturally ordinary things, §. 81, 474. Now natural and naturally ordinary things in every world are contingent, §. 354, 257. Therefore supernatural things and miracles are possible, §. 101.",[]],"met:476":["Things which are actualized in the world through the nature of a contingent being are not supernatural, nor miracles, §. 474. Hence things actualized through the nature of a spirit which is a part of the world are not supernatural, not miracles, §. 354, 257.",[]],"met:477":["Actions of finite spirits upon other parts of the world, not actualized through the determinate nature of the sufferers, hence preternatural with respect to the sufferers, §. 474, and extraordinary, §. 472, 384, are nevertheless not supernatural, nor miracles, §. 474. Hence if they are called comparative miracles (Vergleichungs-Weise), comparative miracles are not the miracles which, as to be contradistinguished from them, are called rigorous (nach der Strenge sogenannte Wunder). If such actions are posited above nature, this can be conceded only of the certain nature of a certain body or corporeal world, §. 470.",["'sufferers': patientes, the things acted upon.","'rigorous': rigorosa, i.e. miracles in the strict sense."]],"met:478":["An extraordinary event whose natural cause we do not know is a prodigy (ein geschehendes Zeichen) (sign, cf. §. 347, ostent, portent). Prodigies are either rigorous miracles, §. 474, 477, or natural events, miracles as regards us (Wunder nach der Fassung des Wahrnehmenden).",["'prodigy': prodigium; 'ostent': ostentum; 'portent': portentum.","'miracles as regards us': miracula quoad nos."]],"met:479":["Since supernatural things and miracles are not actualized through universal nature, §. 474, 466, and are nevertheless contingent beings, §. 474, 354, hence effects, §. 334, when they exist they have as efficient cause an extramundane being, §. 388, of which they bear witness, §. 333.",[]],"met:480":["An event not natural succeeding natural things happens contrary to the course of nature, §. 471, 473. Now the supernatural and a miracle is not a natural event, §. 474, 469. Therefore the supernatural and a miracle comes about contrary to the course of nature, succeeding natural things, and indeed contrary to the course of universal nature, §. 471. What is preternatural to a certain body, or a certain corporeal world, a certain spirit, or a certain pneumatic world comes about contrary to some course of nature only, §. 474, 471. Hence not everything happening contrary to any course of nature whatever is supernatural, or a miracle, §. 474.",["'happens': evenit; 'comes about': fit."]],"met:481":["Supernatural things and miracles are not absolutely extraordinary, §. 475, 385, nor contrary to all order of the world, §. 359, 7, nor yet are they naturally ordinary, §. 472, 474. And since an event not natural coexisting with or succeeding natural things happens contrary to the order of nature, supernatural things and miracles are contrary to every order of universal nature. Therefore they do not happen according to the common norms of a certain order of nature, §. 472. Therefore they are extraordinary relative to the order of nature, §. 384. Supernatural things and miracles are extraordinary with respect to the order of universal nature, §. 472. The preternatural is extraordinary with respect to a certain body, or corporeal world, a certain spirit, or pneumatic nexus, and the order of nature determined in the same, §. 474, 472, so that it happens contrary to this, §. 384. Therefore not everything extraordinary with respect to any order of nature whatever, not everything which is contrary to any order of nature whatever, is supernatural, much less a miracle, §. 474.",[]],"met:482":["The law of order in the most perfect world, §. 84, and that one of the higher ones, §. 182, is this: Let the best of compossibles in the most perfect world be conjoined with the best, §. 444. This law is called the law of the best in the world, cf. §. 822, to which is subordinated the law of the best in nature: let the best of natural things in the most perfect world be conjoined with the best, §. 469, 444.",["'the best': optimum; 'one of the higher ones': una ex superioribus (laws)."]],"met:483":["Posit in the most perfect world a natural event, hence one that will happen according to the order of nature, §. 473, compossible with the rest. Posit under the same circumstances a supernatural event, hence one that will happen contrary to every order of nature, §. 481, compossible with the rest, but better than the natural. Since opposite events, natural and supernatural, e.g. miraculous, cannot exist at the same time, §. 81, in this case the rules of the order of universal nature, and the higher rule of perfection, §. 482, will collide, §. 97.",[]],"met:484":["Since the rules of the order of nature, both particular and universal, are in the most perfect world inferior and subordinated to the law of the best in the world, §. 182, 482, in the case posited by §. 483 there will come about an exception from the laws of the order of nature, §. 446, and something supernatural will come about, indeed a miracle, §. 483, most conformably to the highest order of the most perfect world, §. 444.",["'particular': peculiaris; 'highest' (summo) agrees with 'order', not with 'world'."]],"met:485":["Supernatural things and miracles through an absolute leap would be impossible, §. 387, hence no supernatural things, no miracles come about through an absolute leap, §. 475. Since, however, the proximate sufficient ground of the naturally ordinary among events in the world is the nature of some contingent being, §. 472, 469, when supernatural things and miracles happen, they happen through a respective leap, §. 474, 386.",["'leap': saltus; 'respective': respectivus, i.e. relative, as opposed to absolute."]],"met:486":["Supernatural things and miracles happening in the corporeal world cannot be explained mechanically, §. 435, 433, much less pneumatic ones, happening in the pneumatic world (the kingdom of grace), §. 433, 403.",[]],"met:487":["If in place of a natural event in the world a supernatural and miraculous one is posited, the world is not totally the same as it would be if the natural event were a part of it, §. 267, 155. Therefore, a supernatural thing and a miracle being posited in the world in place of a natural effect, there is posited a state of the world, coexisting with the given supernatural thing or miracle, partially other than it would be had the supernatural thing or miracle not been performed, §. 378, 369.",["'performed': patratus."]],"met:488":["Nothing is utterly sterile, §. 23, hence no supernatural thing, no miracle is without a corollary, or consequence; this being posited, something is posited again, §. 14, hence its consequence anew, §. 23, and so on. Therefore of every supernatural thing, of every miracle, consectaries are posited into the indefinite, §. 248, through the successive states of the world after the supernatural event, after the miracle, §. 30, 369.",["'consequence': rationatum; 'consectaries': consectaria."]],"met:489":["If in place of the supernatural event, in place of the miracle, there had been posited in the world, in the case mentioned in §. 487, 488, a natural event, this too would have had its consequences into the indefinite through all the states of the world succeeding it, §. 23, 369, but the consectaries of a natural and of a supernatural or miraculous effect cannot be totally the same, §. 267, 38. Therefore, a supernatural thing being posited, a miracle being posited in the world in place of a natural effect, all the states of the world succeeding the supernatural event, or miracle, are partially other than they would have been had the given supernatural thing, or miracle, not been performed, §. 369.",["'consequences': rationata; 'consectaries': consectaria."]],"met:490":["In the system of occasionalism every passion of a finite substance is supernatural, §. 474, 453, although some can be conceived which would not be a miracle, insofar as it is regarded as ordinary, §. 384.",["'passion': passio, also 'undergoing'."]],"met:491":["Since the supernatural and a miracle is an action, §. 474, 323, it will be either simple, or composite, §. 215, and this indeed either a simultaneous being, or a successive being, or both, §. 238. Both the simple and the composite, provided it is a simultaneous being, happen in an instant, destitute of prior and posterior parts, §. 300.",["'provided it is': modo; 'it' may be the composite alone or both.","'this' (hoc): the composite."]],"met:492":["The more fecund, the more noble the supernatural and miracle, §. 166, the greater it is, §. 491, 214, hence the greatest will be the most fecund, most noble, and most composite, §. 214, 215.",[]],"met:493":["The naturalist, said in the wider sense, cf. §. 999, is one who removes all supernatural events in this world. Therefore he removes the existence of miracles in this world, §. 474, if he does so on account of their denied possibility, he errs, §. 475.",["'removes': tollit, also 'does away with'.","'on account of their denied possibility': ob negatam eorundem possibilitatem, i.e. because their possibility is denied."]],"met:494":["The nature of this world being posited, as it is, of mathematically infinite extension, intension, protension, §. 440, 466, it is also of infinite contingency of this kind, §. 468, 361, nor can it exist except as the effect of an extramundane cause, of which it testifies, §. 375, as do supernatural things and miracles, when they exist, §. 479. Hence one not confusing the limits of his theory of the universal nature of this world with the limits of the said nature itself, nor thence securely concluding from his ignorance alone of a natural cause to the denial of it altogether: is not for that reason already unjust toward the cause of universal nature common to supernatural things and miracles, §. 375, 479, nor a naturalist, §. 493.",["The second sentence has no expressed subject; the participles \"confundens\" and \"concludens\" imply a person, rendered minimally as \"one\".","\"Posita natura\" is an ablative absolute; \"posited\" renders \"ponere\"."]],"met:495":["Posit a natural and a supernatural event in the most perfect world, compossible under the same circumstances with the remaining parts of the world, but, as consequences, equally good. The natural will satisfy the law of the best in the world equally as the supernatural, §. 482, and at the same time the rules of perfection which the order of nature prescribes, §. 472, hence more of them than the supernatural, §. 481. In this case, therefore, there will not come about something supernatural, or a miracle, in the most perfect world, §. 482, 445.",["The Latin has \"compossibilem\" in the singular beside the plural \"bonos\"; the English leaves both attached to the pair of events.","\"qua consectaria\": \"as consequences\" (consectarium)."]],"met:496":["Since the universal nature even of the most perfect world is finite, §. 468, it is not a sufficient principle of all the accidents which can inhere in the best world, §. 430, 259. Posit therefore an event compossible with the rest in the best world, the best of the best, but not to be actualized naturally and ordinarily, posit that the same can be actualized supernaturally and extraordinarily; there will come about in this case also, as in §. 484, something supernatural, indeed a miracle, most fittingly to the order of the most perfect world, §. 482, 444.",["\"optimis optimum\": literally \"best among the best\"."]],"met:497":["However many and however great best things can occur in the best world naturally, and ordinarily, happen in the same naturally, §. 495. However many and however great either cannot at all in the same, or cannot equally well occur naturally and ordinarily, happen in the same, the best things, supernaturally and miraculously, §. 484, 496. The former miracles can be called supplementing miracles (ergänzende), the latter emending (ausbessernde Wunder). The case is the same with supernatural things.",["The comma before \"et ordinario\" in the first sentence is the source's; \"ordinarily\" may go with \"can occur\" or with \"happen\".","\"Par est ratio\": literally \"the account is equal\"."]],"met:498":["The number of supernatural things and miracles in the most perfect world is as great as the number of its possibles which cannot be actualized equally well naturally and according to the order of nature, §. 159, 497, neither greater nor smaller, §. 160.",[]],"met:499":["If ever any supernatural event in the best world is better than a natural one, as consequences too, if ever any possible event of the best world is naturally impossible through universal nature: the most perfect world is not such as the naturalist in the more general signification declares it to be, §. 498, 493.",["\"significatu generaliori\": \"in the more general signification\" (significatus)."]],"met:500":["Not everyone who denies that certain preternatural things, §. 474, extraordinary effects of finite spirits, §. 476, comparative miracles, §. 477, prodigies and miracles with respect to us, §. 478, things occurring contrary to the course of nature, §. 480, and to some order, §. 481, are miracles in this world, §. 474, is a naturalist as such in the more general signification, §. 493.",["\"quoad nos\": \"with respect to us\"."]],"met:501":["Psychology is the science of the general predicates of the soul.",[]],"met:502":["Psychology, containing the first principles of the theologies, of aesthetics, of logic, of the practical sciences, is with reason, §. 501, referred, §. 2, to metaphysics, §. 1.",["\"prima\" may go with \"principia\" (first principles) or be read as \"containing first the principles\"; the former is given."]],"met:503":["Psychology deducing its assertions 1) from experience more proximately, empirical, 2) from the notion of the soul by a longer series of ratiocinations, rational.",["\"notio\": \"notion\"; \"ratiocinia\": \"ratiocinations\"."]],"met:700":["I am changed, §. 505-699, internally, §. 126. Therefore I am a finite being, §. 254, and contingent, §. 257. Therefore my existence is a mode, §. 134, and so all my states are contingent in themselves, §. 205, 108. Therefore also the changes of all of them, §. 124, 125. Therefore all my actions, all my passions are contingent in themselves, §. 210. Hence also all my future actions, §. 298. Hence none of my actions, none of my passions are absolutely or intrinsically necessary, §. 105.",["\"Mutor\": passive, \"I am changed\", which may also be read \"I change\"."]],"met:701":["Necessitation (die Nöthigung) (coercion) is the change of something from contingent into necessary, hence it is either active (die vorgenommene), of the necessitating thing, which properly is nothing but a substance, §. 198, or passive (die gelittene), of the necessitated thing, which is attributed both to accidents, either to an action or to a passion, which are changed from contingent into necessary, and to the substance in which accidents necessitated in this way inhere.",["\"coactio\": \"coercion\" throughout; \"necessitatio\": \"necessitation\".","\"die vorgenommene\" and \"die gelittene\" are feminine, agreeing with \"Nöthigung\"."]],"met:702":["Absolute necessitation (coercion) (an und vor sich nothwendig machen, eine unbedingte Nöthigung) would be that which changed what is contingent in itself into something absolutely necessary. Now nothing can be changed into something absolutely necessary, §. 130. Therefore neither any action, therefore neither any action of mine. Therefore absolute necessitation of any action of mine is impossible, §. 7. All my actions are and always remain, in the act itself, and after it, contingent in themselves, hence their opposite also possible in itself, §. 700, 104.",[]],"met:703":["Of the opposite of many of my actions the hypothetical possibility is very great, §. 168. Hence of many of my actions the contingency, even hypothetical, is very great, §. 188, 700. Hence the future ones are very contingent futures, §. 298.",["\"futura contingentia\": \"contingent futures\", the scholastic term."]],"met:704":["An action dependent on a sufficient principle which is internal to the agent is spontaneous (eine selbstthätige Handlung). Whence spontaneity is attributed, 1) to an action which depends on a sufficient principle which is internal to the agent, 2) to a substance performing actions of this kind. Now every action properly such depends on a principle which is internal to the agent, §. 210, 37. Therefore every action properly such is spontaneous. Since, however, changes composed of actions and passions are sometimes denominated actions from the weightier part, insofar as they are passions, they are conceived as not spontaneous, §. 210.",["\"a potiori\": \"from the weightier part\", i.e. named after what predominates in them.","\"spontanea\" / \"spontaneitas\": \"spontaneous\" / \"spontaneity\"; the German is \"selbstthätig\"."]],"met:705":["Many of my actions, indeed all properly so called, hence also the actions of my soul are spontaneous, and so spontaneity is truly attributed both to them and to my soul, §. 704. If an automaton is said to be something changing itself, the soul will be an automaton.",[]],"met:706":["Spontaneity is least if the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices for only one least action, §. 704, 161. Therefore the more, the greater the actions for which the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices, the greater is its spontaneity, until it is greatest in that agent in which it suffices for the most, greatest actions. A very great spontaneity belongs to my soul, §. 705.",[]],"met:707":["External necessitation (die äussre Nöthigung) (coercion from without) is that dependent on a force existing outside the necessitated substance, and is either ideal or real, §. 701, 212. An action externally really necessitated would not be spontaneous, nor properly an action, §. 704, but a real passion, §. 210. Real external necessitation is external necessitation (coercion) simply such (die äussre schlechterdings so genannte Nöthigung). Therefore actions coerced by external necessitation simply such would be real passions. A substance and actions which are not absolutely necessitated are free (cf. §. 719) from absolute coercion (Kraft und Handlung sind frei von unbedingter Nöthigung). A substance and actions which are not necessitated by external coercion simply such are free from external coercion simply such (Kraft und Handlung sind frei von äussrer schlechterdings so genannter Nöthigung). Therefore all my changes are free from absolute coercion, §. 702, all my actions, all the actions of my soul spontaneous, i.e. all actions properly so called, §. 704, and my soul, insofar as it acts spontaneously, are free from external coercion simply such.",["The German equivalents render \"substantia\" as \"Kraft\" (force).","In the last sentence \"spontaneae\" may be attributive (\"the spontaneous actions of my soul\") or predicative (\"all actions of my soul being spontaneous\"); the English keeps the Latin order.","\"simpliciter talis\": \"simply such\"; the German has \"schlechterdings so genannt\"."]],"met:708":["Actions physically possible to me are placed in my power (Handlungen stehn), realities physically impossible to me are placed outside my power (stehn nicht in meiner Gewalt). Therefore there is some action placed in my power either simply only, or also in a certain respect, there is some either simply also, or only in a certain respect outside my power, §. 469. Of actions placed in the power of some agent the opposite either is also placed in the power of the same agent, or outside it, §. 9, and both again either simply, or in a certain respect, §. 469. Those which themselves together with their opposites, simply at least, are placed in someone's power, are free to him (cf. §. 719) with respect to execution (bei denen Thun und Lassen in meiner Gewalt steht), those whose opposite is placed simply beyond someone's power are to him merely natural. An action free with respect to execution, to whose opposite there is an equal physical possibility with respect to a certain agent, is physically indifferent to him (gleich leicht und schwer ist) (indifferent as to the exercise of the act).",["The German equivalents in the first sentence form one clause split across the two Latin phrases (\"Handlungen stehn ... nicht in meiner Gewalt\"); they are given as printed.","\"secundum quid\": \"in a certain respect\"; \"simpliciter\": \"simply\". \"supra potestatem\" (\"beyond\") differs from \"extra potestatem\" (\"outside\").","\"ratione exsecutionis\": \"with respect to execution\"; \"qua exercitium actus\": \"as to the exercise of the act\"."]],"met:709":["Merely natural actions are naturally necessary. Whence actions free with respect to execution, contradistinguished from these, are physically contingent, §. 708, 469. Hence future ones are sometimes called contingent futures (das Zufällig-künftige) simply.",["\"simpliciter\" may go with \"futura contingentia\" (contingent futures simply) or with \"vocantur\" (simply called)."]],"met:710":["Internal necessitation (die innre Nöthigung) (coercion) is that which depends on an internal determination of the necessitated substance, and is attributed to substances and their actions, 1) when the actions of substances are thought to be necessitated by their essence alone, absolute (die wesentliche) (essential). Necessitation of this kind, since it would change actions into absolutely necessary ones, §. 702, 107, is altogether impossible, §. 702. 2) when through the nature of a substance certain actions are changed from ones otherwise physically contingent into ones physically necessary to that nature, either simply or in a certain respect such, §. 469, physical (natural). Hence merely natural actions can be said to be necessitated by internal physical coercion, §. 709, 708. Actions and substances internally coerced in neither of these significations are free (cf. §. 719) from internal necessitation both absolute and physical (frei von der innern so wohl wesentlichen als natürlichen Nöthigung). Now, however, actions free with respect to execution, and substances which, and insofar as they, perform actions of this kind, are in neither of these significations internally coerced, §. 709, 708, therefore they are free from internal coercion both absolute and physical.",["\"determinatio\": \"determination\"; \"essentia\": \"essence\".","\"die wesentliche\" (essential) stands as the German for \"absoluta\"."]],"met:711":["Many of my actions, many spontaneous actions of my soul are free with respect to execution, §. 708. Therefore to both them and the soul, insofar as it performs them, belongs freedom from external coercion simply such, §. 707, and from internal, both absolute and physical, §. 710.",["\"spontaneae\" may be attributive (\"spontaneous actions\") or predicative (\"being spontaneous\")."]],"met:712":["Liking (das Belieben) is the cognition which a substance possesses, from which it can be cognized according to the laws of desire and aversion why it determines itself thus and not otherwise with regard to an action free with respect to execution. But this can be cognized from foresight, presentiment, pleasure or displeasure, §. 665, stimuli and motives, §. 677, 690. Therefore foresight, presentiment, pleasure or displeasure, stimuli and motives, which are cognized by a certain substance, constitute its liking. If a substance with regard to an action free with respect to execution so determines its force as can be cognized from its liking, it desires or is averse according to its liking (nach Belieben begehren oder abgeneigt sein). He, therefore, who would desire, or be averse to, either what is not foreseen, or that which he had not at all presaged would come to exist through any effort of his, or what is neither pleasing nor displeasing, without any stimuli, any motives, would not desire, would not be averse according to his liking. Many things I desire, to many things I am averse according to my liking. Therefore I have the faculty of desiring and of being averse according to my liking, i.e. choice (Willkür). Actions which it is placed in the power of some substance to determine through choice are to it arbitrary (willkürlich). Many of my actions are arbitrary.",["\"lubitus\": \"liking\"; \"pro lubitu\": \"according to liking\". \"arbitrium\": \"choice\"; \"arbitrarius\": \"arbitrary\".","The source reads \"pro lubito meo\" once, beside \"lubitu\" elsewhere; probably a misprint or transcription slip.","\"appetitio\" / \"aversatio\": \"desire\" / \"aversion\"; \"taedium\": \"displeasure\"; \"praesagium\": \"presentiment\"."]],"met:713":["I gladly desire or am averse (ich begehre oder verwerfe gern) 1) to whatever I desire or am averse to according to liking, and then I desire nothing, am averse to nothing not gladly or unwillingly (ungern), §. 712, 665, 2) if the liking contains either mere displeasure, or pure pleasure, or an enormous preponderance: then I desire or am averse unwillingly (not gladly, against liking), where there is not a very great preponderance in the liking: or, where many and great things also seem to impel toward the opposite of that which I desire or am averse to. Unwilling in the last signification, I nevertheless desire or am averse according to liking, and in that signification an unwilling action is nevertheless arbitrary, §. 712.",["\"lubenter\" / \"illubenter\": \"gladly\" / \"not gladly\"; \"invitus\": \"unwilling(ly)\"; the German gives \"gern\" / \"ungern\".","\"superpondium\": \"preponderance\", literally \"overweight\".","\"merum taedium\" (mere displeasure) under a condition for gladly desiring is as printed."]],"met:714":["Since coercion in the strict signification (Zwang in engerer Bedeutung), cf. §. 701, is the production of an unwilling action: an action coerced or unwilling through external coercion simply taken (eine von aussen schlechterdings erzwungene Handlung) would be one which I would perform according to no liking, contrary to all liking, through external coercion simply taken, but this would not be an action properly so called, §. 707. If I do something unwillingly according to liking from §. 713, the preponderance of that which I desire or am averse to is either considered as produced by me, and I am said to have coerced myself (ich zwinge mich selbst zu etwas), or as by another outside me, §. 22, and the action is called unwilling or coerced through external coercion in a certain respect (eine von aussen gewisser Maassen erzwungene Handlung) (mixed, namely of arbitrary, and unwilling through external coercion simply taken).",["Here \"coactio\" has the German \"Zwang\" rather than \"Nöthigung\"; both are \"coercion\" in the English.","\"simpliciter sumpta\": \"simply taken\"; \"secundum quid\": \"in a certain respect\" (German \"gewisser Maassen\")."]],"met:715":["Unwilling actions, to which I am said to coerce myself, or to be externally coerced in a certain respect by others, happen according to my liking, §. 714, hence are arbitrary, §. 712, and are called necessitated insofar as they are conceived as less necessary if either I myself or other things had not produced that which produced them, the preponderance, §. 701, 188.",["\"ut minus necessariae\" may also be read \"as less necessary\" or \"as being less necessary\"; \"alia\" is neuter plural, \"other things\"."]],"met:716":["Unwilling actions through ignorance or error (aus Unwissenheit oder Irrthum ungern vorgenommene Handlungen) are called those which I would not have performed according to liking unless I had been ignorant of something or had erred; which, since they nevertheless happen according to liking, are also arbitrary, §. 712.",[]],"met:717":["Choice would be least which, according to the least liking, determined only a single action, §. 161. Therefore the greater the liking according to which, the more, the greater the actions it determines, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is greatest, determining the greatest, most actions from the greatest liking, §. 161, 712.",[]],"met:718":["Since choice is the faculty of desiring or being averse according to its own liking, §. 712, a substance endowed with choice will either have only the faculty of desiring or being averse sensitively, or only of willing or nilling according to its liking, or the faculty of willing or nilling and of sensitively desiring or being averse according to its own liking, §. 676, 689.",["\"volendi nolendive\": \"of willing or nilling\" (velle / nolle)."]],"met:719":["The faculty of desiring or averting sensitively at one's own pleasure is sensitive choice (das sinnliche Willkür); the faculty of willing or nilling at one's own pleasure is (free choice) freedom (Freiheit), cf. §. 707, 708, 710 (moral, so called without qualification). The freedom of purely willing or nilling is pure freedom (reine Freiheit). Therefore a substance endowed with choice will have either only that sensitive choice, or only pure freedom, or the same mixed with sensitive choice, §. 718. Actions to which it is placed in the power of some substance to determine itself through freedom are free (freie Handlungen), and the substance itself, which and insofar as it can perform free actions, is free (Dinge, oder Kräfte, die frei vor sich bestehn).",["“choice” renders arbitrium throughout, “pleasure” lubitus (pro lubitu suo: at one's own pleasure).","“nilling” renders nolle (volendi nolendive: of willing or nilling), as “nolition” renders nolitio below.","“moral, so called without qualification”: moralis, simpliciter sic dicta."]],"met:720":["I desire and avert many things sensitively at my pleasure. Therefore I have sensitive choice, §. 216, 719. I will and nill many things at my pleasure. Therefore I have freedom, §. 216, 719. Many of my actions, many actions of my soul, and the soul in many of its actions, are free. To all my volitions and nolitions something sensitive is admixed, §. 692. Hence pure freedom does not belong to me; in my freest actions my freedom is mixed with sensitive choice, §. 719. Sensitive choice as well as free are actualized through the power of the soul representative of the universe according to the position of my body in the same, §. 712, 667.",["“power ... representative of the universe”: vis animae repraesentativa universi; “position”: positus."]],"met:721":["Voluntary actions (Handlungen des Willens, die mit Willen geschehn) are called 1) whatever ones are determined through the higher appetitive faculty; then involuntary (die nicht des Willens sind, oder nicht mit Willen geschehn) are those which are not determined through the higher appetitive faculty. And in this signification all voluntary actions are free, not all free actions are voluntary, §. 719. For suppose that I determine myself through sensitive choice where to determine myself through freedom was placed in my power: an action of this kind will be involuntary, yet free, §. 719.",[]],"met:722":["Voluntary actions (freiwillige Handlungen) are called 2) whatever ones are determined through the higher appetitive faculty by one in no way unwilling. Then involuntary (Handlungen, die ich ungern will) are those which I will unwillingly. In this signification voluntary and involuntary actions are all free actions, §. 719. But not all free actions are either voluntary or involuntary in this signification, §. 721.",["“by one in no way unwilling”: nullo modo inviti; invitus, “unwilling”, is “against one's will”."]],"met:723":["More closely connected with freedom is the moral broadly so called (sittlich in weiterer Bedeutung), cf. §. 787. Hence free determinations are moral (sittliche Bestimmungen), habits of free actions moral (Fertigkeiten), laws of moral determinations moral (Gesetze), the philosophy and theology teaching them moral, the state resulting from them moral (der sittliche Zustand). Hence morally possible is 1) that which cannot come about except through freedom, or in a free substance as such, more broadly (sittlich möglich in weiterer), 2) that which cannot come about except through freedom determined in conformity with moral laws, more strictly, or licit (in engerer Bedeutung, erlaubt). Morally impossible is 1) that which on account of freedom alone cannot come about in a free substance, more broadly (sittlich unmöglich in weiterer), 2) what is impossible through freedom to be determined in conformity with moral laws, more strictly or illicit (in engerer Bedeutung, unerlaubt). Morally necessary is that whose opposite is morally impossible, therefore 1) that whose opposite is not, except through freedom, or in a substance insofar as it is free, impossible, more broadly (sittlich nothwendig in weiterer), 2) that whose opposite is illicit, more strictly (in engerer Bedeutung). Moral necessitation is obligation (Verbindung, Verpflichtung). Obligation to an unwilling action will be moral coercion (sittlicher Zwang).",["The German equivalents “in weiterer” break off without “Bedeutung”, as printed.","“or” renders s. (seu) throughout; “unwilling action”: actio invita.","“determinandam” in 2) of the morally impossible is a gerundive: “to be determined”."]],"met:724":["Moral necessity in neither signification can be where there is not freedom, §. 723, therefore it does not remove freedom, nor is it its opposite, §. 81, but a consequence or corollary, §. 14. Hence actions morally necessary, necessitated, and coerced not only can be free, but also are such necessarily, §. 723. Indeed, once the most universal moral law (das allgemeinste sittliche Gesetze) is posited, i.e. one determining all the free actions of all free substances, cf. §. 822, all free actions are morally necessary, or illicit, §. 723.",["“consequence or corollary”: rationatum seu consectarium; rationatum is what follows from a ground (ratio)."]],"met:725":["The least freedom is that actualizing only a single volition or nolition at the least pleasure; the more, therefore, the greater ones, and at the greater pleasure it actualizes, the greater it is, until it is the greatest, actualizing the greatest and most numerous volitions or nolitions at the highest pleasure, §. 719. The more distinct, therefore, the pleasure at which I will or nill something, the more freely I will or nill it. Therefore the more I am conscious of my motives, the more freely I will, §. 712. That one willing or nilling most freely should discern most distinctly the motives for willing or nilling, whenever he wills or does not will, is morally necessary, §. 723.",["“discern”: perspiciat (see through)."]],"met:726":["This is the law of choice: Out of things free with respect to execution, what pleases I desire, what pleases I avert. Hence the rule of freedom: Out of things free with respect to execution, what pleases I will, what pleases I nill. My free actions, while they are determined at pleasure, are not necessitated through their impelling causes, stimuli or motives, by physical coercion, not external, §. 707, for stimuli and motives are my representations, §. 677, 690. Hence my internal determinations, §. 37. Nor are my free actions necessitated through their impelling causes by internal physical coercion, while they are and remain, once motives are posited, free with respect to execution, §. 711, 710. Indeed, motives and stimuli, speaking accurately, do not even act on the soul, hence neither do they coerce it, §. 714, nor necessitate it morally, §. 723, 701, while they are nothing but accidents of my soul, §. 505.",["“what pleases”: quod libet, which can also be read “whatever I please”.","“while” renders dum each time, which can also mean “since”.","“by physical coercion, not external”: coactione physica, non externa, as the Latin punctuates it."]],"met:727":["If I am said to coerce myself, it is placed in my power to determine myself through freedom to that to which I coerce myself, whence I coerce myself freely, §. 714, 719. If I am coerced by another externally in a certain respect, I am determined at a pleasure which is conceived as produced by another placed outside me, §. 714. But if it was then placed in my power to determine myself through freedom to that to which I am coerced externally in a certain respect, an action of this kind is nevertheless free, §. 719. Many of my actions, to which I coerce myself, or am coerced externally in a certain respect, are free.",["“in a certain respect”: secundum quid, as opposed to simpliciter, “without qualification”."]],"met:728":["External coercions in a certain respect will be l) productions of stimuli from things pleasing, allurements (Reizungen, locken), 2) from things displeasing, threats (Drohen), 3) of motives from things pleasing, persuasions (Anrathen), 4) from things displeasing, dissuasions (Abrathen), 5) the very actualization of things displeasing, to be continued until the one coercing can be certain of the preponderance having ensued toward the unwilling action, extortion (die Erpressung), §. 714. Many of my actions, although I am coerced to them by threats and allurements, persuasions and dissuasions, and by extortions themselves, are nevertheless free, §. 727.",["The enumerator “l)” is kept as printed; it is presumably an OCR reading of “1)”.","“preponderance”: superpondium."]],"met:729":["Through the system of preestablished harmony all unwilling actions of the soul to be produced by any finite thing through external coercion taken without qualification are removed, §. 714, 449, and in those coerced through external force in a certain respect that very pleasure from which they arise, as really produced by another finite thing outside the soul, is removed, §. 727, 449, and insofar as through this system the soul, as it never does, so in none of its free actions suffers really from any finite thing, in this sense total independence from the whole and entire world is ascribed to the soul, §. 354, 307.",["“taken without qualification”: simpliciter sumptam; “suffers”: patitur (is acted upon).","“from the whole and entire world”: ab omni totoque mundo."]],"met:730":["My free volitions and nolitions are called elicited acts of the soul, the free actions of the remaining faculties commanded acts, and insofar as they depend on the freedom of the soul, command over them is ascribed to it. Hence the command of the soul over itself (die Herrschaft der Seele über sich selbst) is the faculty of producing at distinct pleasure now the actions of this, now of that faculty, now of producing their opposite. The greater the freedom, therefore, the greater is the command of the free over itself, §. 725. A notable defect of command over oneself is moral servitude in the broad signification (die innre Knechtschaft der Seelen überhaupt). What makes for increasing command over oneself is (ingenuous) liberal (frei, dem knechtischen entgegen gesetzt), what promotes moral servitude is servile (knechtisch).",["“elicited acts”, “commanded acts”: actus eliciti, actus imperati; “command”: imperium.","“to it” (ipsi) can refer to the soul or to its freedom.","“ingenuous”: ingenuum, in the sense “freeborn”."]],"met:731":["If actions of the soul depend on freedom not except through several intermediate acts, they are subject to it indirectly (mediately) (Handlungen, die der Freiheit mittelbar), but directly (die ihr unmittelbar unterworfen) (immediately) those which I perform or omit through freedom without several other observable intermediate acts. Actions which are subject to freedom even most of all indirectly are nevertheless free, §. 719.",["The German equivalent “Handlungen, die der Freiheit mittelbar” lacks its verb (unterworfen), as printed.","“even most of all indirectly”: vel maxime per indirectum."]],"met:732":["The determinate proportion of the appetitive faculties among themselves in a certain subject is its disposition (die Gemüths-Art), either elevated (eine edle), the higher ones habitually dominating, or abject (eine niedrige), the lower ones habitually dominating, of which the greatest is called the dominant passion (der Hang). And since a certain proportion of them is more easily borne toward a certain genus of things desirable or aversable, various genera of temperaments of the soul (Mischung der Gemüths-Neigungen) are hence constituted. Therefore the temperament of the soul can be changed much, and rather often by exercises and custom, §. 650, 577.",["“disposition”: indoles.","“of which the greatest” (quarum maxima) can refer to the lower faculties or to the faculties generally."]],"met:733":["Many motions of my body depend on my choice, §. 14. Motions of the body depending on a choice of which I am conscious are called arbitrary (willkürliche), §. 712, and arbitrary ones depending on the higher faculty are voluntary (freiwillige Bewegungen des Leibes), §. 721. The government of the soul over the body (die Herrschaft der Seelen über ihren Leib) is the dependence of the motions of the latter on the choice of the former. Hence my soul has government over my body.",["“arbitrary”: arbitrarius, from arbitrium, “choice”; “government”: regimen."]],"met:734":["In arbitrary and voluntary motions of my body it can be sufficiently cognized from the soul why an event occurs in the body, at this and not at another time and place, §. 733. Therefore the soul acts on the body, §. 210, and flows into it, §. 211.",["“flows into”: influit; the noun influxus is rendered “influence”."]],"met:735":["In affects of the soul there coexists in the body a motion conformable to them, which, since it can be sufficiently cognized from the same, gives a new argument for the influence of the soul on the body, §. 734.",[]],"met:736":["In external sensations it can be sufficiently cognized from the power of the body why a certain change happens in the soul. Therefore the body acts on the soul, §. 210, and flows into it, §. 211. Therefore there is between my soul and my body a mutual influence, §. 734, 735, a mutual harmony, §. 14, and commerce, §. 448.",["“power”: vis; “commerce”: commercium."]],"met:737":["That the arbitrary motions of the body are sufficiently determined through the power proper to the body, and that sensations are sufficiently determined through the power proper to the soul, I do not experience. He who therefore assumes this major premise: ›whatever I do not experience does not exist‹, will thence conclude: ›Therefore the arbitrary motions of the body are not sufficiently determined through the power proper to the body, and external sensations through the power proper to the soul. Hence the body really flows into the soul, the soul into the body, §. 212, by physical influence, §. 450 (by exp.).‹ If he takes this conclusion for a sensation, thence arise the fallacies of his senses, a false major premise having been assumed, §. 548, through the vice of subreption, §. 546.",["“(by exp.)”: per exp., abbreviating per experientiam, “by experience”.","“fallacies of his senses”: fallaciae sensuum ipsius."]],"met:738":["Changes of the body depending on the soul, and of the soul depending on the body, are harmonic, §. 448. In harmonic changes of the soul and body a change of the soul coexists with, or succeeds, a change of the body, a change of the body coexists with, or succeeds, a change of the soul, §. 733-736. He who therefore assumes this major premise: ›Of things that coexist with or succeed one another, one really flows into the other‹, will hence conclude: ›therefore the body really flows into the soul in harmonic changes, the soul into the body (by exp.)‹. If he takes this conclusion for a sensation, hence arise the fallacies of his senses, §. 548, through the vice of subreption, §. 546.",["“(by exp.)”: per exp., abbreviating per experientiam.","“in harmonic changes” stands after the first clause in the Latin and may govern both."]],"met:739":["My soul and my body constitute me as one. Therefore they are united with each other, §. 73, 79. Their commerce, insofar as through it one human being endures, is union, §. 205, which, insofar as it is very great, §. 734-736, and as great as there is not between my soul and any other body, §. 508, is the closest.",["“the closest”: artissima (tightest)."]],"met:740":["A human soul (eine menschliche Seele) is a soul which is in the closest commerce with a human body. And since a soul with the body with which it is in the closest commerce constitutes an animal (ein Thier): a human soul with the body with which it is in the closest commerce constitutes an animal which we call a human being (einen Menschen).",[]],"met:741":["The human soul represents its body to itself at choice, §. 740. Therefore it acts, §. 210, it also moves its body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it desires and averts, §. 712. Therefore it acts and is a power representative of its body, §. 210. The human body is matter, §. 296, hence divisible, §. 427, and so internally mutable, §. 244, and so a finite being, §. 255, actual, a part of the world, §. 354. From the position of the human body in the universe it can be cognized why the human soul represents obscurely, clearly, distinctly these things, not others, §. 740, 736. Therefore the human soul (die menschliche Seele) is a power representative of the universe according to the position of the human body in the same, §. 513, 155.",["“at choice”: pro arbitrio."]],"met:742":["To thought is required 1) the perception of a thing, 2) of marks sufficient for distinguishing it, §. 524, 3) its distinction itself, §. 67. Thought, however, is an accident, §. 191. Therefore it cannot exist except in a substance, or an aggregate of substances, §. 194. An aggregate of substances, from whose parts' changes or accidents taken together, of which none would yet be a thought, a thought would at last result in the whole, would be thinking matter. This would in the world be an aggregate of finite substances, §. 354, of which one would contribute more to thinking than the rest, §. 272, from which it would suffer aids, §. 210, 321. But this will suffer only ideally, §. 451, 463. Therefore in the world, in all thinking matter, there would be one substance in whose single force all the requisites for thought that are to be furnished by finite things would have their sufficient ground, §. 210, 212, and yet 1) this substance dominant over the given aggregate would never think through itself, which is contrary to §. 30, 220, 2) all the requisites for thought, to be furnished by finite things, would have their sufficient ground in the given dominant substance, by what has been demonstrated, and at the same time would not be actualized except at last through an aggregate of substances, i.e. would not have their sufficient ground except in the force of several finite substances, §. 210, q.a., §. 7. Thinking matter is impossible in the world. Whatever can think is either a substance, a monad, §. 234, or a whole of which a substance that can think is a part. Therefore every soul is a substance, a monad, §. 504. Whatever can understand can think, §. 69. Therefore whatever can understand is either a substance, a monad, a spirit, §. 402, or a whole of which a spirit is a part. A whole of spirits is (a moral person) a mystical body. An intellectual soul is a spirit, §. 504, cf. §. 402, 296. The human soul is a substance, §. 740. Therefore a monad, a spirit, §. 741.",["'q.a.' is left as printed: the abbreviation of quod absurdum, 'which is absurd'.","'thought' renders cogitatio; 'ground' renders ratio (ratio sufficiens, 'sufficient ground')."]],"met:743":["The human soul subsists through itself, §. 742, 192. Therefore it is not a substantiated phenomenon, §. 193. Since, however, it represents according to the position of the human body in the universe, which is in perpetual motion, §. 417, 296, hence always changes its position, §. 283, 281, its representations are always changed, §. 512. Since these are internal determinations of the human soul, §. 37, 741, the human soul is an internally mutable substance, hence contingent, §. 202, and finite, §. 255. That every human soul is a finite and contingent substance is also evident thus: the human soul represents according to the position of the body, §. 741. Therefore it perceives some things distinctly, some less distinctly, §. 512. But now to conceive something distinctly is a reality, §. 515, 531. Therefore the human soul has a degree of reality that is not the greatest, §. 161, hence a limit, §. 248. Therefore it is finite and contingent, §. 257, a substance, §. 742.",["'according to the position' renders pro positu, as throughout.","The relative 'which is in perpetual motion' can attach to the body or to the universe, and the subject of 'changes its position' is likewise unexpressed in the Latin."]],"met:744":["The human soul cognizes, desires, and is averse, §. 741. These are partially diverse actions, §. 267. Therefore the human soul has partially diverse faculties, §. 216, which are not called forces in the strict sense, §. 197, 59, are rather conceived through the single representative force of the soul, strictly so called, §. 521-720, and are not posited outside one another, since accidents, §. 191, are not outside their substance, §. 194, nor are they accurately said to act upon one another, since action is not properly except of a substance, §. 210, much less to influence one another, §. 211.",["'desires' and 'is averse' render appetit and aversatur; 'faculties' facultates, 'forces' vires."]],"met:745":["The human soul, a monad, §. 742, contingent, §. 743, cannot arise except out of nothing, §. 236, cannot perish except through annihilation, §. 237, is not extended, nor does it fill space, §. 241, i.e. it is not in a place repletively, §. 241. Coexisting, however, with simultaneous things posited outside it, it is in space, §. 239, and in a place, §. 281, so that some things posited outside it are nearer to it, others more remote, §. 288, 282. If the complex of the simultaneous things nearer to the human soul be called its seat (der Sitz der menschlichen Seele): the human body before other bodies, and certain of its parts before the other members, are the seat of the soul, §. 409.",["'repletively' renders the scholastic adverb repletive (being in a place by filling it)."]],"met:746":["The human soul does not have quantitative magnitude, §. 744, 243, and is indivisible, §. 244. Perishing through division is physical corruption (das Zergehn). Therefore physical corruption of the human soul is in itself impossible, §. 15, 745, i.e. the human soul is absolutely physically incorruptible (unzergänglich, unauflöslich, unverweslich, unzerstörbar).",["'perishing' renders interitus; 'physical corruption' corruptio physica."]],"met:747":["The human soul does not indeed admit the threefold dimension of extended things, §. 290, 745, yet a philosophical and mathematical cognition of it is possible, as of the human body, §. 743, 249. The human being consists of a finite soul and body, §. 741, 743, hence, internally mutable, §. 740, is a finite and contingent being, §. 202, 257. Therefore a philosophical and mathematical cognition of the human being is possible, §. 249, i.e. philosophical anthropology, and mathematical, or anthropometry, as well as empirical, through experience. The complex of rules to be observed in cognizing the human being is anthropognosy.",["'human being' renders homo; 'or' renders s. (seu).","'finitis' may qualify both soul and body or body alone; the English keeps the same order."]],"met:748":["A finite being changed into an infinite one, an infinite one would arise, §. 125, 227, nor would it be eternal, §. 302, which is absolutely impossible, §. 252. The change of a human being into an infinite being is apotheosis (Vergötterung), therefore apotheosis is absolutely impossible.",["The opening is an ablative absolute (Mutato ente finito in infinitum), rendered literally."]],"met:749":["Total similarity and equality of human souls, or of only two, is impossible, §. 271, 272. Therefore total agreement or identity of the senses, imaginations, previsions, judgments, tastes, distastes, pleasures, stimuli, motives, errors, appetitions, aversions, volitions, nolitions in several human beings, or in very few, is a chimera, §. 590.",["'senses' renders sensuum, which may also be taken as 'sensations'; 'distastes' renders taediorum, 'pleasures' voluptatum."]],"met:750":["The human soul moves its body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it has a faculty of moving something posited outside itself, i.e. a locomotive faculty, §. 216, which, like the remaining faculties of the soul, §. 744, is actualized through its representative force of the universe according to the position of the body, §. 741, 417.",[]],"met:751":["The human soul represents to itself certain parts of this universe as singular, §. 740, 736, therefore as determined in every way, §. 148. To the determination in every way of the parts of this world belongs their nexus with each of the rest, §. 357. Therefore the human soul represents to itself certain things as connected with each of the parts of the world. But now one thing cannot be cognized as connected with another unless the other is cognized in some way or other, §. 14. Therefore the human soul perceives each of the parts of this universe, therefore it also perceives each of the states of this universe, §. 369.",["'determined in every way' and 'determination in every way' render omnimode determinatas and omnimodam determinationem.","'each of' renders singuli, 'the single ones' severally."]],"met:752":["The human soul, according to the position of its body, represents to itself the present state of the world, i.e. it senses, §. 534, 751, the past, i.e. it imagines, §. 557, the future, i.e. it foresees, §. 595. The sensations of the human soul are representations of the single parts of the world simultaneous with it, less or more distinct, confused, or obscure, according as their objects are related to the human body, §. 751. The imaginations of the human soul are representations of the single past parts of the world which preceded the sensing soul, or existed before that act of sensation; and these imaginations too are distinct, confused, obscure, more or less, according as their objects were related to the human body, §. 751. The previsions of the human soul are representations of the single future parts of the world, which will exist after the act of the sensing soul, less or more distinct, confused, obscure, according as their objects will be related to the human body, §. 751.",["'simultaneous with it' renders sibi simultanearum; sibi may refer to the soul or to the parts themselves ('simultaneous with one another').","'foresees' and 'previsions' render praevidet and praevisiones."]],"met:753":["In the same way in which §. 752 proved concerning sensation etc. that it is in every human soul, this can be shown concerning the remaining acts of the soul that are to be detected in the human soul through experience, enumerated by empirical psychology, §. 576 ff.",["'etc.' renders e.c.; 'ff.' renders seqqu."]],"met:754":["The human soul represents distinctly according to the position of its body, §. 741. Therefore it understands, §. 402, and is a spirit, §. 742, 216. Hence it is again evident that all human souls are unequal and one the most perfect of all, §. 405. The human soul is a finite spirit, §. 743, a part of this world, §. 355.",["'understands' renders intelligit."]],"met:755":["Every spirit is a substance, §. 402. Therefore a force, §. 199, hence the sufficient ground of the inherence of certain accidents, §. 197, and so acting, §. 210. And since it is an intellectual substance, §. 402, it has the faculty of actualizing certain distinct representations, §. 216. Therefore of determining its force toward them, §. 210, hence of desiring, §. 663. Whatever it desires, it is averse to the opposite of it, hence a spirit has the faculty of being averse, §. 663. The appetitions and aversions of a spirit are its internal determinations, §. 37. Therefore they are in universal nexus with its remaining internal determinations, §. 49. Therefore also with its remaining cognition, which is an internal determination, §. 37, and so they occur according to the spirit's pleasure, §. 712. Therefore a spirit has choice, §. 712, and since its pleasure can be distinct, §. 402, free choice, or freedom, §. 719.",["The Latin Quicquid appetit, eius oppositum aversatur expresses no subject; it can also be read 'whatever desires is averse to its opposite'.","'pleasure' renders lubitus; 'choice' arbitrium, 'free choice' liberum (sc. arbitrium)."]],"met:756":["The human soul is a spirit, §. 754. Therefore it has freedom, §. 755. And since spirituality, intellectuality, personality, §. 641, 754, freedom, absolute simplicity, §. 744, and incorruptibility belong to it with absolute necessity, §. 746, they are not its modes, §. 108, hence either essentials or attributes, because they are internal determinations, §. 37, 52. One of them being removed, the human soul would be removed, §. 63. Hence a human soul which could not at all conceive something distinctly, nor determine itself from a distinct pleasure, losing all personality and freedom, consisting of several forces as parts to be posited outside one another, and to be corrupted physically, are chimeras, §. 590.",["The plural 'are chimeras' (sunt chimaerae) after a singular subject is the Latin's own, and is kept."]],"met:757":["The human soul is immaterial and incorporeal, §. 744, 422. Whoever denies that the human soul is an immaterial substance is a materialist in the psychological signification, cf. §. 395, and is deceived, whether he takes it for a mere accident of the body, §. 742, or for a material atom, §. 429, or for any most subtle corpuscle whatever, §. 426. A universal materialist is also such in the psychological signification, §. 395, but not everyone who thinks the human soul a material substance is necessarily a universal materialist, §. 395.",[]],"met:758":["The representative force of the universe according to the position of the human body in it, with its modes depending on this, is the complex of internal determinations of the soul which are the principles of its changes and of the accidents inhering in it, §. 751-755. Therefore the same force with its determinations depending on the position of the body is the nature of the human soul, §. 430. Hence whatever is actualized in the human soul through this force, determined according to the laws and rules of the single faculties, is natural to the human soul, §. 470, insofar as it is contradistinguished from the supernatural, §. 469. In this signification even the free and moral actions of the soul are natural, §. 756, 755, although they are not merely natural, §. 709. Besides this proper signification of nature in the human soul, and hence of natural in the same, there are other, improper and synecdochic ones, arisen badly now from a confusion of the part with the whole, now from this, that a particular name has become attached to a particular mode, modification, or state of nature conceivable in the human soul, and then, the remaining natural things being destitute of a particular name, if they had to be contradistinguished from those that had acquired such a one, the name of the genus remained in a stricter signification. Thus e.g. connate things are sometimes called natural, contradistinguished from acquired ones; natural things are contradistinguished from artificial, social, arbitrary ones, obtained only through custom, etc., cf. §. 710.",["'determined' renders determinatam, which agrees with vim ('force'), not with the laws and rules.","'etc.' renders e.c."]],"met:759":["The natural things of the human soul which succeed one another in the human soul according to laws prescribed by its force occur, with respect to the human soul, according to the course of nature, §. 758, 471, and together with those which coexist in the human soul according to the same laws, happen according to the order of nature with respect to the human soul, §. 473. Preternatural things, which are not actualized through its representative force according to the position of its body, according to the laws and rules of its faculties, which, if they are not actualized through universal nature either, are supernatural, §. 474.",["vi ipsius praescriptas may be read 'prescribed by its force' or 'prescribed to its force'.","The last sentence has no main verb for Praeternaturalia in the Latin; it is left elliptical."]],"met:760":["The human soul is in the universal pneumatic, and greatest pneumatic-mechanical, nexus of the best world, §. 464, 754. Therefore it is connected with each of the spirits and bodies of the best world, §. 48, but unequally, §. 272. Therefore with one body, with one spirit most of all, §. 161.",["maximo may qualify only pneumatico-mechanico or the whole nexus; 'most of all' renders maxime."]],"met:761":["Psychological systems are opinions which seem apt for explaining the commerce of soul and body in the human being. Therefore psychological systems are particular systems, §. 462, either simple or composite, §. 457. Of those none is possible except the system of pre-established harmony, of physical influx, and of occasional causes, perhaps, psychological, §. 458.",["'opinions' renders sententiae; 'commerce' commercium.","forsitan psychologicum ('perhaps ... psychological') may attach to the system of occasional causes alone or to the whole list."]],"met:762":["One of the general simple systems being posited, one of the psychological simple ones is also posited, §. 761, 457. Therefore universal pre-established harmony having been demonstrated, §. 463, psychological pre-established harmony has at the same time been demonstrated. One of the psychological simple systems being posited, however, it is not necessary that one, or the most similar, of the general ones be posited, §. 761, 457. If the soul and human body can be in the closest commerce through pre-established harmony, they are also connected through pre-established harmony in the best world, §. 461, so that physical influx or the system of occasional causes are not to be admitted, except where commerce through pre-established harmony is impossible, §. 462.",["The source reads ponendam est, an irregular form for ponendum est ('is to be posited').","humanum may qualify the body alone or soul and body together."]],"met:763":["The psychological system of physical influence, because it is simple, posits that all harmonious changes of all human souls and bodies occur through physical influence, §. 457. Therefore if even a single harmonious change of one soul or human body can be proven possible through pre-established harmony in the best world: it can no longer be defended that the system of physical influence has a place in the best world, §. 762. The psychological system of pre-established harmony, because it is simple, posits that all harmonious changes of all human souls and bodies occur through pre-established harmony, the psychological system of occasional causes for the same reason posits that the same occur through the infinite force alone, §. 457. Therefore if even one harmonious change of one soul or human body were impossible in the best world through pre-established harmony: it could no longer be defended that its psychological system has a place in the best world. If even one harmonious change of one soul or human body is possible in the best world through pre-established harmony or physical influence: neither can it any longer be defended that the system of occasional causes has a place in the best world, §. 762, 460. The psychological system of physical influence is no more evident through experience than the system of pre-established harmony and of occasional causes, §. 737, 738.",["\"Physical influence\" renders influxus physicus; \"force\" renders vis throughout."]],"met:764":["The psychological system of physical influence posits that the human soul really flows into its body, the human body into its soul, in harmonious changes, §. 761, 450. Hence through the psychological system of physical influence 1) in no harmonious changes occurring in the human body does the body act by its own force, §. 212. But now all changes of the human body, as a part of the world which the soul represents, can be sufficiently cognized from the force of the soul, §. 354, 751. Therefore all are harmonious, §. 448, and in none of its changes does the body act, according to the psychological system of physical influence, by its own force, in all really suffering from the soul. If the body never acts, neither does it react, §. 213. Therefore through the system of physical influence there is in the world a transeunt action of the soul upon the human body without reaction, as often as the soul acts upon its body, which, even once, would happen contrary to §. 410.",["\"Suffering\" renders patiens (patior, to be acted upon).","\"In corpus suam\" is printed; read as suum (its body)."]],"met:765":["Through the psychological system of physical influence it is posited 2) that the human body really flows into its soul, as often as there occurs in the human soul a change which can be sufficiently cognized from the force of the body, §. 764, 448. Therefore in the harmonious changes met with in the soul, through the psychological system of physical influence, the body really flows in, which nevertheless through the same system must be posited never to act in any of its changes, §. 764. Through the system of physical influence the soul in sensing does not act at all, §. 212, although it nevertheless by its own force represents to itself the present state of the world no less than the remaining ones, §. 751.",[]],"met:766":["The human body consists of elements, §. 420, monads, representing their world, from each of which its individual parts can be cognized, §. 400, hence also the individual changes of the soul, parts of the world, §. 354. Therefore every action of the soul is a harmonious change, §. 448, 22. Now every volition and nolition of the soul is its action, §. 210, 690. Therefore every volition and nolition of the human soul is a harmonious change of the same. Now through the psychological system of physical influence the human soul in none of its harmonious changes acts by its own force, but really suffers from the body. Therefore through the system of physical influence the soul in all its volitions and nolitions, doing nothing whatsoever, suffers from the body, which is contrary to freedom, §. 755.",["\"Monads, representing their world\": suum may refer to the monads or to the body."]],"met:767":["Through the psychological system of occasional causes 1) the human body does not act in any of its harmonious changes, but the infinite being, §. 761, 452, whence in the same way in which it was shown in §. 764 it is evident that through the system of assistance the body acts in none of its changes, but only the infinite being. 2) Through the same system the human soul does not act in its harmonious changes, but the infinite being alone, §. 761, 452, whence in the same way in which it was shown in §. 766 it is evident that through the psychological system of assistance the human soul does anything in none of its volitions and nolitions, and only really suffers in them from the infinite being, which is likewise contrary to freedom, §. 755.",[]],"met:768":["Through the psychological system of pre-established harmony each part of the commerce actualizes the harmonious changes occurring in itself by its own force, ideally suffering from the other part, §. 761, 448, hence through the psychological system of pre-established harmony it is posited 1) that the voluntary motions of the body, no less than the merely natural and vital ones, are sufficiently determined through its mechanism and that of the bodies surrounding it, 2) that the sensations of the soul, no less than any of its freest thoughts, are sufficiently determined through its representative force, §. 758, 433.",["\"Commerce\" renders commercium; \"voluntary\" renders arbitrarius."]],"met:769":["Suppose that a psychological harmonist is going to give the ground of some harmonious change in a human being, and seeks it 1) from the force of that very part of the commerce in which the harmonious change is observed, 2) from the other part of the commerce, which he himself supposes to flow in ideally, 3) from the infinite force, which he holds to concur, suppose, I say, that he is mistaken, he has most certainly given the true ground, §. 448, 450, although he may have added a false one through hypothesis. Suppose that in the same case the influxionist or occasionalist is mistaken, he has not only passed over a great part of the true ground, but has also denied it, §. 450, 452.",["\"Ground\" renders ratio.","\"Ax altera parte\" is printed; read as ex.","\"He\" in the last sentence renders ille, which may refer to either of the two."]],"met:770":["While a human being is conceived, the soul that will be in the closest commerce with his body, such as human beings have on this earth, either already pre-existed, or begins to exist only in the conception itself, or shortly after it. One who posits the first is called a pre-existentianist (ein Freund des Vorherdaseins).",["\"On this earth\" renders in his terris (literally, in these lands)."]],"met:771":["Those who posit that the existence of the human soul only begins in the conception itself, or shortly after it, either want it to arise from the parents and are called traducianists (ein Freund des Ursprungs durch den Übergang), or admit that it then exists for the first time out of nothing, and are said to be inducianists (ein Freund der augenblicklichen Schöpfung) (infusianists, coexistentianists).",[]],"met:772":["One who posits that the human soul is actualized out of nothing is called a creatianist (ein Freund der unmittelbaren Schöpfung). Hence an inducianist is a creatianist, §. 771. A pre-existentianist can be a creatianist, §. 770. A traducianist is not a creatianist, and vice versa, §. 771. Traducianists, whether they derive the soul that is to arise from the soul of the parents, like a little flame from a little flame, or from their body, are bound to admit that the new soul arises either from a simple part of the parents, or from a composite one, §. 224. If the former: the soul does not arise, but pre-exists, §. 227; if the latter: the new soul will not be a monad, but a composite strictly so called, §. 225, which is contrary to §. 756, 757. The soul cannot arise except out of nothing, §. 745. Therefore it does not arise from the parents, §. 228.",["\"Creatianist\" renders creatianus, kept distinct from \"creationist\"."]],"met:773":["If the propagation of human souls by traduction (die Fortpflanzung menschlicher Seelen durch den Übergang) is said to be the union of ever other and other human souls with a body such as human beings have on this earth, through the continuous succession of parents and offspring in the world, by which the human race, such as it is on this earth, is propagated in such a way that the souls pass over with the bodies of the offspring and are transferred from the bodies of the parents into their own proper places, where they may dwell more separately, it can be conceived 1) such that it at the same time involves the arising of the soul, i.e. such that the soul, which, the act of conception intervening, is to come into the closest commerce with a body such as a human being has on this earth, is held to arise at the same time, and this again either from the parents, concerning which §. 772, or by the parents, assuming that God can put into the human soul a determinate force by which it could produce out of nothing only some individuals of its own species, his creative force concurring and supplying most of the realities in the soul that is to arise, only some quite limited realities proceeding from the soul of the parents. One who posits this view can be called a concreatianist (ein Freund der mitgetheilten Schöpfers-Kraft).",["\"By traduction\" renders per traducem (tradux, a vine layer or shoot).","\"From the parents\" and \"by the parents\" render ex parentibus and a parentibus.","\"Magis seorsim ubi habitent\" is obscure; rendered literally."]],"met:774":["The propagation of human souls by traduction can be conceived 2) such that it does not involve the arising of the soul, but the pre-existing soul is held to have already existed before the intervening act of conception in the closest commerce with a part of the seed, or more accurately with a spermatic animalcule, and so backward, through several generations, gradually increased and evolved, by the representative force of the soul itself, according as the body of the chosen spermatic animalcule, which was to go forth into a greater theater, has gradually been brought nearer and nearer to its great transformation and increases, §. 773.",[]],"met:775":["The arising of the soul and the propagation of the soul by traduction can be distinguished, §. 773, 771. Those also who posit the arising of the soul by traduction from the parents can be distinguished from those who posit that the same is to be derived by the parents, §. 773, 772. Therefore not everyone admitting traduction, in whatever way understood, is guilty of psychological materialism, §. 772, 757.",[]],"met:776":["As long as even a single smallest change natural to a being, or in general a single smallest accident, inheres in a being naturally, its nature endures, §. 469, 29, and the being lives, §. 430. But if all changes natural to a being, or in general all accidents naturally inhering in it, are ended or cease, its nature is ended, §. 469, 23, and the being dies, §. 430. Therefore the life of the human body (das Leben), or the duration of its nature, which it possesses as a human body, is continued while even a single smallest vital or voluntary motion survives, natural to it insofar as it is a human body, §. 733, 740. The death of the human body (der Tod des menschlichen Leibes), or the end of its nature, which it possesses as a human body, is the total cessation of vital and voluntary motions. Therefore the death defined in §. 556 is the death of the human body.",["\"Or\" renders s. (sive) throughout."]],"met:777":["Since an animal is a composite of a soul and a body with which the former is in the closest commerce, §. 740, its nature is composed of the nature of the body and of the soul placed in the closest commerce, §. 430. Hence while certain harmonious changes of a soul and body placed in the closest commerce exist, the animal lives (lebt). The cessation of all harmonious actions of a soul and body placed in the closest commerce is the death of the animal (stirbt das Thier). Now through the death of the body all harmonious changes of the soul and of the dying body placed in the closest commerce cease, §. 776, 448. Therefore the death of the body is also the death of the animal, the death of the human body the death of the human being, §. 740. Therefore the death defined in §. 556 is the death of the human being, §. 776.",["The German equivalents are verbal (lebt, stirbt das Thier) where the Latin has vivit animal and mors animalis."]],"met:778":["The death of an animal is either absolute (der schlechterdings), the cessation of all harmonious changes of the soul with any animal body whatever, or only relative (der nur verhältnissweise so genannte Tod des Thieres) (transformation and metamorphosis of the animal), the cessation of the harmonious changes of the soul with only some certain body, with which it had been in the closest commerce. The death of a human being is either absolute, or only relative, i.e. when the body dies, such as we experience human beings to have on this earth, either all closest commerce of the human soul with any body ceases, or it comes into a new commerce of this kind with another, §. 776, 777.",[]],"met:779":["Since daily certain parts of the human body cease to be in the closest commerce with the soul, certain ones come into it, it is evident in what sense a human being can be said to die daily, to revive daily, §. 777. The view by which the death of a human being is posited to be nothing but a transformation of the animal is called the exile of death (der aufgehobne Tod) (of absolute death).",["\"Exile\" renders exsilium; the German has der aufgehobne Tod (abolished death)."]],"met:780":["The life of the human soul (das Leben), or the duration of its nature, is continued while even a single smallest accident survives, natural to it insofar as it is a human soul, §. 776. But now, while the human soul endures, there is in it perception or representation of the state of the world and of itself, past, present and future, §. 742, 400, which according to the position of a certain body can be distinct, §. 741, an accident, §. 191, natural to it, §. 758. Therefore the human soul, while it exists, lives. The death of the human soul (der Tod der menschlichen Seele), or the end of its nature, is at the same time that of its existence. Now all existence, nature, life of the human soul is in itself contingent, §. 743, 430. Therefore the death of the human soul is in itself possible, §. 81, 104.",["\"Of the state of the world and of itself\": sui may also be read as of its own (state)."]],"met:781":["Mortal (sterblich) is what can die, and the possibility of dying is mortality (Sterblichkeit). What cannot die is immortal (unsterblich), and the impossibility of dying is immortality (Unsterblichkeit). Mortality and immortality are either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. Not only absolute but also a sufficiently great hypothetical mortality belongs to the human body and to the human being. §. 777, 168, to the soul none but absolute, and hypothetical as one to be annihilated, §. 745, 780. Absolute immortality indeed cannot be attributed to the soul, §. 780, yet because, indestructible, it cannot die in the six hundred ways in which the body can die, §. 746, a quite great hypothetical immortality befits it. No substance of this world, §. 354, 358, is annihilated, §. 227, 228. Therefore the human soul, when the body that human beings have on this earth dies, survives and lives immortally, §. 780, 742. The thnetopsychites (Freunde des Seelen-Todes), positing that the human soul dies with the body, err.",["\"Duale in his terris\" is printed; read as quale.","\"As one to be annihilated\" renders the gerundive annihilandae."]],"met:782":["The human soul after death (of the body and of the human being, such as we experience them on this earth) preserves spirituality, freedom, personality, §. 781, 756. If you call the preserved intellectual memory of itself immortality, in this signification too the human soul is immortal, §. 781, 641. As to use, either its total perceptions are for a longer time only sensitive, or it soon obtains intellectual ones. Those positing the former are hypnopsychites (Vertheidiger des Seelen-Schlafes), who, if they deny that the soul ever understands after the death of this body, are psychopannychites (der ewigen Nacht). The human soul before death had cognized something clearly or distinctly, §. 754. This reality, §. 520, is never altogether sterile, §. 517, insofar as it is a reality has no consequents, except realities, into the indefinite, §. 140, and is into the indefinite in universal nexus with the spirituality, understanding, reason of the soul, again realities, §. 531, 49, which have no consequents, except realities, as such, into the indefinite, §. 23, 140. Hence the nexus between the clear or distinct cognition of this life and the spirituality, understanding, reason of the soul is again a reality having real consequents into the indefinite, §. 140. But the natural real consequents of all these cannot be increased into the indefinite, §. 23, 162, without distinct perceptions, §. 631, reasonings, §. 642, volitions or nolitions, §. 665, 690. Hence the human soul after death, preserving its nature, §. 780, 781, naturally, at least in the end, also exerts an intellectual life through spiritual actions, §. 639, and the psychopannychites err.",["\"Qua usum\" is taken as \"as to use\" (quoad usum); the reading is doubtful.","\"der ewigen Nacht\" is printed as a fragment; the German gloss presupposes the preceding Vertheidiger.","\"consectaria\" is rendered \"consequents\"; \"intellectus\" \"understanding\"; \"intelligere\" \"understand\"."]],"met:783":["The human soul understanding after death either exerts personality also to such an extent that it distinctly recollects its state in this life, or less, §. 782. Those positing the latter can be called patrons of the Lethean cup (Freunde des Bechers der Vergessenheit). The former is more natural, §. 583, 561.",[]],"met:784":["The state of the human soul after death can be considered, 1) positing the absolute death of the human being, §. 778. Then the human soul after death is posited as destitute of every body, or that there is no body with which it would again have come into the closest interaction, §. 742, 740. 2) positing the death of the human being as only respective, as of one sent again into the closest interaction with a new body, §. 778. The beginning of a new closest interaction with a new body is called palingenesis (der Tausch des Leibes gegen einen andern) (regeneration, metensomatosis and metempsychosis taken more broadly). Those positing this either at the same time patronize the Lethean cup, and posit a new body such as are known on this earth, or posit neither of these together with it. The former defend metempsychosis strictly so called and gross (die grobe Seelenwanderung), improbably, §. 783.",["\"commercium\" is rendered \"interaction\" throughout.","\"missae\" is genitive feminine, agreeing with the soul: \"as of one sent\"."]],"met:785":["The human soul enduring after the death of the body which it had on this earth, §. 781, still represents the single parts of this universe, §. 752, 780, therefore also its bodies, §. 155. Bodies simultaneous to it act on it, suffer from it, it itself suffers from the bodies simultaneous to it, influences them, §. 408, but it does not influence two of them equally, nor suffer from two of them equally, §. 272. Therefore there is one body with which the human soul enduring after the death of the body comes into the closest interaction, §. 448, 739. Hence if a soul of this kind is called separated, that is to be understood respectively to the body such as we experience human beings to have on this earth, and the death of the human being is nothing but the transformation of an animal, §. 779, and palingenesis, which however would wrongly be confused with gross metempsychosis, §. 784.",["\"pati\" is rendered \"suffer\" (passio), \"influere\" \"influence\" (influxus)."]],"met:786":["The human soul enduring after the death of this body is in the closest interaction with another, §. 785. That new body will in its various states be congruent with this body, now less, now more, §. 270, 265. Therefore there will be some state of it in which it will be most congruent with the body which in this life had been in the closest interaction with the soul, §. 161, hence the same, §. 70.",["The source prints \"corpori. quod\"; the stray period is taken as a misprint and not rendered."]],"met:787":["Just as the perfection of a finite spirit is 1) either absolutely necessary, or contingent, §. 147, 2) either natural, or supernatural, §. 496, 3) either internal, or external, §. 98, 4) either moral broadly so called, or less so, §. 723: so too the goods to a spirit, by whose positing that is posited, §. 100, are 1) either metaphysical, or contingent, §. 147, 2) either natural, or supernatural, §. 469, 3) either domestic, or adventitious, §. 660, 4) either moral broadly so called, or less such, § 723. Those moral broadly so called are more closely connected with the freedom of a given spirit, either as grounds, §. 14, *a priori*, §. 24, and as antecedents of a certain state of the same, or as consequences, §. 14, *a posteriori*, §. 24, and as consequents of a certain state of the same, §. 596, or in both ways, §. 24. Those which depend more closely on a given freedom are called strictly moral (sittlich in engerer Bedeutung) and sometimes simply so. Hence the goods to a spirit depending more closely on its freedom are strictly moral (sittliche Güter in engrer Bedeutung), and the perfection to be posited when these are posited is blessedness (Seligkeit). The complex of the perfections befitting a spirit is happiness (Glückseligkeit). The complement of blessedness to the happiness of a finite spirit is prosperity (gutes Glück, Wohlfart), and the goods by whose positing it is posited are prosperous goods (Glücks-Güter) (physical ones strictly so called). The happiness of a finite spirit is the complex of prosperity and blessedness.",["\"rationes\" / \"rationata\" are rendered \"grounds\" / \"consequences\", \"antecedentia\" / \"consectaria\" \"antecedents\" / \"consequents\".","\"prospera\" (neuter plural) is rendered \"prosperous goods\"; \"beatitudo\" \"blessedness\", \"felicitas\" \"happiness\"."]],"met:788":["The evils of a spirit depending more closely on its freedom are strictly moral (das sittlich Böse in engrer), §. 787 (evils of guilt, sins), evils broadly moral (in weitrer Bedeutung) to a spirit are those more closely connected with its freedom. The imperfection from these is moral corruption broadly (das sittliche Verderben in weiter), from those, strictly so called (in engerer Bedeutung, oder Unseligkeit). The complex of the imperfections befitting a spirit is unhappiness (Unglückseligkeit). The complement of strictly moral corruption to the unhappiness of a finite spirit is misery (Elend), and the evils by whose positing it is posited are damages broadly so called (Widerwärtigkeiten, Leiden, ein schmerzendes Übel) (physical evils strictly so called). Unhappiness is the complex of misery and moral corruption.",["The German glosses \"in engrer\" and \"in weiter\" are elliptical as printed (Bedeutung understood from the next gloss)."]],"met:789":["The human soul in this life (in diesem Leben), i.e. while it lives in the closest interaction with a body such as we experience human beings to have on this earth, is changed continually, §. 418, 754. Of these changes none is objectively absolutely indifferent, §. 654. All therefore are either good or bad, or both. The changes of the soul which are at the same time good and bad, as all are, §. 264, are either at the same time good and bad equally, or unequally, §. 70. Those more good than bad posit more happiness than unhappiness, those more bad than good posit more unhappiness than happiness, and so, to be denominated *a potiori*, the former are good, the latter bad, §. 787, 788.",["\"mala\" is rendered \"bad\" (adjective) and elsewhere \"evils\" (noun)."]],"met:790":["Suppose a thing as much totally bad as it is good: it would not have the highest degree of reality, §. 246, 248. Therefore it would be a finite being, §. 248, hence contingent, §. 257, and so its opposite would be possible, §. 101, and since its existence would be contingent, §. 109, the existence of its opposite would be possible, §. 101. But the opposite of a thing of this kind could not exist except outside it, §. 81, 7, and would be, as good and bad, totally equal to the posited thing itself, §. 81, 267. Therefore two actual things outside one another, totally equal, would be possible, which is contrary to §. 272. Therefore a thing which would be as much totally good as bad cannot exist. Therefore there are also no changes of the human soul except either good or bad ones. Therefore every human soul in this life is either happy or unhappy, §. 789.",[]],"met:791":["The human soul enduring after the death of the body goes on being changed continually, §. 781, 782. Therefore at every moment of its duration its happiness or unhappiness is increased, §. 790, 162. Therefore the human soul enduring after the death of the body will either enjoy greater happiness than in this life, and is blessed (eine selige), or will labor under greater unhappiness, and is damned (verdammte Seele). The blessedness which the soul is posited to have once entered after this life will either coexist with the soul while it endures, or damnation will at some time succeed it. The damnation which the soul is posited to have once entered after this life will either coexist with the soul while it endures, or blessedness will at some time succeed it, §. 790, 789. The former is in both cases more natural, §. 139, 140.",["\"eidem\" (\"it\") may refer to the soul or to the blessedness / damnation; the ambiguity is kept."]],"met:792":["Since every soul is that in a being which can be conscious to itself of something, §. 504, it has a cognitive faculty, §. 519, and that either only the lower, or also the higher, §. 520, 524. The former will be a merely sensitive soul (eine bloss sinnliche Seele). An animal which has a merely sensitive soul is a brute (das Vieh); one whose soul is a spirit is a rational animal (ein vernünftiges Thier). Therefore the human being is a rational animal, §. 754, 740.",[]],"met:793":["The souls of brutes are in the closest interaction with an animal body, §. 740, hence they represent their body clearly and obscurely, §. 792, 736. Therefore they are powers representative of the universe according to the position of the body of brutes in it, §. 741, hence substances, §. 198, monads, §. 234, simple beings, §. 230, do not have parts outside parts, §. 224, finite, §. 202, 792, indivisible, §. 244, hence physically incorruptible, §. 746, immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422, equipped with sensation, imagination, foresight and the remaining faculties to be actuated without distinct cognition through the power representative of the universe according to the position of the body, §. 792, hence to be impelled by sensitive appetite and aversion, §. 667, and by choice, §. 718, by instincts, flights, and stimuli, §. 677, even to affects, §. 678.",["\"vis repraesentativa\" is rendered \"representative power\" (vis = force; power).","\"fugis\" (fugae, the counterpart of instinctus) is rendered literally \"flights\"; \"arbitrium\" \"choice\"."]],"met:794":["With the questions: whether brutes have a soul, whether a rational one, easier ones, §. 792, 793, let there not be confused: 1) whether all phenomena on this earth, bodies besides the human, are destitute of a soul, §. 504, 2) whether some of them are the seat of a rational soul, §. 745, 3) whether all which are animal bodies are those of one either already now enjoying some use of reason, or at last going to enjoy it, §. 782, 639.",["In 1) \"corpora\" stands in apposition to \"phaenomena\" and \"humanum\" is singular; the construction is kept.","In 3) the genitives \"gaudentis\" / \"gavisurae\" refer to a soul not named in the clause; the ellipsis is kept as \"of one\"."]],"met:795":["The souls of brutes are destitute of understanding, §. 792. Therefore they are not spirits, §. 402, they lack personality, §. 641, reason, §. 640, will, counter-will, §. 690, and freedom §. 719, nor are they immortal, as human souls are, §. 781-784, nor capable of happiness or unhappiness either now or hereafter, §. 787, 788.",["\"noluntas\", the faculty opposed to voluntas, is rendered \"counter-will\" to keep it apart from \"nolitio\" (nolition)."]],"met:796":["Spirits endowed with a greater essential degree of understanding than that which is of the human being are superior (höhere), with a lesser, inferior (niedrigere). Both are finite, either happy, or unhappy, §. 790, the former agathodaemons (gute) (calodaemons), the latter cacodaemons (böse Geister).",["\"illi ... hi\" may refer to the happy / unhappy or to the superior / inferior; the ambiguity is kept.","The gloss \"gute\" is elliptical as printed (Geister understood)."]],"met:797":["Every finite spirit, whether it be superior to the human being, or inferior, as actual has a body with which it is in the closest interaction, §. 785, 796, either constantly gravitating toward a certain center outside itself, or less. The former is an inhabitant of a certain total body in the universe, and that either fixed, or wandering, etc.",["\"actualis\" agrees with \"spiritus\"; rendered \"as actual\".","\"e.c.\" in the source is rendered \"etc.\"."]],"met:798":["Whatever degree of understanding a superior finite spirit may have had, it nevertheless does not have the greatest, §. 248, hence does not represent all things most distinctly, §. 637, but in such a way that from its body it can be cognized why it represents these, not others, more purely, more profoundly, more distinctly, confusedly, obscurely, §. 797, 512. Therefore every finite spirit has a lower cognitive faculty, §. 520.",[]],"met:799":["No finite spirit, whether it be superior to the human being, or inferior, can be physically corrupted, §. 746; hence even when some one of its bodies is dissolved to the utmost, it will nevertheless survive, unless it is annihilated, §. 745, while it endures it preserves personality, with the use of understanding, reason, freedom rather increasing than decreasing, or ceasing altogether, §. 782, and it is more natural to it to remember its past state distinctly than the Lethean cup, §. 783. Therefore every finite spirit is by its nature, i.e. through its power representative of the universe, §. 782, immortal, as the human soul, §. 781, 783, more naturally persisting, while it endures, in the blessedness or damnation which it has once entered, §. 787, 791, than that, once remarkably blessed, it be damned, or vice versa, §. 791.",["\"v.v.\" in the source is rendered \"vice versa\"."]],"met:800":["Natural theology is the science of God insofar as he can be cognized without faith.",[]],"met:801":["Natural theology contains the first principles of practical philosophy, teleology and revealed theology. Therefore it is referred, §. 2, with reason, to metaphysics, §. 1.",["\"cum ratione\" may mean \"with reason\" (rightly) or \"together with reason\"; the ambiguity is kept."]],"met:802":["Natural theology considers 1) the concept of God, 2) his operations.",[]],"met:803":["The most perfect being (das vollkommenste) is that to which belongs the highest perfection among beings: i.e. in which so many, so great, so much agree in so many and so great, as many, as great, as much as can agree in the most and the greatest of possibles in any being, §. 185. There is therefore in the most perfect being a certain plurality absolutely necessarily, §. 74.",[]],"met:804":["The predicates of the most perfect being are called its perfections. In the most perfect being there are as many perfections agreeing most as can be in a being at the same time, as many as are compossible, §. 803.",[]],"met:805":["Every perfection of the most perfect being is as great as can be in any being, §. 803, 804.",[]],"met:806":["The most perfect being is a real being, §. 803, 135. Therefore there belongs to it as great a reality as can be in a being. The most perfect being is the most real, §. 805, 804, in which are the most and greatest realities, the highest good and metaphysically the best, §. 190.",[]],"met:807":["All realities are truly positive, nor is any negation a reality, §. 36. Therefore even if all are conjoined in a being to the utmost, a contradiction will never arise from them, §. 13. Therefore all realities are compossible in a being. Now the most perfect being is the most real of beings, §. 806. Therefore to the most perfect being belongs the omnitude of realities, and of those the greatest which can be in any being, §. 805, 190.",["\"omnitudo realitatum\" is rendered \"omnitude of realities\"."]],"met:808":["When a reality is posited a negation is canceled, §. 36. Now in the most perfect being all realities are to be posited, §. 807. Therefore all negations are to be canceled.",["\"ponere\" / \"tollere\" are rendered \"posit\" / \"cancel\"."]],"met:809":["In everything impossible something is to be at the same time posited and canceled, §. 7. That is either a negation, or a reality, §. 36, 9. No reality in the most perfect being is to be canceled, §. 807. No negation in the most perfect being is to be posited, §. 808. Therefore nothing in the most perfect being is to be at the same time canceled, and posited. The most perfect being is possible, §. 8.",[]],"met:810":["Existence is a reality compossible with the essence and the remaining realities, §. 66, 807. Therefore the most perfect being has existence, §. 807.",[]],"met:811":["God (Gott) is the most perfect being. Therefore God is actual, §. 810, 55.",[]],"met:812":["The predicates of God are perfections, §. 811, 804, all of them greatest realities, §. 807. His very possibility is the greatest, 1) internal and absolute, §. 165, since in him the most and greatest things are compossible, §. 805, 807, 2) external and hypothetical, since the most fruitful and weightiest grounds in him have, in the whole nexus of all possible worlds, consequences that can be outside God, the most fruitful, the weightiest, §. 168.",["'ground': ratio; 'consequences': rationata."]],"met:813":["In God there are no negations, §. 808, therefore neither negations strictly so called, nor privations, §. 137, nor metaphysical evil, nor contingent, §. 146, nor physical, nor moral in any signification whatever, §. 788.",["'evil': malum, understood with each of the following adjectives."]],"met:814":["In the concept of God not only totally negative marks are impossible, §. 525, but also those truly partially such, §. 813, 808, although to us many things in him that are most real seem to be negative, §. 12, because partly we express negations in affirmative terms, partly we do not sufficiently remember that the negation of a negation affirms, §. 36, 81.",["'mark': nota.","vere may go with 'partially such' or with 'impossible' ('are truly impossible'); left ambiguous."]],"met:815":["The perfections of God are either absolute in him, or respective, and among these some relative, §. 37: either they can be represented by us without the mark of action, quiescent (ruhende), or they cannot, operative (wirksame Vollkommenheiten).",["'quiescent': quiescentes; 'operative': operativae."]],"met:816":["The internal perfection of God from which all the remaining internal ones can at length be so deduced that it itself cannot afterwards, by those who would avoid a circle, be deduced from another internal perfection of God, is the first concept of God, §. 40, 39. Now from the infinite perfections of God such a deduction of the remaining ones is possible, §. 24, 49, since each is the greatest ground, §. 812, 166, and the most sufficient, §. 169, hence also ultimate simply, §. 170, the greatest essence, §. 171. Therefore infinite are the first concepts of God, of which nevertheless each, taken for the essence, is the unique essence of God, §. 40, 77.",["ex infinitis dei perfectionibus: 'from the infinite perfections', i.e. possibly from any one of the infinitely many; kept as in the Latin.","'infinite are the first concepts': infiniti sunt conceptus, i.e. infinite in number."]],"met:817":["Although in God there is the greatest nexus, §. 808, 167, and the most universal, §. 172, so that in him all things are most truly from all, §. 876: nevertheless from one perfection of God his remaining perfections are more easily cognized by us than from another, §. 527. Therefore it is better to take that one for the essence from which we hope most easily to deduce the remaining ones, §. 816. So far is it from being the case that all plurality in God is impossible, that rather in his very essence and through it a certain plurality is posited absolutely necessarily, §. 812, 816.",["ex omnibus omnia verissime: the Latin has no verb; 'are' is the barest reading.","'cognized': cognoscuntur."]],"met:818":["God, besides the essence, whichever you have taken, §. 817, is determined as regards the remaining internal perfections too, §. 816, as much as anything, as internal, can be determined, §. 812. Therefore God is actual, §. 54.",["'as regards' and 'as': qua."]],"met:819":["God is determined as regards existence, §. 818, 811. Therefore God is a being, §. 61, 57, neither a negative nor a privative non-being, §. 7, 54.",["'non-being': non ens."]],"met:820":["From the being able of God to the being of the same the consequence is valid, i.e. his existence is sufficiently determined through his essence, §. 809-811, and indeed the greatest, §. 805, of the complex of the most and greatest affections compossible in any being, or of the greatest essence, §. 816, whichever you have taken, §. 817, the complement, §. 55, eternal, §. 302.",["A posse ... ad esse valet consequentia: the scholastic maxim; 'consequence' here is the inference.","'the greatest' (maxima) agrees with exsistentia; 'complement' (complementum) is in apposition to it."]],"met:821":["To God belongs unity, §. 819, 73, the greatest, §. 812, the inseparability of the most and greatest realities, §. 173, hence it is evident, 1) how it can eminently be called a perfect unity, which can stand by way of example of how even those that seemed most sterile among the perfections of God are nevertheless his essence, §. 816, 2) that, the highest unity of God being posited, a certain plurality of inseparables in him is not only not removed, but even posited, §. 74.",["'removed': tolli; 'posited': poni.","'highest' renders summus, 'greatest' maximus throughout."]],"met:822":["To God belongs transcendental truth, §. 819, 90, the greatest, §. 812, the highest order of his perfections, §. 175, 89, the highest possibility, §. 812, the highest nexus, §. 817, the highest agreement with the strongest rules, §. 179, 184, such as the law of the best in general: let the best be joined with the best, §. 482, 187, and of the best in beings: let the best of compossibles in the most perfect being be conjoined with the best, §. 803.",["'agreement': convenientia."]],"met:823":["God not actual would be a being enjoying all realities, which nevertheless would lack some, §. 66, 812, determined as regards all internal perfections, as much as a being, as internal, can be determined, §. 818, and yet, as regards some, not so determined, §. 54. Therefore the opposite of divine existence is impossible in itself, §. 15. The existence of God is absolutely necessary, §. 102. God is a necessary being, §. 109, whose highest existence, §. 820, is his essence, §. 819, §. 817 nevertheless being preserved.",["Deus non actualis esset: either 'God, if not actual, would be' or 'a non-actual God would be'; kept open.","salva tamen §. 817: 'without prejudice, nevertheless, to §. 817'."]],"met:824":["If God were not actual, the principle of contradiction would be false, §. 823, 7, the first principle both of form and of matter in all our demonstrations. Therefore, although many sciences can be demonstrated completely without any theological premise, §. 1-800: nevertheless they would not be, either they themselves, or their objects, e.g. §. 61, 354, 504, indeed they would not even be possible, §. 8, unless God were actual.",[]],"met:825":["God is a necessary being, §. 823, 824. In a necessary being there are no modes, §. 111. Therefore in God there are no modes, or predicable accidents.",[]],"met:826":["If we detect something in a necessary being that is partially similar to, partially different from what is represented in a contingent being, yet we do not sufficiently understand the differences, nor find a name peculiar to it: we call it the analogue of that which we noted as similar in a contingent being, and it is attributed to God by analogy, if realities seem to reign in its concept, by eminence (in unendlich-ausnehmender) (excellence), if negations, by reduction (in geläuterter Bedeutung) (the way of negation).",["'by eminence (excellence)': per eminentiam (excellentiam); 'by reduction (the way of negation)': per reductionem (via negationis).","The German equivalents (in unendlich-ausnehmender, in geläuterter Bedeutung) are printed incomplete in this form; carried over unchanged."]],"met:827":["If in a necessary being we detect some things which it is absolutely necessary to be hypothetically necessary, insofar as, as regards hypothetical necessity, they are similar to modes, §. 108, they are called analogues of modes (das den Zufälligkeiten ähnliche), §. 826, insofar as it is absolutely necessary through the essence that they exist in God as hypothetically necessary, §. 823, 54, they are attributes, §. 107. Hence the divine attributes can be divided into those more similar to the attributes of finite things, and to the modes of finite things. The last too are true attributes, since the sufficient ground is in the essence of God; 1) of their existence, insofar as it is contradistinguished from indetermination, §. 54. A contingent being can be, as regards many things, undetermined, §. 34, 134. A necessary being, as regards none, and indeed through its essence, §. 820. 2) of the duration, eternity, and immutability of those things which are once in God. In contingent beings the essential limit is the sufficient ground why they must continually be changed, while they exist, internally. In the necessary being essential infinitude is the sufficient ground of the opposite: 3) why other things in the place of these could have existed in God from eternity to eternity, and hence, 4) why the existence of these, not of others, is determined thus, not otherwise, only through hypothetical necessity, §. 102.",["'limit': limes; 'infinitude': infinitudo.","After the colon, 3) and 4) may continue the enumeration begun at 1) (what the essence of God is the ground of) or spell out 'the opposite'; the Latin leaves both open."]],"met:828":["Holiness (Heiligkeit) is the reality of a being by which several of its imperfections, truly such, are removed, hence the most holy (das Allerheiligste) is that by whose reality all its imperfections are removed. Now through the omnitude of the greatest realities in God all imperfections in him are removed, §. 142, 808. Therefore God is most holy.",["'omnitude': omnitudo, the sum of all."]],"met:829":["In God there is no imperfection, §. 828. Therefore neither essential, nor accidental, nor internal, nor external, §. 121, 808. The same is evident from §. 813.",[]],"met:830":["In God there is the sufficient ground why his perfections inexist in him, §. 822, 823. Therefore force strictly so called, §. 197. Hence God is a substance, §. 199.",["'inexist': inexsistant, 'exist in'.","'force': vis."]],"met:831":["God has force, §. 830, the greatest, §. 812. Therefore one sufficient for actualizing the most and greatest accidents, §. 203.",[]],"met:832":["Accidents do not exist outside their substances, §. 194. Therefore a force sufficient for actualizing the most and greatest accidents suffices for actualizing the most substances. Therefore all, §. 191, 247. A force sufficient for actualizing something is power (Gewalt). Hence omnipotence (Allgewaltigkeit) is a force sufficient for actualizing all things. God is omnipotent, §. 831.",["'power': potentia; 'force': vis."]],"met:833":["Things absolutely impossible are nothing, §. 7. Therefore he who could do things absolutely impossible could do nothing, §. 469. God can do all things, §. 832. Therefore the omnipotence of God does not extend to things absolutely impossible. Impossible to someone is that for the actualizing of which his forces do not suffice, §. 469. In this signification no thing is impossible to God. Every thing therefore impossible to us and to every finite being is possible to God, §. 832.",["'forces': vires."]],"met:834":["Miracles are possible, §. 475. The force of God suffices for actualizing all possible things, §. 832. Therefore God can perform rigorous miracles, §. 477, 833.",["'rigorous': rigorosa."]],"met:835":["God can actualize all possible worlds, §. 832. Therefore also the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect without exceptions, §. 442.",["'without exceptions' (sine exceptionibus) may go with 'the most imperfect' or with 'can actualize'; left ambiguous."]],"met:836":["God is a substance, §. 830, and has no modes, §. 825. Therefore he is a necessary substance, §. 202, and has no internal state, §. 206, and is not modifiable, §. 209. Whence it is evident anew that the world cannot be a modification of God, §. 388.",[]],"met:837":["All arising and perishing, arising from nothing and annihilation of a necessary substance are absolutely impossible; God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore all arising and perishing in God is absolutely impossible, §. 227, 228.",["'arising': ortus; 'perishing': interitus."]],"met:838":["Every substance is a monad, §. 234. God is a substance, §. 830. Therefore he is a monad, and a simple being, §. 230. The highest simplicity of God being posited, it is indeed removed that he is in any way composed of parts outside parts, §. 224, but the diversity of many things in God is not removed, §. 817, a most real one, §. 807, because even in finite things it is false that all things really diverse are posited outside one another, §. 755.",[]],"met:839":["God is a necessary being, §. 823, 824. The determinations of every necessary being are absolutely and internally immutable, §. 132. Therefore God is absolutely and internally immutable, §. 126, 127. The same is evident thus: if God were absolutely and internally mutable, at least some internal perfection of his could exist after another, §. 124. Hence the existence of that which could have existed before it could be removed. This is a reality internal to God, §. 37. Therefore some of the realities of God could be separated from the others, §. 72, which is against the highest unity of God, §. 821.",[]],"met:840":["If God were composite, he would be extended, §. 241, and a force of inertia would have to be attributed to him, §. 832, 294. Hence he would be matter, §. 295. Therefore divisible, §. 427, and so internally mutable, §. 244, 126, which is against §. 839. Therefore God is a simple being, §. 224. And since he is a substance, §. 830, he is a monad, §. 230. It is evident anew that the universal materialists err, §. 395.",["'force of inertia': vis inertiae."]],"met:841":["In God there are no simultaneous things posited outside one another, parts of him, §. 840, hence no space either, §. 239. Therefore God is not extended, nor does he fill space in that signification in which extended things are said to fill it, §. 241.",[]],"met:842":["God does not have quantitative magnitude, §. 838, 243, although he is the greatest being, §. 161, of the most and greatest realities, §. 807, 812. It is evident anew that God is indivisible, and indeed absolutely, §. 244.",[]],"met:843":["The omnitude of the greatest realities that can be is the greatest degree of reality, §. 247, 248. This belongs to God, the most real being, §. 807, 812. Therefore God is an infinite real being, §. 248. The same is evident from the fact that God is a necessary being, §. 823, 258.",["'omnitude': omnitudo.","ens infinitum reale: 'real' may qualify 'being' or the kind of infinity ('really infinite'); left as in the Latin."]],"met:844":["All the perfections of God have the degree of their reality which they can have in a being, the greatest, §. 812. Hence they themselves too are all infinite, §. 248. To the highest reality of any one of these perfections belongs agreement in the most perfect being, §. 139, 140. Therefore in whom there is one infinite reality, in him are all, in whom all, in him the highest, §. 843. Therefore he in whom there is one infinite reality is God, §. 811. Hence it can be gathered for what reason several perfections of God, now these, now those, can be taken as first concepts, §. 816, and why there cannot be admitted, outside God, an author of evil equally powerful as he himself. Manichaeism is the opinion positing that an author of evil equally powerful with God exists, and it is an error.",["'agreement': consensus.","'opinion': sententia."]],"met:845":["God is in act whatever can be, as regards internal perfections, §. 843, 259. It is evident anew that God is internally immutable, §. 252, and a necessary being, provided a circle is avoided, §. 843, 256.",["'in act': actu."]],"met:846":["Several gods are impossible. For if there were several, they would partly be diverse, §. 74, hence there would be in one what would not be in the other, §. 38. This would be either a reality or a negation, §. 36. If it were a reality, the one whom it was lacking would not be God, §. 807. If it were a negation, the one in whom it inhered would not be God, §. 808. If it were posited that one reality is in one, but another equipollent in the other, in neither would there be the omnitude of realities, hence neither would be God, §. 807. Since therefore the God whom we have thus far meditated on, §. 811-845, is in such a way the supremely one of all the greatest absolutely inseparable realities, §. 821, that another one or other diverse from this is not, nor can be, which we have thus far seen it is necessary to be God, §. 811-845, the God whom we have thus far meditated on is the unique God, §. 77. Polytheism (die Vielgötterei) is the opinion positing several gods, and it is an error. We rather truly venerate in God the highest uniqueness, by which the greatest being, §. 842, from all things, even the greatest in their kind, e.g. the universal nature of the best world, and the greatest spirit of finite things, through the most and greatest differences, §. 844, is distinguished, §. 173. This greatest difference extends to the relations themselves in such a way that such and so great a relation cannot intervene between a thing which is not the God thus far considered, and a third, as such and so great as between this God and a given third thing, §. 812, 817. By this highest uniqueness of God there is indeed removed the equality of any thing which is not God, even if otherwise the greatest, with him, and a similarity not only almost total, but also one removing the infinite dissimilarity to be posited between God and all other things, §. 844, but there is not removed the plurality of the greatest things within God and the at the same time infinite respective difference of these supremely the same, §. 37, 174, but rather it is posited in infinite characters what distinguishes God from all things, §. 67.",["'uniqueness': unicitas; 'unique': unicus.","quod deum esse necessarium esse, hucusque vidimus: obscure; either 'which, as we have seen, would necessarily be God' or 'what we have seen is necessary to being God'; kept literal.","natura mundi optimi universa: 'universal' (universa) may mean 'the whole nature'."]],"met:847":["The simplicity of God is evident anew. For if God were composite, his parts would be substances posited outside one another, §. 225, 282. Of these only one would be an infinite substance, §. 846. Therefore the remaining ones finite, §. 77, 248. Hence in God there would have to be posited certain absolutely necessary imperfections, §. 250, 155. Which is against §. 828.",[]],"met:848":["God does not have a figure, §. 280, 847. Cruder anthropomorphism is the error of those attributing to God a figure, e.g. a human one, subtler is the error which attributes to God the imperfections of finite things, e.g. of human beings, §. 828.",["'figure': figura, shape."]],"met:849":["In God there are no successive things, §. 839, 124. Therefore neither is there time in God, §. 239. He is not in time in such a way that he constitutes a part of the successive things, §. 124, 837. And while arising and perishing are absolutely impossible for him, §. 837, he has duration, §. 299, the greatest, §. 812, the only really infinite one, §. 844, 846, hence he is eternal, §. 302, and, coexisting with all time, sempiternal, §. 303, he was, is, will be, §. 298. God exists.",["ortus et interitus: 'arising and perishing'","realiter infinitam: 'really infinite'; dum rendered 'while' throughout"]],"met:850":["If a contingent eternal being is posited, its eternity differs much from the eternity of God, §. 67. 1) Its duration is subject to continual successions of modifications, §. 209, 836, 2) its eternity indeed has no ends of protension, but for that reason it cannot yet be called really infinite, §. 259, 849, 3) its eternity would be a time without beginning and end, whence it could be called infinite, but mathematically, not for that reason really, §. 248, 849, because, a successive being, §. 238, it is never in act whatever it can be, as to its internal determinations, §. 259.",["fines protensionis: 'ends of protension' (extension in time)","actu: 'in act'; qua: 'as to'"]],"met:851":["God is a necessary being, §. 823, 824, and an infinite one, §. 843. Hence he is a being from itself and independent, §. 310. He exists, §. 849, in such a way that he is not the caused of another posited outside him, a cause of his effects simply such, §. 318. All perfections of God are really infinite, §. 844, hence no perfection of God is the caused of something posited outside God, nor can be, §. 381, 248, 310. But a passion would be a predicate of God which would be the caused of something posited outside God, §. 210. Therefore God is altogether impassible, suffers from no thing posited outside himself, neither ideally nor really, §. 212, nor does anything posited outside him act upon him, §. 210, therefore neither does he react, §. 213. Every action of God upon the universe is without reaction, as much ideal as real, §. 212.",["causatum: 'the caused', Baumgarten's substantive; simpliciter talis: 'simply such'","passio / patitur: 'passion' / 'suffers' (is acted upon)","in universum: 'upon the universe'; could also be read adverbially, 'in general'"]],"met:852":["Images (Bilder und Gleichnisse) are 1) signs of the figure of another. Now God does not have a figure, §. 848. Therefore images of God in this signification are impossible, §. 347. 2) things similar to another in a notable degree. Since every being is similar to God in some degree, §. 265, every more perfect being will be an image of God, §. 70, 811, and the more perfect it will be, the more similar to God it will be, the greater therefore an image of God, §. 265, 160.",["quoque read as quo-que, 'and the more'"]],"met:853":["The world is the whole of actual finite things, §. 354. God is not the whole of actual finite things, §. 844. Therefore God is not the world: neither this one nor any world is God. The same is evident from §. 361, 823, from §. 365, 839, from §. 370, 837, from §. 388, 843.",[]],"met:854":["This world has an extramundane efficient cause, §. 375, 388, and this a necessary substance, §. 381, 319. Therefore a necessary substance is possible, §. 333, 69. If a necessary substance is possible, it is actual, §. 109, and sempiternal, §. 302. Therefore a necessary substance exists. God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore God exists.",[]],"met:855":["God is an extramundane being, §. 843, 388, and the world is neither an essential, nor the essence, nor an attribute, nor a mode, nor a modification, nor an accident of God, §. 843. God is not the only substance, §. 391. Theological Spinozism is the opinion that removes God as an extramundane being, and is an error.",["sententia: 'opinion'; tollens: 'that removes' (does away with)","unica: 'the only'"]],"met:856":["As §. 811 proves the existence of God *a priori*, so §. 854 presents the same as established *a posteriori*, each independently of the propositions which egoism, §. 392, idealism, §. 402, and materialism remove, §. 395. Therefore they can be employed for convincing egoists, idealists, materialists of the existence of God, even as such.",["evictam: 'established' (conclusively proved); uterque: 'each' of the two"]],"met:857":["God is not matter, §. 295, 841, therefore he is immaterial, §. 422. God is not a body, §. 296. Therefore he is incorporeal, §. 422. Therefore he is physically incorruptible, §. 746. The same is evident from §. 837.",[]],"met:858":["The most perfect world is among finite things the greatest image of God, §. 436, 852, in the world the substances, §. 400, 857, among the substances the spirits, §. 402, 531, among the spirits those enjoying the greatest understanding, §. 637, and among those enjoying the greatest understanding the most happy, §. 787, the most remote from any evil whatever, especially moral, §. 788, 813, the most holy, §. 828.",["The elliptical construction is kept: imago dei maxima is understood with each member after the first"]],"met:859":["The nature of God (*naturans*, cf. §. 466) is the complex of his internal perfections, §. 430, by which he is a cause of his effects simply such and impassible, §. 851. Therefore whatever is absolutely possible is physically possible to God, §. 833, and nothing is physically impossible to God which would not be impossible in itself, §. 469, 833, whence nothing is physically necessary to God which would not at the same time be absolutely necessary, absolutely contingent things are also physically contingent to God, §. 469, 104, not only all preternatural but also supernatural events of the world, §. 474, 475.",["naturans kept in Latin (natura naturans); complexus: 'complex'"]],"met:860":["If a naturalist in the more general signification denies all supernatural events in this world, he errs on account of the denied hypothetical possibility of the same. For since miracles, even in nexus with God, to be posited outside them, §. 855, 474, are possible, §. 834, they also have extrinsic possibility, §. 16, 859.",["ob negatam ... possibilitatem: literally 'on account of the denied possibility', i.e. his denial of it"]],"met:861":["To the degree of reality in God, and to all his perfections, a homogeneous is impossible, §. 843, 844, while uniqueness belongs to God and to all his perfections, §. 846, 844. From a homogeneous quantity taken as one, therefore, the degree of reality met with in God and all his perfections cannot be understood. Therefore we can measure neither God nor any perfection of his, §. 291. What we cannot measure is immense (unermesslich). Therefore God and all his perfections are immense. Which immensity we understand from this also, that one who has measured must have as many clear perceptions as there are parts, degrees, or various things in that which he truly measures. Hence one who had truly measured God, or anything internal to God, would have to have really infinite clear perceptions, which do not fall into any finite understanding, §. 844.",["homogeneus: substantive use kept, 'a homogeneous' (thing)","unicitas: 'uniqueness'; obvius: 'met with'"]],"met:862":["God is conceivable in himself, §. 632, 809, and to man, §. 804-1000, and can be defined rightly in many ways, indeed really, §. 816. That is called comprehensible (fasslich, ergründlich, erforschlich, erschöpflich) of which a full cognition (völlige, gänzliche Kenntniss) is possible, i.e. one excluding all ignorance, hence a complete historical, philosophical, mathematical one. That is incomprehensible to someone (einem unfasslich, unergründlich, unerforschlich, unerschöpflich) for acquiring the full cognition of which his powers do not suffice, and the more necessary it is for this one to be distant from the full cognition of that, the more incomprehensible to him is what is given. The greater something is, the more difficult or more impossible is its comprehension for a finite cognizer, §. 160, 527, hence the greatest is maximally incomprehensible to all finite things, §. 261. Deism is the opinion positing that almost nothing about God is conceivable, except perhaps his existence, and is an error, although God and everything in him are maximally incomprehensible to us and to all finite things, §. 861, 806.",["cognitio: 'cognition'; Baumgarten's German equivalent is Kenntniss","huic / illius: 'this one' (the cognizer) / 'that' (the thing)","impossibilior: comparative 'more impossible' kept"]],"met:863":["Distinct cognition is a reality, §. 531. In God are all realities, §. 807. Therefore God cognizes distinctly. Therefore he has an understanding, is an intellectual substance, §. 830, a spirit, §. 402.",["intellectus: 'understanding'"]],"met:864":["In God are all the greatest realities, §. 812. The more things, the more distinctly, are cognized, the greater the distinct cognition is, §. 634. Therefore the distinct cognition of all things will be the most distinct, §. 161. Now the distinct cognition of all things is possible, §. 632. Therefore in God is the distinct cognition of all things, and the same the most distinct.",[]],"met:865":["The understanding of God is the highest, §. 863, 812, and immutable, §. 839. Therefore in it there are no preceding and following thoughts, §. 125. It is the highest, while it represents the most numerous, clearest marks of the most numerous, greatest things, in the strongest and most diverse associates. Therefore the understanding of God is the most profound, the most extensive, the purest, §. 637.",["notas: 'marks'; sociis: 'associates', the sense of the phrase is doubtful","patentissimus: 'most extensive' (literally 'most open')"]],"met:866":["God cognizes himself, as far as can be done, most fully, §. 865. Cognition about God is theology in the broader signification, the theology by which God cognizes himself is exemplary (αρχετυποσ), inasmuch as theologies of finite things similar to it are to be striven for, §. 346.",["Greek kept as the transcription prints it (archetypos)","intendendae: 'to be striven for'"]],"met:867":["God, representing all things, represents to himself all possible worlds, §. 864. This is an internal perfection of God, §. 37, the essence of the same, §. 816.",["eiusdem essentia: literally 'the essence of the same'"]],"met:868":["God cognizes most distinctly all the essences of all finite things, §. 864. Inasmuch, therefore, as the essences of things are represented in the understanding of God, they depend on him, §. 14, are eternal in the same, §. 849.",[]],"met:869":["God represents to himself all possible worlds most distinctly, §. 867. Therefore both the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect, §. 442, and this one. The world, inasmuch as it is represented sensitively, is sensible (die Welt, als ein Schauspiel der Sinnlichkeit) (visible), inasmuch as it is distinctly cognized, intelligible (die Welt, als ein Gegenstand des Verstandes). God cognizes this intelligible world most distinctly. Therefore he cognizes most distinctly all the monads of this world, all the souls of the same, §. 864. One who is powerful in the most distinct cognition of the human soul is a searcher of hearts (Herzens-Prüfer). Therefore God is a searcher of hearts, §. 740, and sees through the representation of the sensible world of each monad, of each soul, §. 400, 741, and indeed much more perfectly than a given monad, a given soul knows itself and its representation of the world, §. 864.",["visible: adspectabilis, Baumgarten's second Latin term","pollens: 'powerful in'; perspicit: 'sees through'"]],"met:870":["In God there is no sensitive cognition, §. 864, 521. Therefore he does not have lower cognitive faculties, §. 520, nothing is obscure to him, nothing confused, hence he cognizes no thing more clearly than another, §. 528, neither attends nor abstracts, §. 529, neither reflects nor compares, §. 626.",["facultates cognoscitivas inferiores: 'lower cognitive faculties'"]],"met:871":["Since God cognizes all things signified most distinctly, he has an intuition of all, §. 620, 864. He cognizes also all signs, and all symbolic cognition of souls in the world, §. 864, 869. Nor yet is there ever in him a greater or lesser perception of signs than of things signified, §. 870, each always the greatest, §. 864.",["intuitum: 'intuition' (intuitus)"]],"met:872":["God represents to himself every nexus most distinctly, §. 864. Therefore he has the highest reason, §. 640. His reason is the highest, while it is the highest understanding, hence immutable, without any succession of ratiocinations, §. 865, seeing through the greatest nexus of the most numerous things, §. 645.",["ratio: 'reason'; ratiocinia: 'ratiocinations'"]],"met:873":["The cognition of God is of the greatest extension and majesty, most exact, §. 864, and most ordered, §. 822, without any ignorance and error, narrowness and levity, in which there is nothing tumultuary, §. 515, most clear, most distinct, of every truth, hence most certain, in which there is nothing obscure, nothing confused, nothing inadequate, nothing incomplete, nothing impure, nothing superficial, probable, doubtful, or improbable, nothing dead, inert and speculative, §. 669, 671. He most distinctly cognizes all certain principles, all their principiates, every nexus of principles and principiates, §. 864, 872, therefore the cognition of him is the highest science. God most distinctly cognizes all the ignorances of souls, errors, narrownesses of cognition, meagernesses, whatever there is in cognition of this kind of the gross and tumultuary, of night, of confusion, of inadequacy, of incompleteness, of impurity, all the moral certainties of souls, probabilities, doubts, improbabilities, opinions, arguments, scruples, inert and dead speculations, §. 869.",["cognitio dei / cognitio ipsius: the genitive may be objective or subjective, kept ambiguous","citra: 'without' (literally 'on this side of'); tumultuarii: 'tumultuary' (hasty, disorderly)","scientia: 'science' throughout, as for Wissenschaft"]],"met:874":["God knows, §. 873, I) all the determinations of all things, inasmuch as they are regarded as merely possible. This is the science of simple intelligence (die Wissenschaft des Möglichen).",["scientia simplicis intelligentiae: usually 'knowledge of simple intelligence'; scientia kept as 'science'"]],"met:875":["God knows, §. 873, II) all the determinations of actual things, 1) of this world, by free science (of vision) (die freie Wissenschaft), α) past things by divine recollection (das Angedenken),β) present things, by the science of vision (Sehn),γ) future things, by foreknowledge (Vorhersehung Gottes). Philosophical Socinianism is the opinion that removes the divine foreknowledge of future contingents, and is an error.",["The missing spaces before β) and γ) are the transcription's"]],"met:876":["God knows all the determinations of actual things 2) of another world than this one, by middle science (die mittlere Wissenschaft Gottes, oder dessen Einsicht bloss möglicher Welten). In place of any event of this world another could exist, §. 363, 324. But any one would have its consequences, partially different, into the indefinite through all the states of the world that are to follow, §. 488. Therefore if, in place of even only one event of this world, another had existed, this world would be, through all the states successive to that event, indeed the antecedent ones as well, §. 357, 278, partially other than it is. Therefore whatever could have existed in place of any event in this world with all its consequences, God knows, by middle science, §. 378.",["scientia media: usually 'middle knowledge'; scientia kept as 'science'","consectaria: 'consequences'; in indefinitum: 'into the indefinite'"]],"met:877":["God is always most distinctly conscious to himself of the state of the present world, §. 875. Therefore he never sleeps, §. 556. The same is evident from §. 870. He does not sense, as we do, §. 870, 544, but by eminence, by the science of vision, §. 875, waking, §. 552, 826.",["per eminentiam: 'by eminence'","status may be singular or plural, 'state' or 'states'"]],"met:878":["Eternally God has represented to himself a future thing as much as he can, §. 875, 843. Therefore while it becomes present in the world, nothing is added to the cognition of God, §. 161, although it itself is changed from an object of foreknowledge into an object of vision, §. 125. Eternally God will represent to himself a past thing as much as he can, §. 875, 843. Therefore while, present, it will become past, it itself will indeed be changed from an object of vision into an object of recollection, but nothing will depart from the cognition of God, §. 161. God intuits all the successive states of this world eternally, §. 875, whence the internal immutability of divine cognition can be thought, §. 839.",["aeternum (adverbial): 'eternally', also 'from eternity' / 'to eternity'","intuetur: 'intuits' (intuitus)"]],"met:879":["One who cannot err is infallible (unfehlbar, unbetrieglich). Therefore God is infallible, §. 873. The least infallibility is if for someone it is least impossible to confuse only a single least truth with a falsehood; the more, therefore, the greater truths someone does not know how to confuse with falsehoods, the greater the impossibility of such confusion, the more he is infallible. While, therefore, we attribute the greatest infallibility to God, §. 812, we venerate him as absolutely not knowing how to confuse the most numerous, greatest truths with anything false.",["nescire: 'not know how' (be unable)"]],"met:880":["The least certainty is the least clear cognition of one least truth, §. 161. The more clearly, the more numerous, the greater truths are cognized, the greater the certainty will be, §. 160. While, therefore, we attribute to God the highest subjective certainty, §. 873, 812, we venerate him as cognizing the most numerous, greatest truths most distinctly. The same God is objectively most certain, §. 93, 812, while his truth is the highest, §. 822, and his existence demonstrable through his essence and through the existence of any finite thing whatever, §. 856.",[]],"met:881":["The free science of God is a respective perfection of his, §. 875, 815. And since it is absolutely necessary that it be most true, §. 879, it presents to God this world as existing contingently in and through itself, §. 361, hence that it is not, except hypothetically, necessary is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Therefore the free science of God is an analogue of a mode, §. 827.",["analogon modi: 'analogue of a mode'"]],"met:882":["Wisdom of the final nexus in general (Weisheit überhaupt) is insight, and indeed insight into ends is wisdom specifically (insonderheit), into means prudence (Klugheit). Hence God is the most omniwise, §. 872; when we call him the most omniwise, we venerate him as 1) all ends, 2) all means, 3) all their possible nexuses, §. 343, 4) with respect to all qualities, 5) all quantities, 6) in the greatest possible nexus, 7) most certainly and most ardently perceiving, §. 880, 873.",["'Insight' and 'perceiving' render perspicientia / perspicientem; 'means' renders remedia throughout.","The participle perspicientem stands last in the Latin and governs items 1)-6); the order is kept."]],"met:883":["God knows all ends, hence both the best and the worst, which of them can be a means to another, hence every possible subordination of all ends, also every coordination, §. 315, all goals in a certain respect, and the absolutely ultimate one, §. 343, according to their every goodness, and degree of goodness, every possible nexus with means, in such a way that to him indeed no end ever appears other than it is, §. 879, but that nevertheless he at the same time cognizes how each end appears to all and each of the souls cognizing it, §. 869. Therefore God knows best concerning the best ends that they are the best, §. 882.",["'Knows' renders both scit (first sentence) and novit (last sentence).","'In a certain respect' and 'absolutely' render secundum quid and simpliciter."]],"met:884":["God knows all means, hence also the best, §. 882, which being posited, the highest perfection is posited, §. 187. Now when means are posited, the end is posited, §. 326, 341. Therefore the best means are subordinated to the best end. God knows them insofar as they are subordinated to the best end, how, and to what extent they are subordinated, §. 883.",[]],"met:885":["A certain means (ein gewisses Mittel) is one that in truth is such and so great as it is cognized to be, hence it is opposed to the apparent one, which only seems, and either is not at all, or is not so great as it seems, and to the uncertain one, whose true goodness, or degree of goodness, is not clear. The best means are the most certain, both insofar as they are not apparent, and insofar as God is most certain both of the goodness and of the degree of goodness of them, §. 882, nonetheless perceiving all possible doubts of souls about them, §. 869, 873.",["'Certain' renders certum, said of the means, and certissimus, said of God."]],"met:886":["The more fruitful a means is, the greater the perfection posited when it is posited, §. 166, 341. Therefore the most fruitful means are the best, and vice versa, §. 187. God knows the most fruitful means, §. 884, and how much fruitfulness each has, §. 882, and how much fruitfulness is ascribed to each by given souls, §. 873.",["'Means' renders remedium and remedia, but media in the second sentence."]],"met:887":["The best means are complete, §. 886, for the best end, §. 884. Therefore they are not incomplete, §. 81. If they were superabundant, they would have some things doing nothing toward the end. Therefore not actualizing good, §. 341, yet not altogether sterile, §. 23. Hence evil, §. 146. Therefore the best means are adequate to the best end. God knows the means adequate to the best end, §. 884. Certain and adequate means are those attaining the end entirely (den Zweck völlig erreichen). God knows the means attaining the best end entirely, §. 885.",["'Entirely' renders ex asse, literally 'to the whole as'."]],"met:888":["The actualization of an end through means which attain it entirely, the fewest, is the shortest way (der kürzeste Weg). The use of the best means is always the shortest way. For then the end is actualized through means attaining it entirely, §. 887, of which each is most fruitful, §. 886, none superabundant, §. 887. They will hence be, of those that can be, the fewest, §. 161. God knows every shortest way, §. 887.",["paucissima agrees with remedia, set after the relative clause: 'through the fewest means which attain it entirely'.","'Means' renders remedia, but mediorum in the second sentence."]],"met:889":["Omniscience (Allwissenheit) is the knowledge of all things. God is the most omniscient, §. 873. When we call him such, we venerate him as §. 863-888 present him.",["'Knowledge' renders scientia."]],"met:890":["God intuits all the perfections or imperfections of all things most distinctly, §. 889, 871. Whatever is in God is the greatest ground, §. 23, 812. Hence the intuition of perfections or imperfections in him is most lively, §. 669, 873. Therefore satisfaction and dissatisfaction belong to God, §. 655, the highest, §. 812.",["'Ground' renders ratio.","'Satisfaction' and 'dissatisfaction' render complacentia and displicentia, kept apart from voluptas and taedium ('pleasure', 'displeasure')."]],"met:891":["The satisfaction and dissatisfaction of God are nothing but the truest, §. 880, most distinct, §. 870, most rational, §. 822; he is never totally indifferent, §. 653, nor ever partially toward any thing, §. 654, has neither pleasure, nor displeasure, §. 656, neither appetite nor aversion that are sensitive, neither instincts nor flights, §. 677, nor affects, §. 678, nor apparent pleasures nor displeasures, § 655.",["sensitiva (neuter plural) may qualify only appetite and aversion or also the pleasure and displeasure before them; the Latin allows both.","'Pleasure' and 'displeasure' render voluptas and taedium; 'flights' renders fugas."]],"met:892":["Since God intuits himself, as a good so great, as the best, most holy, most distinctly, §. 866, 828, he draws the purest pleasure from himself, §. 661. This is God's highest acquiescence in himself, §. 682. Exemplary theology is most sweet, §. 866. God has mere displeasure from no thing, §. 891, 661. His dissatisfaction and satisfaction is all immutable, §. 839. Hence he knows no transitory pleasures and displeasures, §. 662, nothing is troublesome to him, §. 658. Nor does his highest dissatisfaction obscure his highest pleasure in any way, §. 870, nor can anything outside him actualize any pleasure or displeasure in him, §. 851.",["'Acquiescence' renders acquiescentia; 'knows no' renders nescit."]],"met:893":["In God there is free knowledge of this world, although there could be of none, and of another, §. 881. Yet the free knowledge of this world does not exist in God except by the force of God, §. 851, 197. Therefore God determined his force to actualizing the free knowledge not of none, not of another, but of this world. Therefore God desires and is averse, §. 663. Now he does not desire, is not averse, sensitively, §. 891. Yet his desire and aversion follow cognition, §. 668, 822. Therefore he wills and does not will, has a will and a nill, §. 689, the highest, §. 812, i.e. one following the highest omniscience most perfectly, §. 668, 889.",["'Free knowledge' renders scientia libera.","'Nill' renders noluntas, the counterpart of voluntas; 'desire' and 'is averse' render appetit, aversatur."]],"met:894":["A will or nill following the mathematical cognition of the intellect, or desiring in proportion to the degree of goodness distinctly cognized in the things to be desired, averse in proportion to the degree of imperfection distinctly cognized in the things to be avoided, is proportional to the intellect (Wollen und Nicht-Wollen, so der Einsicht des Verstandes), which if it presents a thing as good or evil as it is, the will or nill is proportional to the objects (so den Gegenständen angemessen). Therefore proportionality belongs to the divine will and nill, §. 893, the highest, §. 812, since it follows the most distinct in the highest degree and infallible cognition of the degrees of perfection and imperfection, §. 879, 883.",["'Intellect' renders intellectus; 'or' renders s. (sive).","The relative clause qui si ... sistat does not resume grammatically; the break is kept."]],"met:895":["God, when he wills, §. 393, acts, §. 210. All the actions of God can depend on a sufficient internal principle, God acting, §. 833, and do depend, §. 851. Therefore spontaneity belongs to the divine actions and to God himself, §. 704, and that the highest, §. 872, since it suffices for the most and greatest actions, §. 706, 832. God's most spontaneous actions will be either immanent or transeunt, §. 211.",["agenti deo stands in apposition to principio: the sufficient internal principle is God acting."]],"met:896":["God is free from absolute internal coercion, §. 710, 702, and physical, §. 710, 859, and external both ideal and real, §. 707, 851, and thus also moral, §. 723, of motives, §. 726, to unwilling actions through violence in a certain respect, §. 714, from enticements, threats, persuasions, dissuasions, extortions, §. 728.",["'In a certain respect' renders secundum quid."]],"met:897":["All actions are placed in the power of God, §. 708, 859. If therefore some action and any action opposed to it are both possible in themselves, §. 15, i.e. if some action is contingent in itself, §. 104, and hence has an opposite action also contingent in itself, §. 104, both are placed in the power of God. Therefore all actions contingent in themselves are free to God with respect to execution and indifferent as to the exercise of the act, §. 708, because with regard to God the physical possibility of both is the highest, §. 844. Hence the actualization of this universe, or of none, or of another, was free to God with respect to execution, §. 835. He was indifferent, as to the exercise of the act, thus as much toward the best world as toward the most imperfect, and he is the same, and will be. §. 849.",["'With respect to execution' and 'as to the exercise of the act' render ratione exsecutionis and qua exercitium actus.","tam ergo mundum optimum: ergo reads as a misprint for erga, and is so taken; as printed, 'thus' and the accusative stand without a governing word."]],"met:898":["Since God determines himself in acting according to his liking, §. 893, 712, and that a distinct one, §. 893, he has freedom, §. 719, and that the highest, §. 812, i.e. one actualizing the greatest and most actions according to the most distinct liking, §. 725. fatalism, the opinion that removes divine freedom, is an error.",["'Liking' renders lubitus (pro lubitu).","The lower-case fatalismus after the full stop is the source's."]],"met:899":["God wills most freely, §. 898. Therefore the good, §. 719, 665. God is averse most freely, §. 898. Therefore evil, §. 719, 665. Volition or love of the good, hatred or nolition of evil, are infinite in God, §. 844, 1) extensively, since he loves every good, hates every evil, §. 898, 889, 2) protensively, since they are eternal, §. 849, and 3) intensively, since they are most proportional, §. 894. Divine volition or nolition, extensively infinite, because there cannot be in God dead and merely speculative knowledge, §. 873, is borne toward the objects 1) of simple intelligence, universals, which present notions to us, and whatever there is in them of good or evil, toward which God cannot be totally indifferent, §. 891, 2) of middle knowledge, the actual things of another universe, 3) of free knowledge, §. 874-876. The will of God, insofar as it desires the objects of free knowledge, or the actual things of this universe, is called consequent (der nachfolgende), insofar as it is borne toward universals and the actual things of another universe, antecedent (der vorhergehende Wille Gottes), by reduction, §. 826, 695, the former efficient, §. 671, both efficacious, the antecedent too, not only because it is serious, but also because it is among the motives of the decree, §. 675.",["'Simple intelligence', 'middle knowledge', 'free knowledge' render simplicis intelligentiae, scientiae mediae, scientiae liberae.","'Volition' and 'nolition' render volitio and nolitio; 'serious' renders seria."]],"met:900":["A will is inscrutable (ein unausforschlicher Wille) whose impulsive causes are incomprehensible. Now the impulsive causes of the divine will are its own most distinct liking, §. 898, hence an internal perfection in God, §. 37, incomprehensible to us, §. 862. Whoever conceives some motives of God in some degree of distinctness does not scrutinize the divine will, §. 862. Hence neither does one aiming at some understanding of the divine motives imagine the will of God to be scrutable to himself, §. 664. The more the impulsive causes of a certain will, the less fully they can be cognized, the more inscrutable is the will, §. 160. Hence when the will of God is said to be inscrutable to us in the highest degree, §. 812, we think 1) that very many of its motives are not fully cognized, 2) that each of them is very far removed from our comprehension, §. 862, 898.",["ipsius lubitus may be read as God's own liking or the will's own; 'its own' keeps the Latin open.","'Understanding' renders intellectionem."]],"met:901":["The conformity of a free action with its ground, hence with the law, §. 83, the moral one, §. 723, is its rectitude (Richtigkeit einer Handlung, oder Gesetzmäßigkeit); insofar therefore as all the most free actions of God, §. 898, 725, have the highest conformity, §. 176, with the law of the best, §. 822, the moral one, §. 723: Let the best be freely joined to the best, they are most right. The highest rectitude belongs to the divine will, §. 899, holiness, §. 828, moral, §. 723.",["'Ground' renders ratio.","moralis at the end may qualify sanctitas or rectitudo."]],"met:902":["The most free actions of God are not absolutely necessary, since their opposite is possible in itself. §. 102. All the actions of God transeunt into the world, §. 854, 211, have an opposite possible in itself, §. 361. Hence none of them are absolutely necessary. And since the opposite of all the most free actions of God can be actualized through the omnipotence of God, §. 897, none of them, nor therefore any action of God into the world, is physically necessary to him, §. 470, 859, and thus neither is any morally necessary in the sense as if through freedom the opposite of any were rendered physically impossible to God, §. 723. Since nevertheless the highest freedom of God always determines itself most rightly, §. 901, since all his actions are morally most holy, they are morally necessary, §. 723, 724.",["'Since' renders both dum and cum in the last sentence."]],"met:903":["Goodness (Gütigkeit) (*benignitas*) is the determination of the will to doing good to another. A benefit (Wohlthat) is an action more useful to another, proceeding from goodness. The least goodness would be the least propensity or disposition of the will to confer one least good on a single least, i.e. least worthy, one, §. 161. The more therefore, the more numerous, the greater the benefits, on the more numerous, the more worthy, goodness desires to confer, the greater it is, §. 160. A benefit is a greater good. §. 336, 187.",["'Goodness' renders bonitas, here with the German Gütigkeit and the Latin benignitas; elsewhere bonitas is the goodness of a thing."]],"met:904":["God wills benefits to be conferred on others, §. 903, 899. Therefore he is benign, §. 903, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most benign, we venerate him as loving infinitely and eternally, §. 899, and immutably, §. 839, to confer the most, the greatest benefits on the most, the most worthy. §. 903.",[]],"met:905":["Love of human beings is philanthropy (Menschen-Liebe), willing the mutual, proportioned love of the beloved is jealousy (Liebes-Eifer), which, with mercy, favor, benevolence, clemency, are universal in God and the greatest, §. 684, 904. And since constant intense love is fidelity (Treue), God, loving eternally, infinitely, is in the highest degree faithful, §. 904, 812.",["'Jealousy' renders zelotypia; the participle volens has no noun of its own, amor being understood from the first clause."]],"met:906":["Goodness toward persons or spirits that is proportional is justice (Gerechtigkeit), cf. E. §. 317. The least justice would be the least goodness following the least, least clear, least certain, least lively mathematical cognition, §. 903, 161. The more therefore 1) intensely it loves, 2) the more, 3) the greater, 4) on the more, to confer benefits in proportion to the degree of perfection or imperfection cognized in the spirit, 5) the more clearly, 6) the more certainly, 7) the more ardently that degree is cognized, the greater is the justice, §. 903, 160. God is just, §. 904, 894, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most just, we venerate his highest goodness, §. 904, most proportional, §. 894, most prepared to confer the most, greatest benefits on the most in proportion to the most distinct, and infallible, and most lively cognition of the degrees of perfection or imperfection met with in individual spirits.",["The infinitive conferre in 4) depends on amat in 1): 'loves ... to confer'.","cf. E. is a reference to another work of Baumgarten's, carried over as printed."]],"met:907":["A reward (eine Belohnung, Lohn) (*remuneratio*) is a contingent good conferred on a person on account of moral good. Justice in conferring rewards is remunerative (Belohnungs-Gerechtigkeit), which we venerate in God as the highest, §. 906, 812, when we look up to it as most prepared to confer the most proportional rewards for all, even the least, moral goods of all spirits.",["'Reward' renders praemium; 'look up to' renders suspicimus."]],"met:908":["Punishment (Strafe) is a contingent evil inflicted on a person on account of moral evil. Punishments as well as rewards, certain ones, can be sufficiently conceived from the essence of the sin and the nature of the sinner, or from the essence of the moral good and the nature of the one acting well, and they will be natural. Certain rewards cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another who confers them, and they are arbitrary (willkürliche Belohnungen). Certain punishments cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another who punishes, and they are arbitrary (Strafen).",["quaedam may qualify rewards only or punishments and rewards both; the order is kept.","'Choice' renders arbitrium; the German equivalent of the last arbitrariae is printed only as Strafen."]],"met:909":["He in whom there are moral evils is a sinner (ein Sünder). He in whom certain moral evils are not is, with respect to them, innocent in the broader sense (der Unschuldige in weiterer Bedeutung) (*insons*), cf. E. §. 319. A sinner, other things being equal, is not as good as an innocent one, §. 187. Therefore neither is he as much loved by the most proportional goodness, §. 906. Therefore God wills to confer certain benefits on the innocent one which he does not will to confer on the sinner, §. 904. Toward these benefits not to be conferred on the sinner God is not indifferent, §. 891, hence according to his most distinct liking he is averse to conferring them on the sinner, §. 898, 669. Therefore he wills the opposite of benefits of this kind, §. 663, 690. The opposites of benefits are evils, §. 81, 903, and those contingent, §. 146. Therefore God wills certain contingent evils to be inflicted on the sinner on account of moral evils, i.e. punishments, §. 908.",[]],"met:910":["Justice in inflicting punishments is punitive (die Straf-Gerechtigkeit) (vindicative, avenging, vindicating, nemesis), punitive justice belongs to God, §. 909, and that the highest, §. 812, since it is most ready to punish all the sins of all sinners most proportionately, §. 906. Philosophical Dippelianism, the opinion removing the punitive justice of God, is an error.",["'vindicative, avenging, vindicating, nemesis': vindicativa, ultrix, vindicatrix, nemesis.","'of all sinners': peccatorum omnium, read as the genitive plural of peccator."]],"met:911":["The opposite of a natural reward is a natural punishment, the opposite of an arbitrary reward is an arbitrary punishment, §. 908. Therefore, while the remunerative justice of God dispenses arbitrary rewards, vindicative justice imposes arbitrary punishments, §. 907, 909. Natural punishments themselves, in respect of him without whose choice the nature of the one sinning would not have a sufficient ground, are arbitrary, §. 908. The case of natural rewards is the same.",["'arbitrary': arbitrarius; 'choice': arbitrium.","'ground': ratio."]],"met:912":["The complex of causes posited outside a spirit concurring toward its prosperity or misery is good and bad fortune (gutes und böses Glück), things actualized through fortune are fortuitous (Glücks-Fälle), and these contingent, §. 146, 787. Goods of this kind conferred on a spirit on account of moral goods are rewards, §. 907, and, not to be cognized sufficiently except from the choice of God, §. 854, are arbitrary, §. 908. Fortuitous evils inflicted on a spirit are misfortunes (Unglücks-Fälle). Misfortunes inflicted on a spirit on account of moral evils are punishments, and, not to be cognized, except from the choice of God, sufficiently, §. 854, are arbitrary punishments, §. 908.",["'fortuitous': fortuita, a neuter plural, 'fortuitous things'."]],"met:913":["Miraculous and supernatural rewards and punishments are possible in themselves, §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, to divine omnipotence, §. 833, 834, are arbitrary, §. 908, 898, yet not the only ones, §. 911, 912.",["'to divine omnipotence': the dative omnipotentiae divinae may go with 'hypothetically' possible or with 'arbitrary'; left as ambiguous as the Latin."]],"met:914":["An event: whatever in it is real and positive is called the remote material (das bejahende), the thoroughgoing determination the proximate material (die gänzliche Bestimmung), and whatever in finite things is negative to it, the formal (der mangelhafte Schranken einer Begebenheit) of the event. Hence evils, and so also punishments, §. 908, are either regarded materially and formally at once, and are, like all finite things, partly good, partly evils taken precisely or formally, §. 264, are loved by God insofar as they are good, God hates them insofar as they are evil, infinitely, §. 899, or are regarded only materially, as the remote material, and are positive and real, only an object of divine love, §. 899, or only formally, and are nothing but either negations strictly so called, or privations, §. 137, and are only an object of divine hatred, §. 899, or as the proximate material and the formal.",["The sentence opens with a suspended Eventus, picked up by 'of the event' (formale eventus) at its end.","'remote material', 'proximate material', 'formal': materiale remotum, materiale proximum, formale; 'thoroughgoing determination': omnimoda determinatio.","'taken precisely or formally' (praecise s. formaliter sumpta) may qualify 'evils' alone or both 'good' and 'evils'."]],"met:915":["God wills by punitive justice, §. 910, the remote material of punishment, and whatever of reality is in the proximate, but not the formal, §. 914. But now that the same befall this sinner, not another, but him to whom they are most proportionate, is a reality, §. 36, 909. This therefore God wills, §. 899. Hence also the distinction of contingent evils, §. 146, and so both of punishments, §. 908, and of sins, §. 788, into privative and positive is to be judged, §. 525.",["'the same': easdem, feminine plural, agreeing with poenae.","'is to be judged': diiudicanda."]],"met:916":["Long-suffering (Langmuth, und richterliche Geduld) (the patience of a judge) is justice punishing only when the best occasion has been seen. God, knowing infallibly the best opportunities of all punishments, §. 889, 879, will also will this proximate material of any punishment, since it is a reality, §. 36, most proportionately, §. 894, 914. Hence he is supremely long-suffering.",["'Long-suffering': longanimitas; 'will also will': the future volet.","'when the best occasion has been seen': the ablative absolute optima visa occasione."]],"met:917":["Impartiality is aversion from deciding from apparent stimuli. In God not even stimuli, §. 898, much less apparent ones, are possible, §. 889, 12, and since his most holy will is averse both to them and to a decree from them, §. 902, he is most impartial.",[]],"met:918":["Impartial justice is equity (Billigkeit). God, most just, §. 906, and most impartial, §. 917, is most equitable.",[]],"met:919":["Sincerity (Aufrichtigkeit) is goodness in signifying one's mind, and this is in God, §. 904, the highest, §. 812, since he is most ready to signify his mind by the most apt signs to all to whom, and as much as, the highest wisdom counsels him to signify it, §. 884-888.",["'mind': mens."]],"met:920":["Veracity (Wahrhaftigkeit) is sincerity in speech. Therefore, since divine wisdom judges it best to signify the divine mind through speech, God is most veracious. §. 919.",["'speech': oratio."]],"met:921":["The least duration of the least nature would be the least life. The greater the nature whose, the greater the duration is, the greater is the life, until it is the greatest, the highest duration of the most perfect nature, §. 430, 161. The highest life is to be attributed to God, §. 859, 850.",["'The greater the nature whose, the greater the duration is': Quo maioris naturae, quo maior est duratio, two correlative clauses with the first elliptical.","'until it is the greatest': donec sit maxima, whose subject may be the life or the duration."]],"met:922":["Since the greatest life of God is absolutely necessary, §. 921, because it is his essence, §. 816, and existence, §. 823, 780, God is not only immortal, but also alone has absolute immortality, §. 781.",[]],"met:923":["Since with the highest metaphysical and physical perfection in God is joined the highest moral perfection, §. 806, 859, through his highest rectitude, §. 901, of which most distinctly conscious to himself, §. 866, enjoying the highest pleasure from it, §. 892, he is most blessed, §. 787.",["'of which': quarum, feminine plural, referring to the perfections.","'pleasure': voluptas; 'most blessed': beatissimus."]],"met:924":["God is happy, §. 923, 787, supremely, §. 812, since 1) all moral corruption and misery is not only absent from him, §. 813, but cannot even be in him, physically, §. 859, or morally, §. 902, 2) independently and from himself, §. 851, 3) without any change both of the goods themselves and of the intuition of them he is most happy, §. 839.",["'happy': felix; 'intuition': intuitus."]],"met:925":["A being more perfect than another is higher than it (etwas höheres). God is the supreme spirit, §. 889, 796, a supramundane being (etwas über die Welt erhabenes), insofar as he has greater perfection than any whole world, and that even the best, §. 361, 843. And since several most perfect beings posited outside one another are impossible, §. 846, God is the absolutely highest being.",["'higher': superius; 'supreme': supremus; 'highest': summum."]],"met:926":["The efficient cause actualizes the effect, §. 319, 210. God is the efficient cause of this universe, §. 854. Therefore God actualized this universe, either from eternity, i.e. in such a way that this world did not have a beginning, or in time or not from eternity, §. 10, in neither case did a part of the world pre-exist the world, §. 371, 394. Therefore in both cases the world is actualized out of nothing, §. 228, and God is the one who actualized it out of nothing, §. 854. To actualize something out of nothing is to create (erschaffen). Therefore God is the creator of this universe.",["'or not from eternity': s. (sive) non ab aeterno, an equivalent, not an alternative."]],"met:927":["Creation by emanation (die Schöpfung durch einen Ausfluss) would be the actualization of the universe out of God's essence, by a creation of this kind 1) the world would not be actualized out of nothing, §. 926. For the essence of God is a necessary being, §. 109, 816. Which is contrary to §. 926. 2) either the whole essence of God, or a part of it could be changed into the universe, §. 370, which is contrary to §. 839. 3) a part of God would be posited outside God, §. 388, and God composite, §. 225, which is contrary to §. 838. In still more ways it is evident that creation by emanation is impossible, §. 7.",[]],"met:928":["All monads of this universe are finite beings, §. 396, hence all beings from another, §. 308, nor can any be actualized except out of nothing, §. 229, 236. Therefore all monads of this universe are created, §. 926. A being that cannot exist except through creation is a creature (ein Geschöpf). Therefore all monads of this universe are creatures, and indeed of God, §. 381, 854. Whatever in this universe is substantial is a creature of God, §. 396.",["'beings from another': entia ab alio."]],"met:929":["Every spirit of this universe, §. 404, all human souls, §. 744, 741, all elements of this world are creatures of God, §. 928, 423, each actualized by a simultaneous, §. 238, and instantaneous creation, §. 299, 228. Now the bodies of this world are aggregates of elements, §. 155, 420, hence whatever in bodies is substantial, as many as have parts subsisting through themselves, are creatures of God, §. 928.",["The singular 'whatever ... is substantial' passes into the plural 'as many as ... are creatures' (quotquot ... sunt), as in the Latin."]],"met:930":["What cannot exist except as the caused of another can also not endure except as the caused of another, §. 299. Hence the world and all its parts cannot endure except as beings from another, §. 928, 375. Therefore a creation actualizing an independent duration in creatures is impossible, and not an object of divine omnipotence, §. 833. The creation of this universe is possible, §. 926, 57. Therefore through the creation of this universe an independent duration in it was not actualized.",["'the caused': causatum; 'endure': durare."]],"met:931":["Whatever does not pertain to the existence of the world and of its substantial parts is not created, §. 926. But now the essences of creatures, hence also their metaphysical evil, §. 146, do not pertain to their existence, §. 132, 134, hence neither the essences nor the metaphysical evils of creatures are created, unless you wish all the determinations of an actual creature to be called created, contrary to the notion of §. 926.",["'notion': notio."]],"met:932":["That this universe does not exist, that no universe exists, is in itself possible, §. 370, 361. All things possible in themselves are also possible to God, §. 859. Hence God could, as to omnipotence, not create a universe, create another than this, with which existing, this would not have existed, §. 379, 926. Yet he created this one impassibly, §. 851, through the highest spontaneity, §. 895, by a creation free with respect to execution, §. 897. Therefore most freely, §. 898.",["'as to omnipotence': qua omnipotentiam.","'with which existing': the ablative absolute quo exsistente."]],"met:933":["God created this world most freely, §. 932. Therefore he willed to create it, §. 893, willed it efficiently, §. 671, hence also fully, because he is infallible, §. 879, and so consequently from complete motives. Therefore God decreed to create this world, §. 695, and indeed efficaciously in every signification, §. 675.",["'consequently': consequenter, which may also mean 'consistently'; left ambiguous.","'motives': motiva; 'signification': significatus."]],"met:934":["God decreed the creation of this world, §. 933, with a most proportionate will, §. 894. Hence he decreed the existence of this world in proportion to the degree of goodness cognized in it, §. 926. The existence of another world he did not decree with a most proportionate will, §. 379. Therefore God did not cognize so much goodness in the existence of any other world as he cognized in the existence of this world, §. 70. Now the cognition of God is most distinct and maximally infallible, §. 879. Therefore the existence of this world, preferred and chosen before all the rest, §. 697, is the best that a world's can be, §. 187.",["'the best that a world's can be': quae mundi esse potest, optima, the genitive mundi depending on an understood exsistentia."]],"met:935":["With the existence of this world posited, the highest perfection that can be in a world is posited, §. 187, 934. In which world the highest perfection that can be in a world is posited, that one is the most perfect, §. 185. Therefore this world is the most perfect of all possible ones.",[]],"met:936":["In this world there are the most of the greatest, the greatest of the most parts that can be in a world, an agreement as great as can be in a world, §. 436, 935. Hence the world is extensively, protensively and intensively the greatest of worlds, §. 437, really indeed finite, yet in such a way that it can be called infinite in many ways, §. 440, the best, §. 443, in it the greatest universal nexus, §. 441, order, §. 444, and the greatest harmony that can be, §. 462.",["'agreement': consensus.","'the most of the greatest, the greatest of the most parts': maximarum plurimae, plurimarum maximae partes."]],"met:937":["Posit a world totally equal to this world in perfection, it would have to be posited outside it, otherwise it would be a part of this world, §. 354, 155. The perfection of this world cannot be outside it, nor could the perfection of the wholly equally perfect be outside it, §. 194. If therefore such a world were possible, two perfections totally equal outside one another would be possible, which is contrary to §. 272. Therefore the most perfect world is unique, §. 77.",["'of the wholly equally perfect': prorsus aequaliter perfecti, with no noun; 'outside it' (extra ipsum) may refer to either world.","'unique': unicus."]],"met:938":["Of this, that this world is the best, we can be certain, §. 935, 936. Therefore this doctrine is not a philosophical hypothesis. Those who demand a demonstration of it *a posteriori* either understand by a demonstration *a posteriori* one that has in its premises an intuitive judgment, and they have it, §. 933, into whose premises there enters: this world exists, an intuitive judgment, or they demand to experience the discrepancy of this and a worse world, hence to be posited outside this world, §. 548, a wondrous ecstasy, §. 552.",["'a wondrous ecstasy': mirabilem ecstasin, in apposition to 'to be posited outside this world'."]],"met:939":["Whatever God created, he willed to create, §. 932. But now the formal of contingent evils, and of moral ones in particular, he did not at all will, §. 914. Therefore neither did he will to create it, §. 926. God is not the creator of any contingent evil, hence neither of any moral evil formally regarded.",["'the formal': formale; 'formally regarded': formaliter spectati."]],"met:940":["An author (Urheber) is the cause of a free action, and both the action and the things caused by it are effects of the author, or deeds (sittliche Thaten). Now God created the world most freely, §. 932. Therefore God is the author of the creation and of this world. God is not the author except of those things that he wills, §. 719, 891. Now the formal of no contingent evil, of no sin, does God will, §. 914. Therefore of no contingent evil, of no sin formally regarded, is God the author. A moral cause strictly so called (ein sittlicher Urheber) is an author through the free determination of another, such as one enticing, threatening, persuading, dissuading, extorting, §. 728. Now a moral cause strictly so called is an author. Therefore God is not the moral cause strictly so called of any sin and contingent evil formally regarded.",["'author': auctor; 'the things caused by it': eius causata; 'deeds': facta.","Baumgarten's German for causa moralis stricte dicta is 'sittlicher Urheber', the same German noun as for auctor."]],"met:941":["One actualizing impelling causes toward moral evil formally regarded is a tempter to evil (ein Versucher zum Bösen). God, if he actualized impelling causes toward moral evil formally regarded, would become its moral cause strictly so called. Therefore God is never a tempter to evil, §. 940.",["'impelling causes': causae impulsivae."]],"met:942":["The acknowledgment of a greater perfection in something is honor (Ehre). Greater honor is glory (Preis), cf. §. 684. The glory of God therefore is the greater cognition of his highest perfections. The more, the more greatly, therefore the more clearly, the more certainly, the more ardently, the more truly, the more, the greater perfections of someone they cognize, the greater is the glory, §. 160. Hence the highest glory of God is the clearest, truest, most certain, most ardent cognition of his most and greatest perfections in the most, §. 161. The glory of God is a good, §. 866.",["'acknowledgment': agnitio; 'cognition': cognitio.","'The more, the more greatly': quo plures, quo magis; plures is masculine plural, 'the more (persons)'; 'in the most': in plurimis.","'the more, the greater perfections': quo plures, quo maiores ... perfectiones."]],"met:943":["Through this world the highest perfections of God can be cognized more clearly, more truly, more certainly, more ardently, §. 333, 375. Therefore this world is useful for the glory of God to those able to cognize the same from it, §. 336. This usefulness has been actualized by the creation both of the world itself and of all the spirits in it, §. 926. Therefore the creator of the universe uses it for actualizing his glory, which he cognizes most distinctly as good, §. 889, 942. Therefore God had an end in creating, §. 341.",["'the same': eandem, feminine singular, agreeing with gloriam."]],"met:944":["God created the world according to his highest knowledge of the final nexus, §. 943, hence most wisely, §. 882. Hence all the best ends of the best world in creating it, in the most perfect subordination and coordination, §. 883, their best means, §. 884, most certain, §. 885, most fruitful, §. 886, and attaining them in full, §. 887, by the shortest way, §. 888, he knew, and actualized, §. 894. Again it is evident that God created no contingent evil formally regarded, §. 146, 914.",["'knowledge': scientia; 'means': remedia.","'attaining them in full': eosque ex asse consequentia.","'Again': the text prints Denno, evidently for denuo."]],"met:945":["The end of God in creating the world was not some perfection internal to him. For through the world it is impossible for any of them either to be actualized or to be increased, §. 851, but God does not will impossible things, §. 891, 893. But now whatever is, besides God, is the world, or a part of the world, §. 846, 354. Therefore the end of God in creating the world was the perfection of creatures, §. 928.",[]],"met:946":["The end of God in creating the universe was the perfection of creatures as great as is possible in the best world, §. 945, 944. Therefore all utilities of created things, §. 336, all uses of the same, §. 338, are ends of God, §. 944. The science of the divine ends in creatures is teleology, both physical, of bodies, and pneumatic, exhibiting the ends of spirits.",["utilities: Latin utilitates, the usefulness of things; uses: usus.","'ends of God' (fines dei) and 'divine ends' (fines divini) are kept distinct as in the Latin."]],"met:947":["The good determination of a spirit from motives of the divine glory is the glorification of the divine glory (the worship of God) (Verherrlichung Gottes, Gottesdienst). The glory of God and its glorification are religion. Now the glory of God is useful for his worship, §. 336, 712. Glory and worship for religion, §. 336. Therefore the ends of creation were the worship of God and religion, §. 942, 946.",["glorification: Latin illustratio, literally a making illustrious or bright; worship: cultus.","The fourth sentence has no verb in the Latin ('Gloria et cultus ad religionem'); 'are useful' is understood from the preceding sentence and not supplied."]],"met:948":["The prosperity and blessedness or felicity of spirits is useful for the glory of God, §. 787, 942, its glorification and religion, §. 947, 336. Therefore the prosperity and blessedness or felicity of spirits as great as is possible in the best world was an end of creation, §. 946, 942.",["blessedness: beatitudo; felicity: felicitas; joined by seu, 'or' in the sense of an equivalent name."]],"met:949":["All creatures, whether they are regarded as means or as ends, are useful for the glory of God, this for the glorification of the divine glory, §. 943, 947, hence all co-ends of religion in creation are subordinated to it, §. 315, 947. Therefore religion is the ultimate end of creation, §. 343.",["co-ends: Latin confines, ends standing alongside (coordinate with) religion; the word also means 'neighbors'."]],"met:950":["This world endures, §. 299, but at no moment independently, §. 930. Therefore it cannot endure except as something caused by something posited outside itself even for one moment, §. 307. Therefore an extramundane force effects its duration at any moment of duration, §. 210. This is God, §. 855, 839. Therefore God effects at any moment of duration the duration of the universe. The actualization of duration is conservation (die Erhaltung). Therefore God is the conserver of this universe.",["'even for one moment' (vel uno momento) may attach to 'endure' or to 'posited'; left ambiguous as in the Latin.","effects: operatur; actualization: actuatio; conservation: conservatio, also 'preservation'."]],"met:951":["Conservation is a continuous influx of God, §. 950, 895, and that a real one, §. 212, because its own existence can be the effect of nothing finite, §. 308. The same is creation, §. 926. Whence conservation is not wrongly called continued creation.",["'its own existence' (exsistentia sua): the referent of sua is not stated in the Latin."]],"met:952":["What is not created is not conserved, §. 950, 926. Therefore neither the essences of things, nor the metaphysical evil of finite things, §. 931, nor the formal element of contingent evil, nor moral evil formally regarded, is conserved by God, §. 939.",["the formal element: Latin formale, a substantivized adjective, literally 'the formal' (of evil)."]],"met:953":["Whatever cannot exist except through creation cannot endure except through conservation, §. 950, 926. Now all monads of this world, and whatever is substantial in the same, is a creature of God. Therefore all monads of the world, and whatever is substantial in this world, is conserved by God at any moment of its duration, §. 928.",["The singular verbs (est, conservatur) with a compound subject are the Latin's."]],"met:954":["Since efficient causes other than God, all the substances of this world, §. 319, 846, are subordinated to God, §. 928, he is the absolutely first efficient cause, all the others second, §. 315, 28. Now all actions of finite substances are at the same time passions from other finite substances influencing them, § 451. Therefore God concurs, as efficient cause, in all actions of finite substances mediately, §. 314, 320. But since at the same time all of finite substances, the passions from other finite substances, are their own actions, §. 463, not only then where they are chiefly conceived to act, but also then, where they are noted to suffer, God, at that, very moment, at which they are changed, actualizing the sufficient ground of this change of theirs, their force, by conserving it, §. 953, concurs in all actions of finite substances, as efficient cause, immediately, §. 320, for to the present existence of a finite substance belongs its action, §. 210, 55.",["The commas in 'omnes finitarum substantiarum, passiones' and 'deus eo, ipso momento, quo' stand so in the source and are kept; the text may be faulty there.","ground: ratio (ratio sufficiens, sufficient ground); passions: passiones, undergoings; suffer: pati, be acted upon."]],"met:955":["The immediate concurrence of God is his presence, §. 223. Therefore God is proximately present to all the substances of this world, §. 954. What is proximately present to each and every substantial part of something is said to be intimately present (auf das genaueste gegenwärtig) to that very thing. Now God is proximately present to all substantial parts of bodies in this universe, §. 421. Therefore God is intimately present to all bodies in this universe.",["proximately: Latin proxime, literally 'most nearly'; intimately: intime, literally 'most inwardly'."]],"met:956":["God is present to every monad of this world proximately, to every body intimately, §. 955, and that at any moment, and as regards all the actions of creatures, §. 954. Therefore God is most omnipresent.",["most omnipresent: Latin omnipraesentissimus, a superlative of 'omnipresent'."]],"met:957":["Wherever God is, there he is whole, and undivided, §. 842. Therefore also as regards substance and essence, §. 830. 816. Therefore God is present to all monads and bodies of this world, as regards substance and essence, §. 955.",["as regards: qua."]],"met:958":["The conservation of the forces of this universe whatsoever, in their very act, is the physical concurrence of God, and this, because and insofar as it extends to the individual actions of the individual substances, is called general (die allgemeine Mitwirkung Gottes) (universal).",["general: generalis; universal: universalis, given by Baumgarten as an alternative term."]],"met:959":["Every concurrence of God is most free, §. 932. Therefore if he concurred in the formal element of any evil action, he would become its author, §. 940. Therefore he concurs in all physically and morally evil actions as regards the material element, §. 958, but not as regards the formal element, §. 940.",["the formal element / the material element: Latin formale / materiale, substantivized adjectives."]],"met:960":["The concurrence of a moral cause strictly so called is moral (die sittliche). God actualizes the motives of his worship, §. 947. Therefore he concurs in certain actions in this universe morally, §. 940. The moral concurrence of God added to the general is special (die besondre Mitwirkung). Therefore God concurs in certain actions in this universe specially.",["added to: Latin accedens ad, coming in addition to."]],"met:961":["God is not a tempter to evil, §. 941. Therefore he concurs morally in the formal element of no morally evil actions, §. 940. Therefore not specially either, §. 960.",[]],"met:962":["The supernatural concurrence of God added to the special is the most special (die besonderste Mitwirkung Gottes), and possible in itself, §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, and actual in this world, whenever some action in the same cannot be actualized equally well through the special concurrence of God, §. 498, 935.",[]],"met:963":["Governance (die Führung) is the action by which several means are successively actualized toward some further end. The better therefore, the more of the best, hence also of those attaining the end by the shortest way, §. 888, the more means, the better, the further the ends toward which they are actualized, the more perfect is the governance, §. 185. The ends God had in creating, he has and actualizes in conserving, §. 951, 944. When therefore we venerate him as the supreme governor of the universe, we worship him actualizing at any moment the best, and the most numerous means of those proceeding by the shortest ways, leading to the best ends and finally to the ultimate goal of this universe. When God in governing 1) sets a certain limit to the forces (hence also to the actions, §. 331) of a created thing, he is said to determine in the stricter sense (Maass und Schranken setzen), 2) subordinates to his ends an action of a created thing not undertaken by it toward the same, to direct in the stricter sense (lenken in engerer Bedeutung).",["The second sentence is a correlative quo ... hoc construction ('by how much ... by so much'); its compression is kept.","'of the best' (optimorum) is genitive: means belonging to the best things, or to those attaining the end most directly.","governance: gubernatio; goal: scopus."]],"met:964":["The first sin is called the fall (der Fall), and the potency of falling lability (dessen Möglichkeit), this is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. The opposite of hypothetical lability is confirmation in the good (Bestätigung im Guten).",["lability: Latin labilitas, liability to fall; potency: potentia.","The German equivalent 'dessen Möglichkeit' (its possibility) glosses 'potentia labendi' rather than labilitas."]],"met:965":["Absolute lability belongs to the essence of a finite spirit, §. 40, 964, hence is in the same absolutely necessarily, §. 106, a metaphysical evil, §. 146, which is not concreated to any spirit, §. 931, whose opposite could be concreated to no creature, §. 833. A falling spirit acts, §. 964, 788. Therefore God concurs in the material element of the fall, §. 918, but in the formal neither physically nor morally, §. 909, 911. Therefore he neither becomes the author of the fall formally regarded, nor a moral cause strictly so called §. 940.",["'absolutely necessarily' (absolute necessario) could also be read with eodem, 'in the same, as something absolutely necessary'; left ambiguous.","The missing comma before the final §. 940 is the source's."]],"met:966":["The fall is in itself contingent, §. 964, since it is a change of something finite, §. 361. Therefore the opposite of the fall is in itself possible, §. 104. Therefore the hindrance of the fall is in itself possible, §. 221. Therefore God could have hindered every fall, §. 833.",["hindrance / hinder: Latin impedimentum / impedire."]],"met:967":["Moral hindrance (sittliche Verhinderung) is the actualization of motives toward the opposite of some change. God has actualized for spirits very many motives toward their felicity, §. 948. The felicity of a spirit is the opposite of its fall, §. 81, 787. Therefore God hinders every fall morally.",["hindrance here: Latin impeditio."]],"met:968":["Hindrance which is not moral is called physical. The fall is a sin, §. 964, therefore a moral action, §. 788, therefore free with respect to its execution, §. 719, 722, hence physically contingent, §. 709. Therefore besides the freedom of the one who fell and the motives of that one, §. 967, many other things were possible, which being posited the fall which followed would not have followed, §. 378, physical hindrances of the fall, §. 81, 221. Therefore God could also have hindered the fall physically, §. 833.",["'the motives of that one' (motiva illius): illius may refer to the one who fell or to the freedom; left ambiguous.","physical hindrances: impedimenta physica."]],"met:969":["God hinders every sin morally, §. 967, he can hinder physically through omnipotence, §. 968. Yet he does not hinder every one physically. Non-hindrance is permission (Zulassung). Therefore it is either physical. §. 968, or moral, §. 967.",["The period after 'physical' stands so in the source."]],"met:970":["God permits no fall, §. 967, no sin, morally, §. 969. Yet certain sins in this world he permits physically. Suppose these too to be hindered physically, it would not be this world in which they were physically hindered, but another, §. 378, less good, §. 937. Therefore God physically permits certain sins in this world, because the world in which they are physically permitted is the best means of religion, §. 949, 935.",[]],"met:971":["Right (taken for a quality of a person) in the wider signification (Recht in weiter Bedeutung) is a moral faculty; and whoever has the right of deciding concerning some thing whatever he pleases is its full lord (völlig Herr davon). Full dominion over persons is full power (völlige Herrschaft).",["right: ius; lord: dominus, also 'owner'; dominion: dominium; power: potestas."]],"met:972":["God has a right to his most right actions, §. 901, 971. Among them is the decree concerning the world and its single creatures, §. 934. Therefore God is the full lord of the world and of all creatures in the same. Therefore he has full power over the spirits of this world, §. 971.",["most right: Latin rectissimas, most upright or most correct; not etymologically related to ius, right."]],"met:973":["Whoever has full power over a spirit has the right of obligating it in whatever way he pleases. Whoever has the right of obligating some spirit in whatever way he pleases has the right of giving it laws. For the author of the obligation which a law enunciates is said to give the law (Gesetze geben). One having the right of giving laws is a legislator (der Gesetzgeber). Therefore God is the legislator of all spirits of this world, §. 971, 972.",["give laws: Latin leges ferre."]],"met:974":["Whoever alone has the highest potency and power over several spirits is their monarch, whoever full, is their despot. Now God alone has the highest potency, §. 832, and power, and that full, over all created spirits, §. 971. Therefore his is the highest and greatest despotic monarchy, of which all created spirits are subjects, §. 844.",["potency: potentia; power: potestas.","'whoever full' (qui plenam): the noun, power, is understood from the preceding clause and not supplied."]],"met:975":["The providence of God (die Vorsehung Gottes) is the action by which he bestows on any created thing as much of goods as the highest goodness can. Therefore by conserving, §. 950, concurring, §. 954, governing, §. 963, hindering evil morally, §. 967, and not permitting it physically except what to hinder would detract from his ends, §. 970, and giving the best laws to his state, §. 973, 974, God exercises providence, §. 903-920, through the impulsive causes to blessedness, *verticordia* (deren herzlenkende Kraft), through fortuitous things, holy fortune (das heilige, das göttliche in dem, was man Glück nennt), through the goods implanted in the nature of anything, kindly nature. Epicureanism, the opinion abolishing the providence of God, is an error, §. 515.",["verticordia: literally 'heart-turning', an epithet of Venus; kept in Latin. kindly nature: alma natura, nourishing nature.","blessedness here: Latin beatitas, not beatitudo as in §. 948; state: civitas."]],"met:976":["The decree concerning the existence of the world is in God most free, §. 933, hence following the most distinct and most accurate cognition of all parts of this world and of every good and evil in the same, §. 889, immutable, §. 839, eternal, §. 849, and irresistible, §. 844, 222. Which can be conceived in a human manner thus: Purpose, προθεσισ: Let the complex of the best compossibles outside God exist, §. 822, 901. Foresight, προγνωσισ: This world is the best complex of the compossible best things outside God, §. 935. Decree, προορισμοσ: Let this best world exist.",["purpose: propositum; foresight: praevisio, prevision; complex: complexus.","The Greek is given as printed, with σ for final ς."]],"met:977":["More worlds than this one, more finite things than those which are parts of this world, do not exist, §. 379, nor did God decree more concerning contingent things than is comprehended in the decree concerning the existence of this world, §. 976. That one therefore, insofar as it extends to all realities of this universe, is called universal, insofar as it is such that there are not more, unique, §. 77.",["'insofar as it is such that there are not more' renders 'qua est, quod plura non sunt', whose construction is compressed in the Latin."]],"met:978":["Particular decrees of God (besondre Rathschlüsse Gottes) either are parts of the universal and unique decree, §. 977, or would be primitive (ursprüngliche), either opposed to it or at least not comprehended under the universal. Such are not in God, §. 976.",[]],"met:979":["All decrees of God are acts of a will most proportional to its objects, §. 894, following most perfectly the knowledge of the degree of perfection or imperfection in the object either to be desired or to be averted, §. 976. The same is evident also thus: Suppose that in some decree of God the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object had not been a motive at all: either God was ignorant of it, which is contrary to §. 875, or abstracted from it, which is contrary to §. 870, or such cognition was most distinct, but dead, which is contrary to §. 890.",["knowledge: scientia; cognition: cognitio; averted: aversando, turned away from in aversion."]],"met:980":["If that decree of God is called absolute (der unbedingte) whose motive was neither the foreseen perfection nor the imperfection of the object, hypothetical (der bedingte Rathschluss Gottes), on the contrary, that which follows the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object; no decrees of God concerning contingent things are absolute, all hypothetical, §. 979. Theological absolutism is the opinion positing absolute decrees of God concerning contingent things, and is an error, §. 515.",["opinion: sententia."]],"met:981":["The decree concerning the felicity of some spirit eternal is predestination more strictly so called (die Gnadenwahl), since in the wider sense (die Vorherbestimmung) it is a decree concerning the future. The decree concerning the infelicity of some spirit eternal is reprobation (die Verwerfung), each hypothetical, §. 980. predestinarianism, or absolutism in conceiving predestination more strictly so called, is an error, §. 515.",["aeterna may agree with felicitate / infelicitate ('eternal felicity') or, in the first sentence, with praedestinatio ('eternal predestination'); its place is kept.","The lower-case 'predestinarianism' after a period follows the source; 'or' renders s. (sive)."]],"met:982":["Revelation more widely so called (die Offenbarung in weiterer Bedeutung), cf. §. 986, 989, is a signification of the divine mind made by God to creatures. Therefore by divine revelation creatures are taught the divine cognition and volition, never all of it, §. 862, but always as much as has pleased providence, §. 975.",["mind: Latin mens."]],"met:983":["God is the author of universal nature, §. 940, 466. Whatever therefore a creature cognizes through its nature from universal nature concerning the mind of God, it cognizes through divine revelation, §. 982, which is natural, §. 469. Such is natural theology, §. 800.",["'Talis theologia naturalis est' can also be read 'Such a theology is natural'; left ambiguous."]],"met:984":["All finite things are means of cognizing the divine mind, §. 858, 880, the representative forces of this universe all signify their creator, §. 929, the best, the wisest, §. 933. The soul itself is a force representative of its God, §. 741, 744.",["force: vis; signify: significant."]],"met:985":["Through the soul itself, through the single monads to which it is present, hence also through single bodies, and the senses, to the soul the divine mind is revealed naturally, §. 984, through the uses of single things, the ends of God, §. 946, through the cognition of the best, his will, §. 894.",["'to which it is present' (quibus praesens est): the subject is not stated and may be the soul or the divine mind.","animae after the comma may be dative ('to the soul') or genitive with sensus ('the senses of the soul')."]],"met:986":["Revelation more strictly so called (die Offenbarung in enger Bedeutung), cf. §. 982, 989, is supernatural divine revelation made to human beings through speech, a most special concurrence toward religion, and possible in itself, and hypothetically, and actual in this world, as often as religion cannot be equally well actualized through natural revelation alone, §. 962, 949.",["\"speech\": *oratio*.","\"concurrence\": *concursus*."]],"met:987":["Posit that in this world a natural revelation is to be made only to certain individuals, e.g. to human beings; posit that under the same circumstances a revelation to be made supernaturally to the same individuals is richer, nobler, clearer, truer, more certain, more ardent than a merely natural one, §. 669, revelation strictly so called can occur most conformably to the order of this world, §. 484, and exists in it, §. 986.",["\"Posit\": *pone*, imperative, \"suppose\"."]],"met:988":["Posit some signification of the divine mind in this world very good for religion, §. 947, but not to be actualized naturally, while it nevertheless could be actualized supernaturally; there will occur in this world revelation strictly so called, §. 496, 987.",[]],"met:989":["Revelation strictly so called of things naturally uncognizable by creatures is revelation most strictly so called (die Offenbarung in engerer Bedeutung), cf. §. 982, 986. Whatever cognition of the divine mind in this world, very good for religion, is therefore naturally uncognizable by certain creatures, is actualized in it through revelation most strictly so called, §. 988.",["\"uncognizable\": *incognoscibilis*; the first instance is a genitive plural, \"of things uncognizable\"."]],"met:990":["If something is taken for a revelation of God strictly so called, and yet neither promotes the glory of God and religion more, nor furnishes a richer, nor nobler, nor clearer, nor truer, nor more certain, nor more ardent cognition of God than the natural, it is not a revelation of God strictly so called, §. 986, 495.",["\"than the natural\": *quam naturalis*, which may refer to natural revelation or to natural cognition; left as ambiguous as the Latin."]],"met:991":["In the mind of God there is no contradiction, §. 822. Therefore whatever truly contradicts natural revelation is not a revelation of God strictly so called, §. 983, 986, nor most strictly, §. 989.",[]],"met:992":["Revelation of God strictly and most strictly so called does not contradict the natural, §. 991, hence neither reason taken objectively, insofar as it is cognized by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 646, 984.",["\"taken objectively\", \"taken subjectively\": *obiective sumpta*, *subiective sumpta*."]],"met:993":["Sacred faith taken objectively (der Inhalt des heiligen Glaubens) is the complex of things to be believed to revelation strictly so called, as faith had in revelation strictly so called is sacred faith taken subjectively (der heilige Glaube). Therefore not only the things wholly uncognizable naturally by creatures pertain to sacred faith, §. 989, 986.",["\"faith\": *fides*, which also covers \"belief\".","\"to be believed to revelation\": *revelationi ... credendorum*, a dative, as in believing someone."]],"met:994":["Sacred faith, taken objectively, insofar as it is believed by a human being through faith taken subjectively, does not contradict reason taken objectively, insofar as it is cognized by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 992, 993.",[]],"met:995":["Revelation strictly so called signifies things possible in the highest degree, §. 986, 822. Now the things that are contrary to reason are impossible, §. 643. Therefore neither revelation of God strictly so called, nor the most strict, §. 989, nor sacred faith taken objectively are contrary to reason. But the things that are contrary to reason are neither revelation of God strictly, nor most strictly so called, nor sacred faith taken objectively.",["\"things possible in the highest degree\": *summe possibilia*.","\"contrary to reason\": *contra rationem*."]],"met:996":["Sacred mysteries (heilige Geheimnisse) are things placed above the reason of creatures in sacred faith taken objectively. In every revelation most strictly so called there are mysteries, §. 989, 644. Therefore they are not repugnant to revelation strictly so called, §. 989, nor are they contrary to reason, §. 643.",["\"placed above the reason\": *supra ... rationem posita*."]],"met:997":["In all his revelation God is maximally sincere, §. 919, hence, his wisdom judging speech to be the most apt sign for signifying the divine mind to human beings, and that supernaturally actualized, he is most veracious in revelation strictly so called, §. 920.",["\"his wisdom judging ...\": an ablative absolute, *sapientia ipsius ... iudicante*.","\"that supernaturally actualized\": *eamque ... actuatam*, referring to the speech (*orationem*)."]],"met:998":["In revelation strictly and most strictly taken God is a witness, §. 986, 946, maximally sincere and most dexterous, §. 997, 889, hence most worthy of faith, whose revelation strictly so called gives the highest certainty of faith.",["\"faith\": *fides*, in both places; also \"belief\", \"credence\"."]],"met:999":["The naturalist in the stricter signification (cf. §. 493) is one denying revelation of God strictly so called in this world. The atheist (theoretical), denying the existence of God, errs, §. 811, 854. Naturalism in neither signification posits atheism necessarily, §. 493.",["\"in neither signification\": *significatu neutro*; *neuter* read as \"neither (of the two)\"; the sentence is translated as it stands."]],"met:1000":["The naturalist in the stricter signification, denying that revelation strictly so called exists in this world, because 1) it is a supernatural event, §. 986, hence seems to him impossible, errs, §. 860, 2) thinks sacred faith to be contrary to reason, hence impossible, errs, §. 995, 3) denies providence toward the human race, and errs, §. 975, by his own fault, §. 982, 986, ignorant of the most benign God, §. 903, Whose honor, name and praises shall always remain.",["The closing words are a line of verse, after Vergil, *Aeneid* I.609."]]};
